window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("infra-capacity-economics", {"topics":[{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-infrastructure","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA GPU Operator","Official docs: NVIDIA Container Toolkit","Official docs: Kubernetes (device plugins and extended resources)"],"why":"CPU servers are interchangeable enough that most engineers never think about the box: any node runs\nany container, the OS hides the hardware, and a scheduler can place work anywhere. GPUs break every one\nof those assumptions. A GPU is a separate computer attached over PCIe with its own memory, its own\ninstruction set and its own driver, and the host kernel does not virtualise it the way it virtualises\nCPU time and RAM. An inference container therefore only works if the driver on the node, the CUDA\ntoolkit inside the image, the device plugin that advertised the card, and the scheduler's placement\nall line up.\n\nThe naive approach, \"install the driver, `pip install torch`, run it\", works on one developer machine\nand collapses at the second node: driver versions drift, images pull in CUDA builds the driver cannot\nrun, pods land on nodes without a card, two pods silently share one card and OOM each other, and nobody\ncan say whether the fleet is 30% or 90% busy because nothing exports GPU metrics. Add the price — one\nhigh-end accelerator costs more per hour than a rack of web servers — and the stack has to be\ndeliberate: every idle hour is wasted money and every misplaced pod is a capacity bug. GPU\ninfrastructure is the set of layers that turn a card into a reliable, schedulable, observable, billable\nresource.","how":"Walk the stack bottom-up. Each layer answers one question, and each has a lesson that goes deeper.\n\n### 1. The accelerator: what does the compute look like?\nA data-centre GPU is thousands of simple cores grouped into streaming multiprocessors (SMs), each with\ntensor cores for matrix maths, fed by on-package memory (HBM on A100/H100, GDDR6 on L4). Two numbers\ndominate everything downstream: memory capacity, which decides whether the model fits (see *GPU\nsizing*), and memory bandwidth, which bounds how fast decode can run (see *GPU utilization & memory\nbandwidth*). Form factor matters too: SXM modules sit on a baseboard with NVLink/NVSwitch between GPUs\nand run at higher power; PCIe cards slot into ordinary servers, draw less power, and talk to each other\nonly over PCIe unless bridged. Which card to buy is the subject of *A100 / H100 / L4 selection*.\n\n### 2. The node: what feeds the card?\nA GPU node is a host with one to eight accelerators plus the CPU, RAM, storage and network that keep\nthem busy. Each can be the bottleneck:\n- **CPU and host RAM.** Tokenisation, request handling, data loading and the serving engine's scheduler\n  run on the CPU. Host RAM must stage weights on the way to the device, so a 64 GB host struggles to\n  load a 140 GB checkpoint.\n- **Storage.** Weights are read from disk at start-up. Pulling 140 GB over a 100 MB/s network volume\n  takes about 23 minutes; from local NVMe at a few GB/s it takes under a minute. Cold-start time is\n  mostly a storage decision.\n- **Intra-node interconnect.** Tensor parallelism exchanges activations between GPUs every layer.\n  NVLink gives hundreds of GB/s per GPU (NVIDIA quotes 900 GB/s for H100 SXM); a PCIe Gen4 x16 link\n  gives roughly 32 GB/s per direction. Sharding across PCIe-only cards works but leaves bandwidth on the\n  table.\n- **Network.** Multi-node training needs RDMA (InfiniBand or RoCE) so gradient all-reduce does not\n  stall in the host networking stack. Serving needs it only when one model replica spans machines (a\n  model too large for one eight-GPU node, or disaggregated prefill and decode that ship KV caches\n  between nodes); otherwise each request stays inside one node and ordinary data-centre Ethernet is\n  enough, *because* only prompts and tokens cross the network.\n\n### 3. Driver and CUDA: will the code run at all?\nThe kernel-mode NVIDIA driver is installed once per node and is the only piece that touches hardware.\nEverything else — CUDA runtime, cuBLAS, cuDNN, NCCL, the PyTorch build — ships inside the container\nand must be a version the node's driver supports. Drivers are backward compatible with older CUDA\nruntimes, not forward compatible, so \"CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version\"\nmeans the image is newer than the node. Two escape hatches exist but neither is a plan: CUDA minor-version\ncompatibility lets, say, a 12.x runtime run on an older 12.x driver with some features (PTX JIT) missing,\nand the `cuda-compat` forward-compatibility package works only on data-centre GPUs. Pin both, and upgrade\ndrivers fleet-wide *before* bumping base images.\n\n### 4. Container runtime: how does a container see the device?\nContainers do not include the driver. The NVIDIA Container Toolkit hooks the container runtime so\nthat, when a container requests GPUs, the device files (`/dev/nvidia*`) and the driver's user-space\nlibraries are mounted in at start. This is why a GPU image is portable across driver versions and why\n`nvidia-smi` works inside a container that never installed it.\n\n### 5. Scheduling: which workload gets which card?\nOn Kubernetes a device plugin advertises GPUs as an extended resource, `nvidia.com/gpu`, in integer\nunits. Extended resources cannot be overcommitted and requests must equal limits. The reason is that\nthe kubelet treats an extended resource as an opaque count it cannot enforce: there is no cgroup-style\ncontroller that gives a container a guaranteed fraction of a GPU or caps its device memory, the way CFS\nquotas and memory cgroups do for CPU and RAM. (The GPU itself does time-slice between processes by\ndefault; what is missing is enforced shares and isolation.) The NVIDIA GPU Operator\npackages driver, toolkit, device plugin, feature discovery (node labels such as the card model) and the\nDCGM metrics exporter as one install. Taints keep ordinary pods off expensive nodes; labels let a pod\ntarget the right tier. Sharing one card between small workloads has three mechanisms with different\nisolation: MIG hardware-partitions an A100 or H100 into up to seven instances with separate memory;\ntime-slicing lets the plugin advertise N virtual GPUs that share memory with no isolation; MPS lets\nprocesses run kernels concurrently on one card and, on Volta and later, can set an opt-in per-client\nmemory cap, but clients still share one fault domain. Only MIG gives hardware-enforced memory and fault\nisolation, so only MIG reliably protects one tenant from another's OOM.\n\n### 6. Procurement: who pays, and how?\nThe same card arrives as on-demand instances, reserved or committed-use discounts, spot/preemptible\ncapacity, bare-metal leases, GPU-specialist clouds, or serverless per-second billing. Quotas gate all\nof them; a fresh cloud account often cannot launch a single H100 without a request. The economics live\nin *cost modeling*, *TCO* and *serverless GPUs*; the operational rule is that procurement latency\n(days for quota, weeks for reservations) is part of capacity planning.\n\n### 7. Observability: is it actually busy?\nThe DCGM exporter publishes per-GPU utilisation, memory use, temperature, power, ECC and XID error\ncounts to Prometheus. Without it the fleet is a black box; with it you discover that \"100% utilised\"\noften means one kernel at a time on an otherwise idle chip, which the utilization lesson unpacks.\n\nGPUs also fail in ways the host OS does not hide or recover from: uncorrectable ECC errors, retired\nmemory rows, a card that drops off the PCIe bus. The driver records these as XID events in the kernel\nlog and DCGM exports them. They matter for scheduling *because* Kubernetes still sees the device\nplugin's advertised count: unless a health check cordons the node, the scheduler keeps placing pods on\na card that will crash them. Automated cordon-on-XID (or the GPU Operator's health checks) is part of\nthe stack, not an optional extra.","example":"A minimal Kubernetes Deployment that asks for one GPU on a node labelled with a card model, tolerates\nthe GPU taint, and fails fast on a mis-provisioned node. The init container runs `nvidia-smi`, which\nonly succeeds if the toolkit mounted the driver libraries; because official `nvidia/cuda` images also set\n`NVIDIA_REQUIRE_CUDA`, the toolkit refuses to start the container on a driver too old for that CUDA\nversion. Use the same CUDA version as the server image, and a bad node fails the pod before the server\ntries to load 16 GB of weights.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: embedder\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: embedder } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: embedder } }\n    spec:\n      nodeSelector:\n        nvidia.com/gpu.product: NVIDIA-L4        # label written by GPU feature discovery\n      tolerations:\n      - key: nvidia.com/gpu\n        operator: Exists\n        effect: NoSchedule\n      initContainers:\n      - name: gpu-check\n        image: nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-base-ubuntu22.04   # same CUDA version as the server image\n        command: [\"nvidia-smi\"]                   # fails the pod if the device is not visible\n        resources: { limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 } }\n      containers:\n      - name: server\n        image: ghcr.io/example/embedder:1.4       # ships its own CUDA runtime, never the driver\n        resources:\n          limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 }           # integer; requests are forced equal to limits\n          requests: { cpu: \"4\", memory: 16Gi }    # CPU for tokenisation, RAM to stage weights\n        volumeMounts:\n        - { name: weights, mountPath: /models, readOnly: true }\n      volumes:\n      - name: weights\n        persistentVolumeClaim: { claimName: model-weights }   # NVMe-backed, not object storage\n```","tradeoffs":["SXM/NVLink nodes make multi-GPU sharding fast but come in fixed eight-card blocks; PCIe cards are cheaper and available singly, which wins until a model must be split across cards.","A GPU Operator or managed node pool makes the driver stack someone else's problem but ties you to their driver cadence; bare metal gives control and costs an ops team.","Sharing a card (MIG, time-slicing, MPS) raises utilisation for small models but caps per-tenant performance and complicates scheduling; it stops paying off once one model saturates the card.","Spot and preemptible capacity is far cheaper but can vanish mid-job; fine for checkpointed training and batch inference, wrong for latency-SLO serving unless on-demand fallback exists."],"pitfalls":["Image CUDA runtime newer than the node driver; symptom is CrashLoopBackOff on exactly the nodes that have not had the driver upgrade, with \"driver version is insufficient\" in the logs.","Loading weights from object storage or a slow network disk on every start; symptom is pods stuck in Running-but-not-Ready for tens of minutes while the readiness probe fails.","Time-slicing two models onto one card without checking combined memory; symptom is CUDA OOM errors in one deployment that correlate with traffic to another.","Judging fleet health by `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util; symptom is \"95% busy\" dashboards while tokens per second sit at a fraction of what the card can deliver.","Leaving a node with a faulty GPU in the pool; symptom is pods failing with CUDA \"unknown error\" or illegal-memory-access on one node while identical pods elsewhere run, with XID entries in that node's kernel log.","Treating quota and node lead time as instant; symptom is the autoscaler requesting a node, the cloud answering \"insufficient capacity\", and the request queue growing for twenty minutes."],"when":"You do not choose whether to have GPU infrastructure; you choose how many layers to own. Managed node\npools with the GPU Operator, or a serverless GPU platform, are right when the team is small and the\nworkload is standard inference: the driver stack, device plugin and metrics come pre-wired. Own more\nlayers when you need a specific NVLink topology, custom kernels or drivers, bare-metal pricing, or\non-prem placement. Whatever the split, measure three things: pod ready-time (storage and image size),\nGPU hours used versus purchased (procurement), and DCGM SM activity (whether \"busy\" is real). In\nday-to-day work this shows up as a CrashLoopBackOff you must attribute to the right layer; in\ninterviews, as \"walk me through what happens between `kubectl apply` and the first token\", where\nnaming each layer and its failure mode is the expected answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the pod spec, pins CUDA and driver versions, mounts weights from fast storage, and reads DCGM metrics to tell a real bottleneck from an idle card.","architect":"Decides which layers to own versus buy, the node shapes (SXM vs PCIe, sharing policy), and the procurement mix of reserved, on-demand and spot capacity.","fde":"Audits a customer's cluster layer by layer — driver, toolkit, device plugin, taints, metrics — before promising that a model will run there on day one."},"check":{"q":"After a base-image bump, new inference pods crash on half the cluster with \"CUDA driver version\nis insufficient\" and run fine on the other half. What happened, and how would you structure the fleet\nso it cannot recur?","a":"The image now carries a CUDA runtime newer than the kernel driver on the older half of the\nnodes; drivers support older runtimes, not newer ones, so only un-upgraded nodes fail. Immediate fix:\nroll the image back or pin the pods to upgraded nodes with a label. Structural fix: manage drivers with\nthe GPU Operator or node-pool images so every node runs one known version, label nodes with the driver\nversion, roll driver upgrades fleet-wide before base-image upgrades, and add an init container that\nruns `nvidia-smi` so a mismatch fails fast and visibly instead of after a long weight load. Capacity\nalso matters: if half the fleet is unusable, the autoscaler should be told, not left to discover it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can `nvidia.com/gpu` not be overcommitted on Kubernetes the way CPU can?","options":["GPUs are too expensive for the scheduler to risk contention","There is no cgroup-style mechanism to enforce a fractional share or memory cap on a GPU, so whole devices are handed out","The NVIDIA driver forbids more than one process per card","Extended resources are only counted at node level, not pod level"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CPU is overcommittable because CFS quotas and cgroups can enforce each container's share. A GPU has no such host-side enforcement, so Kubernetes exposes it as an opaque integer extended resource with requests equal to limits. Multiple processes per card are possible (MPS, time-slicing) but without enforced shares or hard memory isolation; the driver does not forbid them."},{"q":"Which sharing mechanism protects one tenant from another tenant's out-of-memory error on the same card?","options":["Time-slicing configured in the device plugin","CUDA MPS","MIG hardware partitions","Setting memory requests equal to limits on both pods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"MIG partitions an A100 or H100 into instances with their own memory and compute slices. Time-slicing shares one memory pool with no limits; MPS shares the pool and the fault domain (its per-client memory cap is opt-in), so one process can starve or crash another. Pod memory limits govern host RAM only."},{"q":"A GPU container image contains the CUDA runtime but no driver. Where does the driver come from at run time?","options":["It is downloaded from the registry as a hidden image layer","PyTorch bundles a user-space driver in its wheel","The NVIDIA Container Toolkit mounts the host node's driver libraries and device files into the container","Kubernetes injects it through the device plugin's gRPC socket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kernel driver lives on the node and must match the kernel; the toolkit's runtime hook bind-mounts its user-space libraries and `/dev/nvidia*` into any container that requests GPUs. The device plugin only tells the scheduler which devices exist and which ones a container was allocated."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-sizing","sources":["Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM (engine arguments, gpu_memory_utilization)"],"why":"A GPU cannot page to disk the way a CPU process can. When the device runs out of memory, the engine\ndoes not slow down gracefully; it throws a CUDA out-of-memory error and the process dies, often\nminutes into start-up after loading most of a checkpoint. The opposite failure is quieter and more\nexpensive: a team provisions eight 80 GB cards for a model whose weights occupy 16 GB, then wonders\nwhy the bill is high and throughput is low. Both come from treating VRAM as a vibe instead of a sum.\n\nThe naive rule, \"the model needs about as much memory as its parameter count in GB\", is wrong in\nboth directions. In fp16 every parameter is two bytes, so a 70-billion-parameter model needs 140 GB\nfor weights alone, nearly twice the naive estimate. And weights are only the fixed part: every\nin-flight request grows a KV cache proportional to its context length, prefill needs transient\nactivation memory, and the CUDA context and engine allocations take space before the first request\narrives. For fine-tuning the picture flips again: gradients and optimizer state make a full fine-tune\nof that 70B model need hundreds of gigabytes more than serving it. VRAM math is the discipline of\nwriting each term down, with the model's actual shapes, before buying or promising anything.","how":"### The inference budget\n```\nVRAM        ≈ weights + KV cache + activations/workspace + runtime overhead\nweights      = n_params × bytes_per_param\nKV per token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\nKV cache     = KV per token × tokens in flight (summed over all concurrent sequences)\n```\nBytes per parameter: fp32 4, fp16/bf16 2, int8/fp8 1, int4 roughly 0.5 plus a few percent for\nscales and zero-points (call it 0.55). The factor 2 in the KV term is one key and one value vector;\n`n_kv_heads` rather than `n_heads` is why grouped-query attention matters: Llama-3 models use 8 KV\nheads against 32 or 64 query heads, which cuts the cache 4–8× relative to full multi-head attention.\n\nRuntime overhead is everything that is neither weights nor cache: the CUDA context, cuBLAS/cuDNN\nworkspaces, captured CUDA graphs and the engine's own buffers. Order of magnitude, illustrative, it\nis around a gigabyte per process, and it exists before any request. Activations during prefill scale\nwith (tokens in the batch × hidden size) per layer and are transient; engines bound them by capping\nhow many prompt tokens one forward pass may contain (chunked prefill), so the practical rule is to\nleave headroom rather than compute them exactly.\n\nEngines expose the budget directly. vLLM's `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9) is the fraction of\nthe card the engine may use *because* other processes and driver allocations need the rest; it\nprofiles weights plus peak activations once at start-up and gives everything remaining to KV-cache\nblocks. That remaining space, divided by KV-per-token, is the total number of tokens the server can\nhold across all concurrent requests, which is the real capacity number.\n\n### Two models, worked\nLlama-3-8B: about 8.0B parameters, 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128. Weights in bf16: 16 GB.\nKV per token = 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB.\n\nLlama-3-70B: about 70.6B parameters, 80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128. Weights in bf16: 141 GB.\nKV per token = 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes = 320 KB.\n\nNow the fits, taking a 0.9 utilisation budget and ~1 GB overhead per card:\n- 8B bf16 on a 24 GB L4: 21.6 − 16 − 1 ≈ 4.6 GB for KV ≈ 35k tokens ≈ eight or nine 4k-token\n  conversations.\n- 8B bf16 on an 80 GB H100: 72 − 16 − 1 = 55 GB ≈ 420k tokens ≈ a hundred 4k conversations, at which\n  point memory bandwidth rather than memory capacity becomes the limit.\n- 70B bf16 on two 80 GB H100s: 144 − 141.2 − 2 ≈ 0.8 GB for KV ≈ 2.4k tokens *total*. It \"fits\" but can\n  barely serve one request; this is the case the formula catches and intuition misses.\n- 70B in fp8 on the same two cards: 144 − 71 − 2 = 71 GB ≈ 220k tokens, a comfortable server.\n- 70B in int4 (~39 GB) on one 80 GB card: 72 − 39 − 1 = 32 GB ≈ 100k tokens, with a quality cost that\n  must be evaluated, not assumed.\n\nA long-context request is the other surprise: one 128k-token prompt on the 8B model is 128k × 128 KB\n= 16 GiB (about 17 GB) of cache, as much as the weights, and a single such request can evict everything else.\n\n### The training budget\nFull fine-tuning with mixed precision and Adam keeps, per parameter: 2 bytes of bf16 weights, 2 bytes\nof bf16 gradient, 4 bytes of fp32 master weight, and 4 + 4 bytes of Adam first and second moments, for\n16 bytes per parameter, the accounting the ZeRO paper uses. The 8B model therefore needs ~128 GB\nbefore activations, so it does not fit one 80 GB card and must be sharded (ZeRO/FSDP) across several.\nActivation memory adds batch × sequence × hidden per layer, and attention without a fused kernel adds\na sequence-squared term; gradient checkpointing trades roughly a third more compute for storing only\nlayer boundaries. LoRA changes the equation *because* the base weights are frozen: they cost 2 bytes\nper parameter with no gradient or optimizer state, and only the adapter (typically well under 1% of\nparameters) carries the 16-byte burden, so the 8B model fine-tunes in roughly 16 GB plus activations.\nQLoRA stores the frozen base in 4-bit, about 4.5 GB for 8B, which is what makes 24 GB cards viable.\nEight-bit optimizers (for example bitsandbytes' 8-bit Adam) store each Adam moment in one byte with\nblock-wise scales, cutting the optimizer term from 8 bytes per parameter to about 2.\n\n### Multi-GPU arithmetic\nTensor parallelism divides both weights and KV cache by the TP degree, while each card keeps its own\noverhead; the head count must divide evenly (8 KV heads means TP up to 8 without replication).\nPipeline parallelism divides layers instead and is used when cards lack NVLink or the model spans\nnodes; each stage holds the KV cache only for its own layers. Either way, \"fits on N cards\" means the\nper-card sum, not the total, is under budget.\n\n### Mixture-of-experts: size by total, not active, parameters\nAn MoE model routes each token through a few experts, so its *compute* per token follows the active\nparameter count while its *memory* follows the total, *because* every expert must be resident for\nwhichever token picks it. Mixtral-8x7B has about 46.7B total and 12.9B active parameters: in bf16 it\nneeds ~93 GB for weights, like a dense 47B model, even though each token does the arithmetic of a 13B\none. Its KV cache follows the attention shape (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128), so 128 KB per token,\nthe same as Llama-3-8B. Bandwidth sits between the two: at batch 1 a decode step reads only the active\nexperts, but at serving batch sizes different tokens hit different experts and the step reads close to\nall of them. Size the card by total parameters; estimate latency with the batch you will run.\n\n### Order of decisions\nPrecision first (it halves or quarters the dominant term), then card and count (capacity), then\n`max_model_len` (the worst-case single request), then concurrency (what remains ÷ KV per token ÷\ntypical context). Reverse the order and you will size the card to a concurrency figure that the\nprecision choice later invalidates.","example":"A twenty-line planner that reproduces the worked numbers above. It uses decimal gigabytes (1e9) to\nmatch how vendors quote card memory; engines report GiB, so expect a 7% difference in their logs.\n\n```python\nGB = 1e9\n\ndef kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, bytes_el=2):\n    return 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_el        # one K and one V per layer\n\ndef plan(name, params_b, n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim, vram_gb, n_gpus=1,\n         bytes_w=2, util=0.9, overhead_gb=1.0, ctx=4096):\n    weights = params_b * GB * bytes_w\n    budget = vram_gb * GB * util * n_gpus                       # engine's usable share\n    kv_room = budget - weights - overhead_gb * GB * n_gpus\n    tok = kv_per_token(n_layers, n_kv_heads, head_dim)\n    tokens = max(kv_room, 0) / tok\n    print(f\"{name:26s} weights={weights/GB:6.1f}GB KV/tok={tok/1024:.0f}KB \"\n          f\"KV room={kv_room/GB:5.1f}GB ~{tokens:,.0f} tok ≈ {tokens/ctx:.0f} seqs@{ctx}\")\n\nplan(\"8B bf16 / L4 24GB\",       8.0, 32, 8, 128, 24)             # ~35k tok, ~9 seqs\nplan(\"8B bf16 / H100 80GB\",     8.0, 32, 8, 128, 80)             # ~420k tok, ~100 seqs\nplan(\"70B bf16 / 2xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2)   # ~2.4k tok: unusable\nplan(\"70B fp8 / 2xH100\",       70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, n_gpus=2, bytes_w=1)     # ~220k tok\nplan(\"70B int4 / 1xH100\",      70.6, 80, 8, 128, 80, bytes_w=0.55)            # ~100k tok\n```","tradeoffs":["Lower weight precision frees memory for cache and raises concurrency, but int4 costs measurable quality on some tasks and dequantisation kernels can slow decode; it pays when memory, not quality, is the binding constraint.","A higher `gpu_memory_utilization` gives more KV blocks, but leaves less slack for activation spikes and co-located processes; push it only on a card nothing else touches.","Tensor parallelism across more cards multiplies KV capacity and bandwidth, yet adds per-layer communication and per-card overhead; it stops helping once the model already fits with KV headroom.","LoRA and QLoRA make fine-tuning fit small cards at the cost of a smaller parameter update and, for QLoRA, slower steps from dequantisation; full fine-tuning is only worth its multi-card cost when the adapter measurably underperforms."],"pitfalls":["Counting weights and forgetting overhead and activations; symptom is an OOM at start-up or on the first long prompt even though \"the model fits\".","Sizing concurrency on the average context instead of `max_model_len`; symptom is sudden request preemption or queueing whenever a few long documents arrive together.","Assuming a fine-tune needs the same memory as serving; symptom is an OOM in the first optimizer step after the forward pass succeeded, because Adam state is allocated lazily.","Dividing total memory across cards instead of checking per-card budgets with TP; symptom is one rank OOMing while the others wait on NCCL.","Sizing an MoE model by its active parameter count; symptom is an OOM at weight load on a card the \"13B-class\" label said was ample.","Treating int4 as \"half of fp16\"; symptom is a card that fits the weights but throughput below the fp16 figure because dequantisation dominates at the batch sizes used."],"when":"Run the arithmetic every time a model, card, precision, context limit or concurrency target changes,\nand before any procurement conversation. The inputs are all public: parameter count and layer/head\nshapes from the model config, memory from the card datasheet. Reach for it when someone asks \"how many\nusers can this card serve\", \"why did we OOM at 2 a.m.\" or \"can we fine-tune this on what we have\".\nMeasure the engine's reported KV-block count against your estimate; a large gap means an overhead\nterm you missed. In interviews, deriving bytes-per-token from the config and turning it into a\nconcurrency figure, then adjusting for precision and TP, is the expected depth for both engineering\nand architecture roles.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes weights and KV per token from the model config before setting `max_model_len`, `gpu_memory_utilization` and batch limits, and validates against the engine's start-up log.","architect":"Owns the fit table — which models run on which card tier at which precision — and uses it to size clusters and to decide when quantisation or TP is mandatory.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we have four A100s\" into which models and context lengths are realistic, and shows the arithmetic when a 70B request will not fit as asked."},"check":{"q":"You have one 80 GB card and a customer wants Llama-3-70B serving twenty concurrent 8k-token\nchats. Walk through whether it fits and what you would change.","a":"bf16 weights are 141 GB, so no. fp8 weights are 71 GB; with a 72 GB budget at 0.9 utilisation\nand ~1 GB overhead that leaves nothing for cache, so still no. int4 is about 39 GB, leaving ~32 GB\nfor KV at 320 KB per token, roughly 100k tokens; twenty chats at 8k need 160k, so about twelve fit.\nOptions: quantise the KV cache to 8-bit, doubling capacity to ~200k tokens, which meets the target if\nquality evaluation passes; cap concurrency at twelve and queue; or add a second card and run fp8 with\nTP=2 for ~220k tokens and better throughput. A strong answer also notes that twenty concurrent 8k\ncontexts will be bandwidth-bound on one card, so latency targets need checking too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a full fine-tune need roughly eight times the memory of serving the same model in bf16?","options":["Training must keep a copy of every activation for the whole dataset","Gradients, fp32 master weights and two Adam moments add about 14 bytes per parameter on top of the 2-byte weights","Backpropagation requires the weights in fp64 for numerical stability","The KV cache must be stored for every training sequence simultaneously"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mixed-precision Adam keeps bf16 weights and gradients plus fp32 master weights and two fp32 moments, 16 bytes per parameter versus 2 for inference, before activations. Training does not use a KV cache and master weights are fp32, not fp64."},{"q":"Llama-3-70B in bf16 (141 GB) is loaded on two 80 GB cards with tensor parallelism. What is the practical problem?","options":["The head count is not divisible by two, so TP fails","NCCL cannot shard models larger than one card's memory","After weights and overhead only about a gigabyte remains for KV cache, so the server can hold only a few thousand tokens","bf16 is not supported across tensor-parallel ranks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The usable budget is about 144 GB at 0.9 utilisation; weights take 141 GB and overhead about 2 GB, leaving under a gigabyte, roughly 2.4k tokens of cache at 320 KB per token. The model technically loads but cannot serve real traffic, which is why fp8 or four cards are the usual choices."},{"q":"Which single change most increases the number of concurrent 4k-token requests an 8B bf16 model can hold on a 24 GB card?","options":["Raising the number of CPU threads for tokenisation","Switching from grouped-query to multi-head attention","Quantising the weights to int4, freeing roughly 11 GB for KV cache","Increasing `max_model_len` to 32k"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With ~4.5 GB of cache room, int4 weights (~4.4 GB instead of 16 GB) more than triple the space for KV blocks. Multi-head attention would multiply KV per token by four and reduce concurrency; a larger `max_model_len` changes the worst case per request, not the total capacity."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/gpu-utilization-and-memory-bandwidth","sources":["Williams, Waterman & Patterson, 2009 — Roofline: An Insightful Visual Performance Model for Multicore Architectures","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Chowdhery et al., 2022 — PaLM: Scaling Language Modeling with Pathways (model FLOPs utilization)","Official docs: NVIDIA DCGM (profiling metrics)"],"why":"Teams buy GPUs for their FLOPs and are then surprised that an H100 serving a chat model produces\ntokens at a rate that would embarrass its spec sheet, while the dashboard shows 98% utilisation.\nBoth symptoms come from the same mistake: treating the GPU as a compute device when, for the workload\nin question, it is a memory device. Every decode step of a transformer must stream every weight byte\nfrom HBM into the compute units to produce one token per sequence. If only one sequence is in flight,\neach byte is used for a single multiply-add and the tensor cores sit idle most of the cycle, waiting on\nmemory.\n\nThe naive diagnosis, \"utilisation is high, so we need more GPUs\", spends money to replicate idle\nhardware. The naive fix, \"buy the card with the most TFLOPs\", buys the wrong axis. The roofline model\ngives the right question: how many operations does this workload perform per byte it moves (its\narithmetic intensity), and is that above or below the point where the card switches from\nbandwidth-limited to compute-limited? That one ratio explains why batching multiplies throughput\nalmost for free, why quantisation speeds up decode even when the math is not faster, why prefill and\ndecode behave like different workloads, and which utilisation metric to trust. Without it capacity\nplanning is guesswork dressed up as a dashboard.","how":"### The roofline\nA kernel's attainable throughput is the lower of two ceilings:\n```\nattainable FLOP/s = min( peak FLOP/s , arithmetic intensity × memory bandwidth )\narithmetic intensity = FLOPs performed / bytes moved from memory   (FLOP per byte)\nridge point          = peak FLOP/s / bandwidth                      (FLOP per byte)\n```\nBelow the ridge point the kernel is memory-bound and runs at `intensity × bandwidth`; above it, the\nkernel is compute-bound and runs near peak. Using NVIDIA's datasheet figures for dense bf16 tensor\nthroughput and memory bandwidth, the ridge sits around 312 TFLOP/s ÷ 2.0 TB/s ≈ 156 FLOP/byte for an\nA100 80 GB SXM, about 989 ÷ 3.35 ≈ 295 for an H100 SXM, and roughly 121 ÷ 0.3 ≈ 400 for an L4 (whose\nbandwidth is small relative to its compute). The number to remember is \"hundreds\": a workload must\nperform hundreds of operations per byte before the tensor cores are the bottleneck.\n\n### Decode intensity: why batch size is the lever\nIn one decode step, each weight is multiplied by one activation and accumulated: two FLOPs per\nparameter per sequence. In bf16 each parameter is two bytes and must be read once per step regardless\nof how many sequences share the step. So for a batch of *B* sequences the dense layers do about\n`2 × B` FLOPs per 2 bytes, an intensity of roughly *B* FLOP/byte. At *B* = 1 that is 1 FLOP/byte\nagainst a ridge of ~300: the H100 is using well under 1% of its arithmetic and is entirely limited by\nhow fast HBM can stream 16 GB of 8B-model weights. Doubling the batch doubles tokens per second at\nalmost no cost in per-token latency, until *B* approaches the ridge point or, usually sooner, until the\nsequences' KV-cache reads rival the weight reads (next section). This is the whole case for continuous\nbatching.\n\nThe lower bound on a decode step is therefore `bytes read ÷ bandwidth`. For an 8B bf16 model: 16 GB ÷\n3.35 TB/s ≈ 4.8 ms per step on an H100 (at most ~200 tokens/s for a single stream); on an L4 at\n300 GB/s it is ~53 ms (at most ~19 tokens/s). Real engines land below these bounds, but the ratio\nbetween cards holds, which is why decode speed tracks bandwidth rather than TFLOPs.\n\n### Attention does not batch the same way\nWeights are shared across sequences; KV caches are not. Each sequence's attention reads its own cache,\nso attention's bytes grow with *B* × context and its intensity stays low no matter the batch. With a\nhundred 4k-token sequences on the 8B model the step reads ~54 GB of cache (100 × 4096 × 128 KB) on top\nof 16 GB of weights, so the cache, not the weights, dominates the memory traffic. Grouped-query\nattention, KV quantisation and FlashAttention's tiling (which avoids writing the full attention matrix\nto HBM) all exist to reduce those bytes.\n\n### Prefill is the other regime\nPrefill processes all prompt tokens in one pass, so each weight byte serves every prompt token:\nintensity is roughly the number of tokens in the pass. A 2,000-token prompt is far above the ridge\nand compute-bound. That is why time-to-first-token scales with prompt length and TFLOPs, while\ntime-per-output-token scales with bandwidth, and why engines schedule prefill and decode differently.\n\n### What the utilisation metrics measure\n- `nvidia-smi` **GPU-Util** (DCGM `DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL`): fraction of the sample period in which *any*\n  kernel was executing. One tiny kernel per millisecond reads as 100%.\n- DCGM **SM activity** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_ACTIVE`): fraction of time at least one warp was resident on\n  an SM; **SM occupancy** (`DCGM_FI_PROF_SM_OCCUPANCY`): how full the SMs were; **tensor pipe active**\n  (`DCGM_FI_PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE`): whether the matrix units did work; **DRAM active**\n  (`DCGM_FI_PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE`): memory bandwidth in use. A decode-bound server shows high DRAM\n  activity and low tensor activity; a prefill-heavy one shows the reverse.\n- **Model FLOPs utilisation (MFU)**: observed tokens/s × FLOPs per token ÷ peak FLOP/s. It is the\n  honest single number for training and prefill; for decode it is naturally low, *because* the roofline\n  caps a memory-bound step far below peak FLOPs, so a low decode MFU is not by itself a problem.\n- **Model bandwidth utilisation (MBU)**: bytes a step must read (weights plus every sequence's KV cache)\n  × steps per second ÷ peak bandwidth. This is the decode counterpart of MFU: it asks how close the\n  server is to its real ceiling. High MBU with poor tokens/s means you need fewer bytes or bigger batches;\n  low MBU means something other than the GPU is in the way.\n\n### Other things that masquerade as GPU limits\nKernel-launch overhead when batches are tiny (CUDA graphs fix it), Python-side scheduling and\ntokenisation that leave the device starving, and host-to-device copies over PCIe. The tell is low DRAM\n*and* low tensor activity together: the GPU is waiting on the host, and no card upgrade will help.","example":"A small roofline estimator for decode. It takes datasheet bandwidth and peak compute, the bytes a step\nmust read (weights plus the KV cache of every sequence in the batch), and reports the bandwidth-bound\nstep time, the tokens/s ceiling and the batch at which the step becomes compute-bound. The outputs\nfor an 8B bf16 model reproduce the figures above; they are upper bounds, not measurements.\n\n```python\nCARDS = {  # datasheet-level figures, rounded: (memory bandwidth B/s, dense bf16 FLOP/s)\n    \"L4\":       (300e9,  121e12),\n    \"A100-80\":  (2.0e12, 312e12),\n    \"H100-SXM\": (3.35e12, 989e12),\n}\n\ndef decode_step(card, params=8.0e9, bytes_per_param=2, batch=1, ctx=4096, kv_per_token=131072):\n    bw, peak = CARDS[card]\n    weight_bytes = params * bytes_per_param\n    kv_bytes = batch * ctx * kv_per_token              # every sequence reads its own cache\n    bytes_read = weight_bytes + kv_bytes\n    flops = 2 * params * batch                          # dense layers only; attention omitted\n    intensity = flops / bytes_read\n    t_mem, t_compute = bytes_read / bw, flops / peak\n    t = max(t_mem, t_compute)                           # roofline: the slower ceiling wins\n    ridge = peak / bw\n    print(f\"{card:9s} B={batch:4d} ctx={ctx:5d} read={bytes_read/1e9:6.1f} GB \"\n          f\"intensity={intensity:6.1f} ridge={ridge:4.0f}  step={t*1e3:6.1f} ms \"\n          f\"tok/s={batch/t:8.0f} bound={'memory' if t_mem >= t_compute else 'compute'}\")\n\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=1)                  # ~4.8 ms, ~200 tok/s, intensity ~1\ndecode_step(\"L4\", batch=1)                        # ~53 ms, ~19 tok/s\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=64)                 # weights amortised; KV now a large share\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=100, ctx=4096)      # ~54 GB of KV per step dominates\ndecode_step(\"H100-SXM\", batch=8, bytes_per_param=0.55)  # int4: fewer bytes, faster step\n```","tradeoffs":["Larger batches raise tokens/s nearly linearly while memory-bound, but each sequence's latency grows once the step becomes compute-bound or KV traffic dominates; the flip is near the ridge point or when cache bytes exceed weight bytes.","Weight quantisation cuts bytes per step and speeds decode, yet dequantisation kernels can erase the gain at large batches where compute, not bandwidth, is the limit.","Speculative decoding converts spare compute into more tokens per weight read, which pays when decode is bandwidth-bound and acceptance rates are high, and costs throughput otherwise.","A faster-memory card (H100 over A100) buys decode speed roughly in proportion to bandwidth, not price; for prefill-heavy or training workloads the FLOPs gap matters more."],"pitfalls":["Reading `nvidia-smi` GPU-Util as \"busy\"; symptom is a fleet at 95% utilisation that delivers a small fraction of the tokens/s the bandwidth bound predicts.","Scaling out on utilisation instead of on queue depth or tokens/s; symptom is more cards, same per-card throughput, and a bill that doubled.","Benchmarking at batch 1 and extrapolating; symptom is a capacity plan off by an order of magnitude because batching was never measured.","Ignoring attention's share of memory traffic; symptom is throughput collapsing as contexts get long, even though weights never changed.","Blaming the GPU for host-side stalls; symptom is low DRAM *and* low tensor activity together, with a single Python process pegged at 100% CPU."],"when":"Reach for the roofline whenever throughput is below expectation, when choosing between cards, or when\nsomeone proposes \"more GPUs\" as the fix. Compute the bytes a decode step must read, divide by\nbandwidth, and compare with observed time-per-output-token: if they are close you are bandwidth-bound\nand need batching, quantisation or faster memory; if observed time is far worse, look at the host,\nkernel launches or scheduling. Monitor DCGM SM activity, tensor activity and DRAM activity rather than\nGPU-Util, and track tokens/s per card as the business metric. In interviews, \"why is decode\nmemory-bound and what does batching do to it\" is a standard question; the strong answer derives the\nintensity of ~B FLOP/byte and names the ridge point.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes batch limits, enables CUDA graphs and quantisation, and uses DCGM profiling metrics to prove whether a slow server is bandwidth-, compute- or host-bound.","architect":"Chooses cards by bandwidth for decode-heavy services and by FLOPs for training or prefill-heavy ones, and sets capacity targets in tokens/s per card rather than utilisation.","fde":"Shows a customer why their \"fully utilised\" cluster is idle in practice, and sizes the batching change that recovers throughput before recommending new hardware."},"check":{"q":"A customer's H100 serves an 8B bf16 model at 40 tokens/s per request with four concurrent\nrequests, and `nvidia-smi` shows 97% utilisation. They want to buy three more H100s. What do you tell\nthem?","a":"The bandwidth bound for one decode step is about 16 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s ≈ 5 ms, so a single stream\ncould approach 200 tokens/s and a batch of four should deliver far more than 160 aggregate. At 40\ntokens/s per stream the step takes ~25 ms, five times the bound: ~16 GB read every 25 ms is about\n0.65 TB/s, an MBU near 20% of 3.35 TB/s. GPU-Util at 97% only means some\nkernel was always running. Check DCGM: low DRAM activity and low tensor activity point to host-side\nstalls, kernel-launch overhead or a misconfigured engine, which more cards will not fix. Raise\nconcurrency and batch size, enable CUDA graphs, and confirm the engine is using fused attention.\nBuy hardware only after per-card tokens/s approaches the bandwidth bound at the target batch."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does doubling the batch size roughly double decode throughput on a bandwidth-bound GPU?","options":["The GPU clocks up under heavier load","Weights are read from memory once per step and shared by every sequence in the batch, so the dominant cost is amortised","Larger batches compress the KV cache","Tensor cores only activate above a minimum batch size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step must stream all weights from HBM regardless of batch size; with more sequences each byte does more useful work, raising arithmetic intensity. KV traffic still grows per sequence, so the gain is not unlimited."},{"q":"Which DCGM signal most directly reveals that a serving GPU is memory-bandwidth-bound?","options":["High GPU-Util with low power draw","High SM occupancy","High DRAM activity together with low tensor-pipe activity","Low PCIe transmit throughput"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandwidth-bound decode keeps the memory system busy while the matrix units wait, so DRAM activity is high and tensor activity low. GPU-Util cannot distinguish that state from a fully compute-bound one."},{"q":"Prefill of a 3,000-token prompt and decoding of its reply run on the same GPU. Which statement is correct?","options":["Both phases are memory-bound because they read the same weights","Prefill is compute-bound because every weight serves thousands of tokens per pass, while decode is memory-bound at one token per sequence per step","Decode is compute-bound because it runs more steps","Prefill is memory-bound because it must write the whole KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Arithmetic intensity in prefill is roughly the number of tokens in the pass, far above the ridge point; in decode it is roughly the batch size, usually far below it. Writing the KV cache is small relative to the matrix multiplications over thousands of tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/a100-h100-l4-selection","sources":["Official docs: NVIDIA A100, H100 and L4 datasheets","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference"],"why":"Three cards from the same vendor can differ by an order of magnitude in price and in decode speed,\nand the cheapest option per hour is frequently the most expensive per token, or simply cannot run the\nmodel at all. Teams that pick by hourly price end up with L4s that cannot hold a 70B model or that\ndeliver 15 tokens per second to a chat user; teams that pick by prestige put an 8B internal tool on\nan H100 that idles 95% of the day. Neither error shows up until the bill or the latency complaints\narrive.\n\nThe naive heuristic, \"buy the newest card\", fails because inference is usually bound by memory\ncapacity and bandwidth, not by the compute that generation-over-generation marketing emphasises, and\nbecause the cost of a token depends on how full the card is, which depends on your traffic, not on\nthe card. The other naive heuristic, \"buy the cheapest card that fits\", fails because fitting is not\nserving: a card that holds the weights with no room for KV cache serves one user, and a card with\n300 GB/s of bandwidth cannot reach interactive decode speeds on anything beyond a few billion\nparameters. Selection is therefore a short, repeatable procedure over four numbers — memory,\nbandwidth, price and expected load — followed by a feature checklist. This lesson gives the\nprocedure and the datasheet facts it runs on.","how":"### The cards, by the numbers that matter\nRounded figures from NVIDIA's datasheets; variants differ (A100 40 GB has ~1.55 TB/s, H100 PCIe\n~2 TB/s and lower TFLOPs, H100 NVL 94 GB, H200 141 GB at ~4.8 TB/s), so confirm the exact SKU.\n\n| | L4 | A100 80 GB SXM | H100 80 GB SXM |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Architecture | Ada Lovelace | Ampere | Hopper |\n| Memory | 24 GB GDDR6 | 80 GB HBM2e | 80 GB HBM3 |\n| Bandwidth | ~300 GB/s | ~2.0 TB/s | ~3.35 TB/s |\n| Dense bf16 tensor | ~120 TFLOP/s | ~312 TFLOP/s | ~990 TFLOP/s |\n| FP8 | yes | no | yes (Transformer Engine) |\n| MIG partitions | no | up to 7 | up to 7 |\n| Interconnect | PCIe only, no NVLink | NVLink 600 GB/s (SXM) | NVLink 900 GB/s (SXM) |\n| Power / form | 72 W, single-slot PCIe | 400 W SXM (PCIe variant 300 W) | up to 700 W SXM (PCIe 350 W) |\n\nThe bandwidth column is the decode column: the H100 streams weights ~11× faster than the L4 and\n~1.7× faster than the A100, and single-stream tokens/s follow those ratios almost directly. The\nmemory column is the fit column. The FP8 row matters *because* fp8 weights halve the bytes per decode\nstep and double the KV room with far less quality loss than int4. The A100 has no fp8 tensor cores:\nit falls back to int8 or int4 schemes, or to engine-specific fp8 *weight-only* kernels that dequantise\nto bf16 on the fly, which save memory and bandwidth but not compute. MIG matters for packing many small models onto one\ncard with isolation; the L4 cannot do it, but at its price you simply buy more L4s.\n\n### The procedure\n1. **Fit.** Weights at your precision plus KV headroom, from the sizing lesson. Roughly: an L4 holds\n   an 8B model in bf16 with a few concurrent 4k contexts, or a ~13B model in int4; an 80 GB card holds\n   8B with hundreds of contexts, 30B-class models in bf16, or 70B in int4; 70B in fp8 or bf16 needs\n   two or four 80 GB cards with NVLink. Anything that does not fit ends the discussion.\n2. **Per-stream latency.** Decode step time is at least `bytes per step ÷ bandwidth`. For 8B bf16:\n   ~53 ms on L4 (≤19 tokens/s), ~8 ms on A100 (≤125), ~5 ms on H100 (≤200). If the SLO needs\n   30 tokens/s per user, the L4 is out regardless of price.\n3. **Cost per token at your load.** Estimate the throughput ceiling at the batch your memory allows,\n   multiply by expected utilisation, divide price by it. The expensive card wins when traffic keeps\n   it busy; the cheap card wins when traffic is sparse. The example below runs this.\n4. **Features.** FP8 (H100, L4) for cheap precision reduction; MIG (A100, H100) for isolated sharing;\n   NVLink (SXM) for tensor parallelism; L4's low power and single-slot size for edge boxes and ordinary\n   servers; L4's video encode/decode engines for vision pipelines.\n5. **Availability and procurement.** Quotas, regions, reservation terms and spot pricing differ by\n   card; A100 supply is typically easier than H100, and L4s are widely available in small counts.\n   Cloud families to look for: AWS p4d/p4de (A100 40/80 GB), p5 (H100), g6 (L4); GCP a2 (A100),\n   a3 (H100), g2 (L4); Azure ND A100 v4 and ND H100 v5. Check the current catalogue before planning.\n\n### Training versus serving\nTraining and full fine-tuning are compute- and interconnect-bound: H100's bf16 and fp8 throughput and\nNVLink make it the default, A100 remains cost-effective for smaller runs, and the L4 is only sensible\nfor LoRA on small models because 24 GB and PCIe-only links cap everything else. For serving, step 2\nand 3 dominate, which is why the \"A100 is obsolete\" claim is wrong for inference: its bandwidth is\n60% of an H100's at a typically lower price, and for models under 30B it is often the best value.\n\n### Neighbours you will see\nA10G (24 GB, ~600 GB/s) and L40S (48 GB, ~860 GB/s) sit between the L4 and the A100 in price and\nbandwidth; H200 is an H100\nwith more and faster memory for long contexts; newer generations exist and follow the same procedure\nwith their own datasheet numbers.","example":"An 8B bf16 chat model, two traffic levels, illustrative on-demand prices of roughly $0.80 per hour\nfor an L4 and $4.00 per hour for an H100 (ballpark only; prices vary by provider and over time).\n\n*Ceilings.* On the L4, KV room allows about eight 4k-token contexts, so batch 8: a step reads 16 GB\nof weights plus ~4.3 GB of cache, ≈ 68 ms at 300 GB/s, so ≈ 120 tokens/s aggregate. On the H100,\nKV room allows about a hundred contexts: a step reads 16 GB plus ~54 GB of cache, ≈ 21 ms at\n3.35 TB/s, so ≈ 4,800 tokens/s aggregate. Both are upper bounds.\n\n*Cost per million tokens at full load.* L4: 120 × 3,600 ≈ 430k tokens per hour, so $0.80 ÷ 0.43M ≈\n$1.90 per million. H100: 4,800 × 3,600 ≈ 17M tokens per hour, $4.00 ÷ 17M ≈ $0.23 per million. At full\nload the H100 is roughly eight times cheaper per token despite costing five times more per hour.\n\n*Scenario A, internal tool:* 0.3 requests/s averaging 300 output tokens is ~90 tokens/s, which keeps\nthe L4 at about 75% of its ceiling and the H100 at under 2%. Hourly cost is what you pay: $0.80 versus\n$4.00; the L4 wins five to one, and per-user decode (≤19 tokens/s) is acceptable for an internal\ntool.\n\n*Scenario B, public product:* 10 requests/s averaging 300 tokens is 3,000 tokens/s. That needs 25 L4s\n($20/h) or one H100 at ~60% of ceiling ($4/h), and the H100 users also get several times faster\nper-stream decode (the batch-1 bandwidth ratio is ~11×; at these batch sizes the gap is smaller).\n\nThe crossover is wherever the H100's higher hourly price is covered by the tokens it actually serves.\nOne H100 costs the same as five L4s, which together top out near 600 tokens/s, so here the crossover is\nroughly 500–600 tokens/s of sustained demand. Two corrections move it in practice. Redundancy: a\nproduction service needs at least two replicas, and two H100s ($8/h) cost as much as ten L4s, pushing\nthe crossover toward ~1,200 tokens/s unless the second replica can be shared with other models. Latency:\nL4s at batch 8 deliver ~15 tokens/s per user; if the product SLO is higher, the L4 is out at any demand\nand the comparison is H100 against A100. If the demand is bursty, read the serverless lesson before\nbuying either.","tradeoffs":["H100 delivers the lowest cost per token and fastest decode, but only when load keeps it busy; at low or bursty utilisation its hourly price makes the L4 or an API provider cheaper.","A100 is often the best value for sub-30B serving and small training runs, but its lack of FP8 forces int8/int4 for memory relief, with the quality evaluation that implies.","L4 is cheap, low-power and easy to get, yet its 300 GB/s bandwidth caps per-stream decode and its 24 GB caps model size, so it flips from \"obvious choice\" to \"cannot do it\" at roughly 8B–13B.","SXM nodes with NVLink make tensor parallelism efficient for 70B-class models, at the cost of buying eight-card blocks; PCIe cards are flexible but make multi-card sharding slow."],"pitfalls":["Choosing by hourly price alone; symptom is a fleet of L4s that cannot meet a per-user tokens/s SLO or cannot load the model the product team actually wants.","Choosing by TFLOPs for an inference workload; symptom is paying H100 prices for decode throughput that an A100 would have delivered at 60%.","Forgetting the A100 has no FP8; symptom is a quantisation plan validated on H100 that must be redone with int8/int4 kernels and re-evaluated for quality.","Sizing to the 80 GB figure without KV headroom; symptom is a 70B bf16 model that loads on two cards and serves one request at a time.","Ignoring quota and region availability; symptom is an architecture built around H100s that cannot be launched where the data must stay."],"when":"Run the five-step procedure whenever a new model, SLO or traffic forecast arrives, and re-run it when\nprices or quotas change. Reach for the L4 for embeddings, rerankers, vision models, small LLMs and\nanything with sparse traffic; for the A100 when a 7B–30B model needs real concurrency at the best\nprice, or when H100 quota is unavailable; for the H100 for 70B-class serving, fp8, training, or any\nservice whose sustained load would keep it busy. Measure tokens/s per card against the bandwidth\nceiling and the actual utilisation against the plan. In interviews, expect \"which card for X and\nwhy\"; the strong answer names the binding constraint (fit, bandwidth or cost per token) before naming\nthe card.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Benchmarks the candidate card at the real batch size and context, confirms fp8 or int4 kernels exist for the engine, and reports tokens/s per dollar rather than utilisation.","architect":"Owns the card-tier matrix (which workloads go on L4, A100, H100), the procurement mix, and the policy for when a workload must move tiers.","fde":"Maps a customer's available hardware and quotas to what each model can realistically do there, and defends the recommendation with fit and bandwidth arithmetic."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs a 13B model in bf16 for 200 daily-active users and asks whether to buy L4s,\nA100s or H100s. Reason it through.","a":"Fit first: 13B in bf16 is ~26 GB of weights, so the L4 is out unless the model is quantised to\nint4 (~7 GB), which needs a quality evaluation. Both 80 GB cards fit it with tens of gigabytes of KV\nheadroom. Latency next: decode on the A100 is bounded near 26 GB ÷ 2 TB/s ≈ 13 ms per step, around\n75 tokens/s per stream, comfortably interactive; the H100 would be ~1.7× faster. Load: 200 daily\nusers rarely produce more than a handful of concurrent requests even at peak, well inside one A100's\nceiling, so an H100's extra throughput would sit idle. The int4 L4 route also needs its own latency and\nKV check: ~7 GB ÷ 300 GB/s ≈ 23 ms per step, under ~40 tokens/s per stream before batching, and if the\n13B model uses full multi-head attention rather than GQA its per-token cache is several times larger,\nso the remaining ~13 GB holds only a few long contexts. Recommendation: one A100 80 GB (two for redundancy), or an int4 L4 pair if\nthe quality evaluation passes and budget dominates; revisit if traffic grows past the A100's ceiling\nor fp8 becomes necessary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which card property most directly determines single-stream decode tokens per second for a given model and precision?","options":["Peak bf16 TFLOP/s","Memory bandwidth","Number of streaming multiprocessors","PCIe generation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each decode step must stream all weights (and the sequence's KV cache) from memory, so the step time floor is bytes ÷ bandwidth. TFLOPs matter for prefill and training, and PCIe only affects host transfers and multi-card traffic."},{"q":"A team validated fp8 serving of a 70B model on H100s and now must deploy on A100s. What changes?","options":["Nothing; fp8 checkpoints run on any card with 80 GB","The A100 runs fp8 tensor-core matmuls at about half the H100's rate","The A100 has no fp8 tensor cores, so they need a different quantisation path and must re-evaluate quality and speed","They must use the A100 40 GB variant, which supports fp8"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FP8 tensor cores arrived with Hopper and Ada (H100, L4); Ampere lacks them. Memory relief must come from int8/int4 schemes, or from fp8 weight-only kernels that dequantise to bf16 where the engine offers them; either way the kernels and numerics differ, which invalidates the earlier evaluation."},{"q":"An internal tool serves an 8B model at 0.3 requests per second. Why is an L4 usually the better choice than an H100 here?","options":["The L4 has higher memory bandwidth for small models","The H100 cannot run models smaller than 30B efficiently","At that load the H100 would sit almost idle, so its higher hourly price buys throughput nobody uses, while the L4's decode speed is adequate","The L4 supports MIG and the H100 does not"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per token depends on utilisation. The H100 is far cheaper per token at full load, but at a few percent utilisation the hourly price dominates and the L4's lower bandwidth still meets an internal tool's latency needs. The L4 does not support MIG."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/serverless-gpus","sources":["Official docs: Modal","Official docs: RunPod Serverless","Official docs: Google Cloud Run (GPU support)"],"why":"A dedicated GPU costs the same per hour whether it serves ten thousand requests or none. Most\ninternal tools, prototypes, batch jobs and early-stage products have traffic that is bursty, diurnal\nor simply small: a document-processing pipeline that runs for two hours each night, an internal\nassistant used during working hours in one time zone, a feature with a hundred requests a day. Paying\nfor 730 hours a month to use 20 of them is the single largest waste line in many AI budgets, and it\nis invisible because the card shows up as \"in use\".\n\nThe naive fix, \"autoscale the Kubernetes deployment to zero\", runs into the fact that a GPU node\ntakes minutes to provision, pull a multi-gigabyte image and load weights; by the time the pod is\nReady the user has gone, and the cluster autoscaler may not even have the quota to add a node. The\nother naive fix, \"keep one replica warm\", is just a dedicated GPU with extra steps. Serverless GPU\nplatforms exist to make the scale-from-zero path fast enough to be usable — pre-provisioned fleets,\ncached images, weight caches on fast storage, process snapshots — and to bill at the granularity\n(seconds) that makes bursty use cheap. They do not remove the cold start; they shrink it and hide the\nnode layer. Knowing what remains, and what it costs, is the engineering content of this topic.","how":"### The request path\nA request arrives at the platform's router. If a warm container for your function exists and has\nspare concurrency, it runs there immediately. Otherwise the platform takes a GPU from its pooled\nfleet (it keeps nodes and often images pre-warmed *because* node provisioning is the slowest step and\namortises across tenants), starts your container, runs your initialisation, and only then serves the\nrequest. After the container has been idle for a configurable scale-down window, it is stopped and\nbilling ends. Scaling out is the same path repeated: when in-flight requests exceed per-container\nconcurrency, another container is started.\n\n### Anatomy of a cold start\nEach stage has its own mitigation, and the total is the sum:\n1. **Scheduling and container start.** Seconds on a pooled fleet; minutes if a node must be created.\n2. **Image pull.** A multi-gigabyte CUDA image can take tens of seconds; lazy-loading image formats\n   and keeping weights *out* of the image reduce this.\n3. **Process start and imports.** Importing PyTorch, CUDA initialisation and an inference engine's\n   setup take several seconds. Memory snapshots (checkpointing the process after imports, offered by\n   some platforms) skip this on later starts; GPU-state snapshots are newer and provider-specific.\n4. **Weight load.** Storage to host RAM to VRAM. 16 GB of bf16 weights from a local NVMe cache takes\n   seconds; from a remote bucket it can take minutes. Platforms offer volumes or model caches on fast\n   storage precisely to move this term from minutes to seconds; a network bucket on every start is\n   the classic mistake.\n5. **Engine warm-up.** CUDA graph capture, kernel compilation, `torch.compile`: tens of seconds for\n   some engines. Either accept it, disable it for serverless, or snapshot after it completes.\nOrder of magnitude, illustrative: a tuned small-model function cold-starts in 10–30 seconds; an\nuntuned 70B deployment can take ten minutes. Measure yours; it is the key input to every decision\nbelow.\n\n### The knobs\n- **Scale-down window (idle timeout).** Longer keeps containers warm through traffic gaps at the cost\n  of paying for idle seconds; shorter saves money and causes more cold starts.\n- **Minimum warm containers.** Removes cold starts for the base load, billed continuously at the\n  serverless rate, which is usually above on-demand; this is where \"serverless\" quietly becomes\n  \"expensive dedicated\".\n- **Concurrency per container.** Letting one container handle several requests at once lets the\n  engine batch them, raising throughput per GPU-second; set it from the batching lesson, not to 1.\n- **GPU type.** Most platforms let you pick L4/A10-class through H100; the selection lesson's\n  arithmetic applies unchanged, except that per-second billing makes low utilisation free.\n\n### Billing shape\nYou pay per second of container lifetime (not per request), often for CPU and memory as well, at a\nGPU rate usually above like-for-like on-demand hourly pricing (compare the same card from the same class\nof provider): the premium funds the idle pool and the fast storage.\nCold-start seconds are usually billed, and so is the idle tail of every scale-down window: each\ncontainer that scales to zero was first paid for while it sat idle for the full window. Egress, storage volumes and request overhead may be separate lines.\n\n### What it is not\nServerless GPUs are *your container on their pooled GPUs*. Per-token model APIs are a different\nlayer: no container, no GPU choice, billed per token; they are the right answer for standard open\nmodels at modest scale. And not every \"serverless\" product has GPUs: AWS Lambda does not, and\nSageMaker's serverless inference option has been CPU-only; Google Cloud Run and Azure Container Apps\nhave added GPU options, with constraints that change, so check current support before designing\naround any of them.","example":"An 8B model on an L4-class GPU. Illustrative prices, ballpark only: dedicated on-demand $0.80 per\nhour ($584 for a 730-hour month); serverless $1.60 per hour-equivalent, billed per second, a 2×\npremium. Measured cold start after tuning: 45 seconds. Scale-down window: 5 minutes.\n\n*Workload A, internal document tool:* 1,000 requests per day at ~3 GPU-seconds each is 3,000 busy\nseconds a day, ~25 hours a month, $40. Requests cluster in working hours with gaps, producing about\n40 cold starts a day: 40 × 45 s = 30 minutes a day, ~15 hours a month, $24. The line people forget\nis the idle tail: 40 cold starts means 40 scale-downs, each preceded by a billed 5-minute idle window,\nso 200 minutes a day, ~100 hours a month, $160. Serverless total: roughly $225 against $584\ndedicated, about 2.6× cheaper, and the 45-second cold start is tolerable for a tool that already takes\nseconds. The idle tail is the biggest line, so the scale-down window is a cost knob: at 2 minutes it\nfalls to ~$64 (if the number of cold starts does not rise much), at 10 minutes it doubles to ~$320.\n\n*Workload B, steady product traffic at 40% busy:* 292 busy hours × $1.60 = $467, cold starts\nnegligible because containers stay warm. Serverless still wins by 20%. The break-even is where the\nrates cross: 0.80 ÷ 1.60 = 50% utilisation, minus whatever cold starts and idle windows add. At 60%\nbusy, serverless costs $700 against $584 and dedicated wins.\n\n*Workload B with a latency SLO:* p95 response under 2 seconds. Any cold-start rate above 5% puts\n45-second responses at the p95, and even 1% puts them at the p99, which most teams also track. Keeping one container always warm costs\n730 × $1.60 = $1,168, double the dedicated card. The right design is a dedicated card (or\nKubernetes replica) for the base load and serverless only for bursts above it, where the cold start\naffects a small tail of requests during ramps. The arithmetic, not the branding, picks the layout.","tradeoffs":["Per-second billing makes low and bursty utilisation cheap, but the rate premium makes steady high utilisation dearer than on-demand; the flip is near the ratio of the two prices.","Scale-to-zero removes idle cost but introduces cold starts; it is right when the SLO tolerates them or traffic gaps are rare, wrong for tight tail-latency targets.","Warm minimums and long idle windows buy latency with money, and past a point you are paying dedicated prices for a platform you do not control.","Pooled third-party hardware removes node operations but puts your weights and prompts in the provider's regions and tenancy model; for regulated data the compliance review, not the price, often decides.","Managed pooling hides nodes, drivers and images, at the cost of vendor-specific SDKs, less control over GPU topology and limits on multi-GPU or multi-node models."],"pitfalls":["Loading weights from a remote bucket on every start; symptom is cold starts of minutes that no scale-down tuning fixes, visible as a bimodal latency histogram.","Baking weights into the container image; symptom is image pulls that take longer than the model load and rebuilds for every checkpoint change.","Setting per-container concurrency to one; symptom is many containers each running a batch of one, so GPU-seconds per request are several times higher than on a batched dedicated server.","Forgetting that cold-start seconds are billed; symptom is a bill where start-up time rivals serving time, which happens whenever the scale-down window is short relative to request gaps.","Assuming serverless capacity is unlimited; symptom is requests queueing or failing with capacity or quota errors during a burst, because the provider's pool for that GPU type in that region, or your account's container limit, is exhausted exactly when everyone's traffic peaks.","Promising a tight p95 on a scale-to-zero deployment; symptom is an SLO breach on the first request after every lull."],"when":"Reach for serverless GPUs when utilisation would be low or bursty (internal tools, nightly batch,\nprototypes, long-tail models that are rarely hit), when latency can absorb a cold start or the\nplatform's warm pool makes it rare, and when you want no node operations. Prefer dedicated capacity\nor a Kubernetes deployment when sustained utilisation exceeds the price ratio, when tail latency is\ncontractual, or when the model spans multiple GPUs. Measure your cold start by stage, your busy\nseconds per day and your p95 with cold starts included; those three numbers decide. In day-to-day\nwork this is the \"why did the first request take a minute\" ticket; in interviews, the expected\nanswer walks the cold-start anatomy and the break-even calculation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Structures the function so imports and weight loading happen once at container start, keeps weights on a cached volume, tunes concurrency and idle windows, and measures cold-start stages.","architect":"Decides which workloads live on serverless, which on dedicated capacity, and where the hybrid boundary sits, using measured utilisation and the SLO rather than vendor claims.","fde":"Prototypes a customer pilot on serverless to avoid procurement delays, then presents the utilisation data that justifies moving to dedicated hardware when traffic becomes steady."},"check":{"q":"A customer's serverless LLM endpoint shows p50 latency of 1.2 s and p95 of 48 s. They ask\nwhether to raise the minimum warm containers to five. What do you investigate and recommend?","a":"A 48-second p95 against a 1.2-second p50 is a cold-start signature: at least 5% of requests\nstart a container. First measure the cold start by stage (image pull, imports, weight load, warm-up)\nand fix the cheap ones: weights on a cached volume instead of a bucket, snapshotting after imports,\ndisabling or pre-capturing warm-up. Then look at traffic: if gaps are frequent, a longer scale-down\nwindow may cut cold starts more cheaply than warm containers. If base load is steady, one warm\ncontainer removes most cold starts; five warm containers is dedicated pricing for five cards and\nprobably exceeds an on-demand deployment. Recommend tuning first, then one warm container, and move\nthe base load to dedicated capacity if the utilisation arithmetic says so."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do serverless GPU platforms charge a per-second rate above on-demand hourly pricing?","options":["Per-second billing is more expensive to meter","The premium pays for the idle pooled fleet, cached images and fast storage that make scale-from-zero fast enough to use","GPUs run at higher clocks in serverless mode","Serverless containers get larger VRAM allocations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The platform must keep provisioned GPUs and warm infrastructure waiting for tenants' bursts; that idle capacity is shared and amortised through the rate premium. Customers accept it because they pay only for busy seconds."},{"q":"Which change most reduces cold-start time for a function whose 16 GB weights currently download from object storage on every start?","options":["Increasing per-container concurrency","Choosing an H100 instead of an L4","Keeping the weights on a platform volume or local cache so they load from fast storage","Shortening the scale-down window"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Weight loading from remote storage is usually the dominant cold-start stage; moving it to cached NVMe-class storage cuts minutes to seconds. A faster GPU does not speed a network download, and a shorter idle window causes more cold starts, not faster ones."},{"q":"At what utilisation does serverless stop being cheaper than a dedicated card, ignoring cold starts?","options":["Always above 10%, because of the premium","Never; per-second billing is always cheaper","Roughly where busy fraction equals the dedicated hourly rate divided by the serverless hourly-equivalent rate","At 100%, when both bill the same hours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With a 2× premium the crossover is 50% busy; cold starts and idle windows are billed too, so the real crossover is lower. Above it the dedicated card's constant price covers more tokens."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/keda-autoscaling","sources":["Official docs: KEDA","Official docs: Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler"],"why":"The built-in Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) was designed for stateless web services whose CPU usage\nrises with load. An LLM server breaks that assumption twice. First, its CPU barely moves: the work\nhappens on the GPU, and the Python process mostly waits on CUDA kernels. Second, GPU utilisation (the\n`DCGM_FI_DEV_GPU_UTIL`-style \"percent of time a kernel was running\") reads near 100% as soon as one\nrequest is decoding, because a single sequence keeps kernels busy while using a sliver of the card's\narithmetic. So a CPU-based HPA never scales, and a GPU-utilisation-based one scales at the first request\nand then cannot tell 4 requests from 40.\n\nThe quantity that actually tracks pain is demand: requests waiting in the engine's queue, requests in\nflight per replica, or messages piling up in a broker for batch workers. The HPA can consume such\n\"external\" metrics, but only if something serves them through the Kubernetes external metrics API, and\nit cannot scale below one replica in a stock cluster (scale-to-zero is behind an alpha feature gate).\nTeams used to bolt on a Prometheus adapter, write custom controllers for zero, and duplicate\ncredentials for every queue. KEDA packages that: dozens of ready-made \"scalers\" for queues, databases,\ncloud services and Prometheus, an external-metrics server, and its own 0↔1 activation logic. For GPU\nworkloads, where an idle replica can cost dollars per hour, both halves matter: the right signal and the\nability to release the card.","how":"### Components\nKEDA runs as an operator plus a metrics API server in the cluster. You declare intent with custom\nresources:\n- **ScaledObject** targets anything with a `/scale` subresource (usually a Deployment or StatefulSet).\n- **ScaledJob** creates Kubernetes Jobs in proportion to pending work; each Job runs to completion,\n  which suits batch inference where a pod should not be killed mid-item.\n- **TriggerAuthentication / ClusterTriggerAuthentication** hold references to secrets or workload\n  identity for the scalers, so the queue password is not pasted into every manifest.\n\n### Two control loops, split at one replica\nKEDA does not replace the HPA; it drives it. The split is the most important thing to understand:\n1. **Activation (0↔1), owned by KEDA.** Every `pollingInterval` seconds (default 30) the operator asks each\n   trigger whether it is active, meaning above `activationThreshold`. If any is active and the target is at\n   zero, KEDA sets replicas to `minReplicaCount` or 1. When all triggers have been inactive for\n   `cooldownPeriod` seconds (default 300), it scales back to zero. `cooldownPeriod` governs *only* this\n   last step.\n2. **Scaling (1↔N), owned by an HPA that KEDA creates.** KEDA registers each trigger as an external\n   metric and generates an HPA pointing at it. The HPA then applies its usual rule: for an `AverageValue`\n   target (the default for most scalers), `desired = ceil(total_metric / threshold)`, clamped to\n   `[minReplicaCount, maxReplicaCount]`, with a default tolerance of about 10% to avoid flapping.\n   Scale-down is damped by the HPA `behavior` stabilisation window (default 300 s); scale-up has no\n   stabilisation window by default but is rate-limited by the default policy to the larger of +4 pods\n   or +100% every 15 seconds. You tune both through `advanced.horizontalPodAutoscalerConfig.behavior`.\n\nWith several triggers, the HPA computes a replica count per metric and takes the maximum, so you can\ncombine \"queue depth\" with a `cron` trigger that pre-warms capacity before the morning peak.\n\n### Why queue depth beats latency as a trigger\nScaling on p95 latency is tempting because latency is what the SLO says. But latency is a lagging,\nsaturating signal: it barely moves until the server is nearly full and then explodes (the queueing\nknee), and adding replicas changes it only after the new pods are serving, which for a GPU pod can be\nseveral minutes. A controller acting on that signal over-reacts and oscillates. In-flight plus waiting\nrequests is a leading, roughly linear signal: by Little's law, concurrency = arrival rate × time in\nsystem, so it rises the moment traffic does, and dividing it by a measured per-replica capacity gives a\nreplica count directly.\n\nRequest count has one weakness: it treats a 200-token chat and a 30k-token RAG prompt as equal load.\nThe engine's KV-cache usage gauge measures the real resource, but it is a poor *primary* trigger\n*because* it saturates: it sits near 100% whenever the server is full, whether one more request is\nwaiting or a thousand, so `ceil(metric / threshold)` cannot size the fleet from it. Two common fixes:\nkeep waiting requests as the proportional trigger and add KV usage as a second trigger (the HPA takes\nthe maximum), or scale on a token-weighted signal such as prompt plus generation tokens in flight when\nyour gateway exports one. Whichever you pick, re-measure per-replica capacity whenever the prompt-length\ndistribution changes.\n\n### What KEDA does not do\nKEDA changes a number in a Deployment. If the new pod requests `nvidia.com/gpu: 1` and no node has a free\nGPU, the pod sits Pending until the cluster autoscaler or Karpenter adds a node, subject to cloud quota\nand stock. Then the image is pulled and the weights loaded. KEDA cannot shorten any of that; it only\ndecides earlier. For plain HTTP scale-to-zero, something must hold requests while the first pod starts;\nthe separate KEDA HTTP add-on provides an interceptor proxy for that, and serverless GPU platforms exist\nbecause doing it well is hard.","example":"A vLLM Deployment serving an 8B model. A load test showed one replica holds its TTFT target up to about 16\nconcurrent sequences (illustrative: measure your own). The trigger sums running and waiting requests\nacross replicas and targets 12 per replica, keeping headroom for the minutes a new pod takes to arrive.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: chat-llm\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: vllm-chat                 # the Deployment\n  minReplicaCount: 1                # 0 only if a cold start fits the SLO\n  maxReplicaCount: 8                # match the GPU quota you actually have\n  pollingInterval: 15\n  cooldownPeriod: 900               # only for the final 1 -> 0 step\n  advanced:\n    horizontalPodAutoscalerConfig:\n      behavior:\n        scaleUp:   {stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0}\n        scaleDown:\n          stabilizationWindowSeconds: 600\n          policies: [{type: Pods, value: 1, periodSeconds: 120}]\n  fallback:                         # if Prometheus is unreachable, hold 4 replicas\n    failureThreshold: 3\n    replicas: 4\n  triggers:\n    - type: prometheus\n      metadata:\n        serverAddress: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n        query: >\n          sum(vllm:num_requests_running{model_name=\"chat-8b\"})\n          + sum(vllm:num_requests_waiting{model_name=\"chat-8b\"})\n        threshold: \"12\"\n        activationThreshold: \"0\"\n```\n\nWalkthrough: at 09:00 traffic reaches 70 concurrent requests. The HPA computes ceil(70 / 12) = 6 replicas\nand gets there within one or two 15-second policy periods (no stabilisation window; the default scale-up\npolicy allows +4 pods or +100% per period, and the `scaleUp` block above keeps those default policies). If only two GPU nodes are free, four pods go Pending until\nnew nodes join, which is why a cron trigger setting 4 replicas at 08:45 is often cheaper than SLO\nmisses. At 18:00 load falls to 10; the HPA removes at most one pod every two minutes after a ten-minute\nwindow, so a brief lull does not throw away warm replicas that cost minutes to rebuild. Note the vLLM\nmetric names follow its Prometheus exporter and can change between versions; check `/metrics`.","tradeoffs":["Scaling on queue depth tracks demand linearly and early, but it needs a measured per-replica capacity; when prompt lengths shift (longer RAG contexts), the same request count means more load and the threshold drifts.","Scale-to-zero saves the full idle cost of a GPU, but the first request after idle waits for node provisioning, image pull and weight loading; it flips to worthwhile only when idle hours dominate and callers tolerate minutes.","Slow scale-down keeps warm replicas through short lulls at the price of paying for idle GPUs; with per-second billing and fast-loading small models, a shorter window becomes the better bet.","One KEDA layer over standard Deployments is simpler than adopting Ray Serve or a serverless platform, but you own cold-start mitigation, request buffering and node provisioning yourself."],"pitfalls":["Scaling a scaled-to-zero vLLM Deployment on vLLM's own metrics; with zero pods the series vanish, the query returns nothing, and the service never wakes. Activate from a signal that exists without pods (gateway queue, broker depth).","Writing a per-pod average query while the trigger uses `AverageValue`; the HPA divides an already-averaged number again, and the symptom is a deployment stuck near one replica under heavy load.","Setting `maxReplicaCount` above real GPU quota; the symptom is pods Pending for hours while dashboards claim the autoscaler \"scaled up\", hiding a capacity problem behind a scaling one.","Expecting `cooldownPeriod` to slow scale-down from 6 to 2 replicas; it only applies to the last replica, and the symptom is replicas dropping after five minutes despite a long cooldown.","Letting the HPA remove a pod mid-generation without a long `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and a drain hook; the symptom is truncated streamed responses every time load falls."],"when":"Reach for KEDA when a Kubernetes workload's demand is visible in some external signal and CPU is not it:\nLLM servers, embedding workers draining a queue, nightly batch inference as ScaledJobs, or anything that\nshould sit at zero outside working hours. Do not reach for it to fix cold starts (it cannot), for\nsub-second bursty HTTP traffic that needs request buffering (look at serverless GPUs), or when you already\nrun Ray Serve, whose own autoscaler fills the same role. Measure per-replica capacity under realistic\nprompt lengths before choosing a threshold, then watch the replica count against queue depth, Pending\npods, and TTFT during ramps. In interviews, the signal is explaining why GPU utilisation is the wrong\nmetric and where the 0↔1 and 1↔N loops split.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the ScaledObject, chooses the Prometheus query and threshold from a load test, and adds graceful drain so scale-down never cuts off a streaming response.","architect":"Decides which services may scale to zero, the scaling signal per workload class, and how pod autoscaling couples to node provisioning, GPU quota and warm-pool policy.","fde":"Installs KEDA inside a customer's existing cluster, maps their queue or gateway metrics to triggers, and explains why the first morning request is slow unless a cron pre-warm is added."},"check":{"q":"A team autoscales its vLLM pods with an HPA on GPU utilisation targeting 70%. The deployment\nsits at its maximum replica count even overnight, when traffic is a fraction of the daytime peak, and\nthe GPU bill is huge.\nExplain what is happening and design a better setup.","a":"GPU \"utilisation\" reports the fraction of time any kernel is running, so it reads near 100% as\nsoon as one or two requests are decoding on each replica and says nothing about how many more could fit. The\nHPA sees every replica above target, scales up, and never finds a reason to scale down; the signal\ncarries no proportional information, so capacity is set by the cap rather than by demand. Better: a\nKEDA ScaledObject on running plus waiting requests summed across\nreplicas, threshold set from a load test at below-knee concurrency, fast scale-up, slow damped\nscale-down, `maxReplicaCount` equal to GPU quota, a cron trigger to pre-warm before peaks, and a node\nautoscaler with GPU node pools. If scale-to-zero is wanted, activate from a gateway or queue metric."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a KEDA ScaledObject, which mechanism controls scaling from 5 replicas down to 3?","options":["The `cooldownPeriod` field","The HPA that KEDA generates, damped by its scale-down behavior and stabilisation window","The `pollingInterval` field alone","The cluster autoscaler removing GPU nodes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KEDA itself handles only activation between zero and one replica, and `cooldownPeriod` applies to that final step. Between one and N, the generated HPA decides, using its behavior policies and window."},{"q":"Why is in-flight plus waiting request count a better trigger for an LLM server than p95 latency?","options":["Latency metrics cannot be exported to Prometheus","KEDA does not support latency-based triggers","Concurrency rises linearly and immediately with demand, while latency lags and spikes near saturation, causing oscillation","Request counts are unaffected by prompt length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By Little's law concurrency tracks arrival rate times service time, so it is a leading signal. Latency stays flat until the queueing knee, then jumps, and reacts only after slow GPU pods arrive."},{"q":"A ScaledObject scales a vLLM Deployment to zero using a Prometheus query over vLLM's own metrics. What happens when traffic returns?","options":["KEDA buffers the requests and replays them once a pod is ready","The service may never wake, because with zero pods the metric series do not exist to cross the activation threshold","The HPA scales from zero to one on its own","The cluster autoscaler detects pending requests and starts a pod"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trigger must be observable while nothing is running. Activate from a gateway, queue or broker metric, or use the HTTP add-on; the stock HPA does not scale from zero, and nothing buffers requests by default."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/ray-kuberay","sources":["Moritz et al., 2018 — Ray: A Distributed Framework for Emerging AI Applications","Official docs: Ray","Official docs: KubeRay"],"why":"AI workloads rarely fit one box or one shape. Batch inference over ten million documents needs CPU workers\nto read and tokenise, GPU workers that each load a model once and keep it resident, and back-pressure so\nthe GPUs are neither starved nor flooded. Hyperparameter search launches hundreds of short trials.\nMulti-node serving needs a group of GPUs placed together and started as a unit. Reinforcement learning\ninterleaves simulators, learners and inference.\n\nThe obvious tools each break somewhere. A job queue (Celery, SQS workers) has no notion of \"this worker\nholds a 16 GB model and should keep it\"; each task either reloads weights or you build actor management\nyourself. Spark was built for CPU data-parallel stages with a barrier between them; feeding GPUs a\ncontinuous stream and keeping a model warm per executor fights its execution model. Raw Kubernetes\ngives you pods, not a way for one Python program to fan out ten thousand fine-grained calls and pass\nlarge arrays between them without serialising through a database. Multiprocessing stops at one machine.\n\nRay's answer is a small set of primitives (tasks, actors, objects) with a scheduler that understands\nresources, plus libraries built on them for data processing, training, tuning and serving. Running it\non Kubernetes was the remaining gap: someone has to create the head and worker pods, wire up autoscaling,\nand upgrade a serving cluster without downtime. KubeRay fills that gap with CRDs, so platform teams keep\nKubernetes as the control plane while ML code stays Python.","how":"### Core primitives\n- **Task:** a `@ray.remote` function. Calling `f.remote(x)` returns immediately with an `ObjectRef` (a\n  future); the scheduler places the call on any node with the requested resources. Tasks are stateless,\n  so they can be retried elsewhere on failure.\n- **Actor:** a `@ray.remote` class. Instantiating it starts a dedicated worker process that keeps state\n  between method calls, which is exactly how a model stays loaded on a GPU across thousands of batches.\n- **Object:** task results live in a per-node shared-memory object store. Passing an `ObjectRef` to another\n  task moves the bytes node-to-node only when needed and lets same-node readers share memory without\n  copies. Results that do not fit spill to disk, which is slow but avoids crashes.\n\n### Scheduling by declared resources\nEach task or actor states `num_cpus`, `num_gpus`, memory or custom resources, and the scheduler only\nplaces it where those are free. These are *logical* reservations: `num_gpus=1` sets `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`\nfor the worker but does not stop it using memory it was not granted, and `num_gpus=0.5` lets two actors\nshare a card with no memory isolation. **Placement groups** reserve bundles atomically (gang scheduling)\nwith strategies such as `STRICT_PACK` (all bundles on one node, for tensor parallelism over NVLink;\n`PACK` is only a best-effort preference for that) or `SPREAD` (fault isolation). Engines like vLLM use Ray for multi-node tensor and pipeline parallelism for exactly this.\n\n### Cluster anatomy\nA Ray cluster has one **head node** running the Global Control Service (GCS, cluster metadata; the\noriginal paper called it the Global Control Store), the dashboard,\nthe job-submission API and the autoscaler, plus **worker nodes** each running a raylet (local scheduler\nand object store). The head is a single point of failure: lose it and, by default, the cluster's state\ngoes with it. The **autoscaler** scales on *pending logical demand*, not utilisation: if tasks are queued\nasking for GPUs that no node has free, it adds workers; idle workers are removed after a timeout.\n\n### Failure semantics\nRay's recovery model follows from the primitives. A failed task is retried (by default a few times) on\nany node with resources, *because* it is stateless. A lost object is rebuilt by re-running the tasks\nthat produced it (lineage reconstruction), which works for task outputs but not for objects created\nwith `ray.put`. An actor can be restarted (`max_restarts`), but its in-memory state is gone, so a\nmodel actor reloads its weights and any per-actor progress must be checkpointed by your code. Every\nobject has an owner, the worker that created the reference; if the owner dies, its objects are lost\neven if copies survive elsewhere. Design batch pipelines so a retried batch is idempotent and long runs\ncheckpoint their output, and treat the head node as the one component whose loss is not automatically\nsurvivable.\n\n### Libraries\n**Ray Data** streams datasets through CPU and GPU stages with back-pressure, the usual tool for offline\nbatch inference and embedding. **Ray Train** and **Ray Tune** wrap distributed training and\nhyperparameter search. **Ray Serve** runs deployments as replica actors with request-based autoscaling\n(a target of ongoing requests per replica), model composition and multiplexing.\n\n### KubeRay\nKubeRay adds three CRDs. **RayCluster** declares a head group and worker groups (each with pod template,\n`minReplicas`, `maxReplicas`); with in-tree autoscaling enabled, an autoscaler sidecar in the head pod\nedits worker-group replica counts, and a node autoscaler (cluster autoscaler or Karpenter) supplies GPU\nnodes. Scale-down is why this is an operator rather than a plain Deployment: Ray must choose *which*\nworker to remove (an idle one holding no actors or primary object copies), so the autoscaler names\nspecific pods for KubeRay to delete instead of decrementing a replica count and letting Kubernetes pick\none at random. **RayJob** creates a cluster, submits an entrypoint and can tear the cluster down when it\nfinishes, so a nightly batch run pays for GPUs only while it runs. **RayService** runs a Serve application\nand handles cluster-spec upgrades by bringing up a new cluster, waiting until it is healthy, then\nswitching traffic; changes to only the Serve config are applied in place on the running cluster.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: ray.io/v1\nkind: RayJob\nmetadata: {name: nightly-embed}\nspec:\n  entrypoint: python embed.py\n  shutdownAfterJobFinishes: true\n  rayClusterSpec:\n    enableInTreeAutoscaling: true\n    headGroupSpec: {rayStartParams: {}, template: {...}}         # CPU-only head\n    workerGroupSpecs:\n      - groupName: gpu\n        minReplicas: 0\n        maxReplicas: 8\n        rayStartParams: {}\n        template: {...}                                          # requests nvidia.com/gpu: 1\n```","example":"Embedding 10 million documents with Ray Data. CPU stages read and clean; four GPU actors each load the\nmodel once and receive batches as they are ready. Under the RayJob above, the autoscaler sees demand for\nfour GPUs, raises the worker group to four, and the job deletes the cluster when done.\n\n```python\nimport ray\n\nclass Embedder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Runs once per actor: the weights stay resident on that actor's GPU.\n        from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n        self.model = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\", device=\"cuda\")\n\n    def __call__(self, batch: dict) -> dict:\n        texts = [str(t)[:2000] for t in batch[\"text\"]]\n        batch[\"embedding\"] = self.model.encode(texts, batch_size=128)  # (n, 384) array\n        return batch\n\nray.init()  # inside a RayJob this connects to the cluster it created\n\nds = ray.data.read_parquet(\"s3://corpus/docs/\")                    # streamed, CPU tasks\nds = ds.filter(lambda row: row[\"text\"] is not None)                # CPU tasks\nds = ds.map_batches(Embedder, batch_size=512, num_gpus=1, concurrency=4)  # 4 GPU actors\nds.write_parquet(\"s3://corpus/embeddings/\")                         # triggers execution\n\n# Ray Data executes lazily and streams: reading, filtering and embedding overlap, and\n# back-pressure stops readers from filling the object store when GPUs fall behind.\n```\n\nRough cost reasoning (illustrative): if one GPU actor sustains about 1,000 documents per second, four\nactors finish 10 million in roughly 40 minutes plus cluster start-up, and `shutdownAfterJobFinishes`\nmeans you pay for about that long, not for an always-on pool. The argument names (`concurrency`,\n`num_gpus`) follow recent Ray Data releases and have shifted between versions; check yours.","tradeoffs":["One Python program can express a whole CPU-to-GPU pipeline with stateful model workers, but you take on a second scheduler inside Kubernetes; for a single stateless model server, a Deployment plus KEDA is simpler.","Ray Serve gives request-aware autoscaling, composition and multi-node replicas in one framework, but ties serving to Ray's versioning and head node; a dedicated engine behind a gateway wins when one model per pod suffices.","Fractional GPUs pack small models densely and raise utilisation, but sharing is unenforced; it flips to harmful when one actor's memory grows and OOMs its neighbour.","Ephemeral RayJob clusters cost nothing while idle, but every run pays start-up and image pull; for frequent short jobs a long-lived RayCluster with autoscaling to zero workers is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["Mismatched Ray or Python versions between the client, head image and worker images; the symptom is cryptic connection or deserialisation errors at job submission rather than a clear version message.","Exposing the dashboard or job-submission port beyond the cluster; these endpoints have historically had no authentication by default and accept arbitrary code (check what your Ray version offers), and the symptom is unknown jobs (often crypto-mining) on your GPUs.","Declaring `num_gpus` that do not match what the code uses; the autoscaler provisions by declarations, so the symptom is idle GPU nodes held by actors or, conversely, two processes OOMing one card.","Running GCS on the head without fault tolerance for a RayService; the symptom is a head-pod restart taking every Serve replica with it. KubeRay supports external-Redis GCS fault tolerance for this.","Returning huge objects from tasks and holding the refs; the symptom is object-store spilling to disk and a pipeline that slows by orders of magnitude without erroring."],"when":"Reach for Ray when the workload is Python, distributed, and heterogeneous: batch inference or embedding\nover large datasets with GPU actors, hyperparameter sweeps, RL, multi-node model serving, or pipelines\nthat mix CPU and GPU stages with state. Reach for KubeRay when that runs on Kubernetes and you want the\ncluster lifecycle declared in Git. Do not adopt it for a single stateless model endpoint (an inference\nengine Deployment with KEDA is less to operate) or for SQL-shaped ETL (Spark or a warehouse). Measure\nGPU-actor busy time and object-store pressure in the dashboard. In interviews, explain tasks versus actors,\nlogical-resource scheduling, and why the autoscaler reacts to pending demand.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Ray Data pipelines and Serve deployments, sizes `num_gpus`, batch size and actor count, and reads the dashboard to find whether the CPU or GPU stage is the bottleneck.","architect":"Decides whether Ray becomes a platform layer on Kubernetes or stays a per-job tool, and owns head-node fault tolerance, version pinning, network exposure and GPU quota per worker group.","fde":"Deploys KubeRay into a customer's cluster, adapts worker-group templates to their GPU node pools and security policy, and locks the dashboard behind internal networking before handover."},"check":{"q":"A team runs nightly embedding of 20 million chunks with a Celery queue: each task loads the model,\nembeds 100 chunks and exits. GPUs show low utilisation and the job takes all night. How would you\nrestructure it with Ray on KubeRay, and why would it be faster and cheaper?","a":"The waste is reloading weights per task and idle GPUs between tasks, with CPU-side work (reads,\ntokenisation) serialised in front of the GPU. With Ray Data, model loading moves into an actor\nconstructor, so each GPU loads once; `map_batches` with a fixed actor pool streams large batches,\noverlapping S3 reads and filtering on CPU tasks with GPU encoding under back-pressure. A RayJob with an\nautoscaling GPU worker group sized from measured per-actor throughput, minimum zero and\n`shutdownAfterJobFinishes`, means GPUs exist only during the run. Validate by checking actor busy time,\nobject-store spilling, and per-GPU throughput before scaling out."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes a Ray actor from a Ray task?","options":["Actors run on GPUs while tasks run only on CPUs","An actor is a long-lived worker process that keeps state across method calls, while a task is a stateless function call","Tasks are scheduled by Kubernetes while actors are scheduled by Ray","Actors cannot return ObjectRefs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both can request any resources. The difference is state and lifetime: an actor holds, for example, a loaded model between calls, which is why GPU inference workers are actors rather than tasks."},{"q":"On what signal does the Ray autoscaler (as used by KubeRay) add worker nodes?","options":["Average GPU utilisation across the cluster","CPU usage of the head pod","Pending tasks and actors whose declared resource requests cannot be placed on existing nodes","The number of HTTP requests reaching the dashboard"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ray schedules logical resources, so the autoscaler reacts to unmet declared demand. Real utilisation is not consulted, which is why wrong `num_gpus` declarations directly cause over- or under-provisioning."},{"q":"A team gives two model actors `num_gpus=0.5` each on one 24 GB GPU. What does Ray guarantee?","options":["Each actor is limited to 12 GB of GPU memory","The GPU is time-sliced so each actor gets exactly half the compute","Only that both are scheduled onto the same GPU; memory and compute are not isolated","Ray will refuse the placement because GPUs cannot be shared"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fractional GPUs are bookkeeping for placement. The processes share the card freely, so if one grows its memory use, the other can hit out-of-memory errors; isolation needs MIG or framework-level memory caps."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/inference-clusters","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"One model server on one GPU has a hard ceiling: weights plus KV cache must fit in VRAM, decode is\nbound by memory bandwidth, and the number of concurrent sequences that fit sets both throughput and\ntail latency. Once traffic exceeds what one replica serves within its latency budget, the only moves\nare a bigger GPU (there is a largest one) or more replicas. More replicas means a cluster, and a\ncluster immediately raises questions that a stateless web fleet never had to answer.\n\nThe naive answer is \"put the model servers behind the same load balancer we use for the API tier and\nautoscale on CPU\". It fails for three reasons. First, requests are not interchangeable: one request\nmay be a 200-token chat turn and the next a 60,000-token document summary; round-robin sends the\nlong one to a replica that is already saturated and the tail latency of everyone on that replica\ncollapses. Second, the useful capacity signal is not CPU or even GPU utilization but how full the KV\ncache is and how deep the engine's waiting queue is, since a GPU at 40% SM utilization can still be\nunable to admit another sequence. Third, the cache makes replicas stateful: a request whose prefix\nis cached on replica A costs a full prefill on replica B, so routing affects cost, not just balance.\nAlso, a model that needs more memory than one GPU has (a 70B model in fp16 is ~140 GB of weights)\nmust be split across several GPUs that act as one replica, which is a topology decision, not a\nscaling knob. An inference cluster is the structure that answers all of these on purpose.","how":"### The layers\nA cluster is organised as nodes → replicas → engines, with a router in front and a scheduler\nbehind. Each layer answers a different question.\n\n1. **Replica.** One running instance of a serving engine with one copy of the model. If the model\n   does not fit on one GPU, the replica spans several GPUs on the same node using tensor parallelism\n   (each layer's matrices are sliced across GPUs and the shards exchange activations every layer).\n   TP stays within a node *because* it needs an all-reduce per layer per token, which is only\n   affordable over NVLink-class bandwidth; crossing the network for it would make every decode\n   step wait on the slowest link. (Rack-scale systems whose NVLink domain spans several trays move\n   that boundary from \"node\" to \"NVLink domain\", but the rule is the same.) Pipeline parallelism\n   across nodes is used only when a model cannot fit in one node or NVLink domain.\n2. **Model pool.** All replicas of one model (and one configuration: quantization, max context,\n   adapters). Scaling happens per pool, because a 7B chat model and a 70B reasoning model have\n   completely different capacity curves and traffic.\n3. **Router / gateway.** Receives requests, picks a pool by model name, picks a replica inside the\n   pool, streams tokens back. This is where LLM-aware routing lives.\n4. **Scheduler inside the engine.** Continuous batching admits new sequences into the running\n   batch each step as others finish; it is the reason throughput grows with concurrency until the\n   KV cache is full.\n5. **Orchestrator.** Kubernetes (often with KubeRay or a serving layer such as KServe) places\n   replicas on nodes with the right GPU type and count, restarts failures, and runs the autoscaler.\n\n### Routing that knows what a request costs\nA good router does not round-robin. The usual signals, in rough order of value:\n- **Queue depth and in-flight sequences per replica** (\"least-loaded\"), so a long request does not\n  land on a replica that is already at its concurrency cap. Replicas expose these as metrics\n  (vLLM publishes running/waiting request counts and KV-cache usage).\n- **Prefix-cache affinity.** Requests sharing a long system prompt or a conversation are hashed to\n  the replica that already holds that prefix, which turns a full prefill into a cache hit. The\n  simple version is consistent (or rendezvous) hashing on a fixed-length prompt prefix, so adding a\n  replica remaps only a small share of keys; more advanced routers track which prefixes each replica\n  actually holds (for example a radix tree of cached prefixes). Either way there is spillover to\n  least-loaded when the preferred replica is saturated *because* affinity is worth little if it adds\n  queueing delay.\n- **Session stickiness for multi-turn chats**, a special case of the above.\n- **Priority classes**, so interactive traffic is admitted ahead of batch jobs that share the pool.\n\n### Disaggregated prefill and decode\nPrefill is compute-bound (big matrix multiplies over the whole prompt); decode is bandwidth-bound\n(one token per step, reading the weights and the whole cache). Running both on the same replica\nmakes a 40k-token prefill stall every decode stream on that GPU for hundreds of milliseconds to\nseveral seconds depending on model size, which shows up as TPOT jitter. Chunked prefill, which\nsplits a long prompt into pieces interleaved with decode steps, is the first mitigation inside one\nreplica. Larger clusters go further and split the phases: prefill replicas compute the KV cache and ship it over\na fast interconnect to decode replicas, each class sized and scaled separately. The cost is the KV\ntransfer and more moving parts, so it pays only at scale with long prompts.\n\n### Scaling on the right signal\nThe autoscaler should read engine metrics: waiting-queue length, KV-cache utilization, TTFT, or\ntokens/second against the replica's measured capacity. CPU is meaningless and even GPU utilization\nis misleading, since a replica with its KV cache full of idle-but-live sessions is \"available\" by\nutilization and \"full\" by admission. Scale-up is slow (pulling tens of GB of weights and warming\nthe engine can take minutes), so the cluster needs headroom and predictive or scheduled scaling\nrather than purely reactive rules; scale-down must drain in-flight streams before killing a pod.\n\n### Node topology and placement\nReplicas request whole GPUs (or MIG slices for small models) and must be pinned to node pools that\nhave the right card, because a 70B replica scheduled onto an L4 node simply fails. Spreading\nreplicas of a pool across nodes and zones limits blast radius from a node failure; packing them\nraises the chance that a single node loss removes a whole pool. Both are deliberate choices.\n\n### Admission control, failure and rollout\nWhen every replica in a pool is at its concurrency cap, something must say no, and it should be the\ngateway, not the engine. Engines queue internally without bound by default, so an overloaded pool\nlooks like slowly rising TTFT until clients time out and retry, which adds load. A gateway that\nbounds the wait (reject with 429 and a `Retry-After` once the expected queue time exceeds the TTFT\nbudget, or shed low-priority traffic first) turns overload into a fast, explicit signal *because*\na request that will miss its deadline is cheaper to refuse than to serve late.\n\nRollouts are a capacity event. A rolling update of a pool sized with no spare removes one replica\nper step, and the new replica arrives with a cold prefix cache, so the pool is both smaller and\nslower until it warms. That is why the N+1 spare doubles as rollout surge capacity, and why a model\n*version* change (new weights, new behaviour) is usually done blue/green as a separate pool with\ntraffic shifted by weight, so that rollback is a routing change rather than a redeploy that has to\npull tens of GB of weights again.","example":"Sizing a pool and routing inside it. The capacity numbers are illustrative placeholders you would\nreplace with your own load-test results.\n\n```python\nimport math, hashlib\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Replica:\n    name: str\n    max_seqs: int            # concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets the budget (load-tested)\n    running: int = 0\n    hot_prefixes: set = field(default_factory=set)\n\ndef replicas_needed(peak_out_tok_s, out_tok_s_per_replica, headroom=1.3, n_plus=1):\n    \"\"\"Pool size from peak decode demand, with headroom for burst and N+1 for a node loss.\"\"\"\n    return math.ceil(peak_out_tok_s * headroom / out_tok_s_per_replica) + n_plus\n\n# Peak 6,000 output tok/s; a 2xH100 TP=2 replica sustains ~1,500 tok/s within budget (illustrative).\nprint(\"replicas:\", replicas_needed(6_000, 1_500))      # -> ceil(5.2)+1 = 7\n\npool = [Replica(f\"r{i}\", max_seqs=48) for i in range(7)]\n\ndef route(prompt: str, prefix_chars: int = 2048) -> Replica:\n    # Affinity only works if the shared prefix (system prompt) is at least prefix_chars long.\n    key = hashlib.sha1(prompt[:prefix_chars].encode()).hexdigest()\n    preferred = pool[int(key, 16) % len(pool)]  # modulo for brevity; use consistent hashing in prod\n    if preferred.running < preferred.max_seqs:\n        preferred.hot_prefixes.add(key); preferred.running += 1\n        return preferred\n    # Affinity is not worth queueing for: spill to the least-loaded replica with room.\n    cands = [r for r in pool if r.running < r.max_seqs]\n    if not cands:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"pool saturated: shed or queue at the gateway\")\n    best = min(cands, key=lambda r: r.running); best.running += 1\n    return best\n\ndef finished(r: Replica):             # call when the stream ends, or slots leak and the pool \"fills\"\n    r.running -= 1\n```\n\nThe sizing function makes the two hidden assumptions explicit: a replica's capacity is the\nconcurrency at which it still meets the latency budget (not its raw maximum), and one extra replica\ncovers a node loss without breaching it (if one node hosts several replicas, N+1 must be counted in\nnodes, not replicas). The router prefers the replica that already holds the prompt prefix, but only\nwhile it has admission room. The modulo hash is a shortcut: when the pool resizes, almost every key\nmoves and the cache affinity resets, which is why real routers use consistent or rendezvous hashing.","tradeoffs":["Many small replicas (TP=1) give fine-grained scaling and isolation; a few large TP replicas give higher per-replica throughput and are mandatory once weights exceed one GPU, but a single failure removes a bigger slice of capacity.","Prefix-affinity routing cuts prefill cost for shared prompts, but it concentrates load; when one tenant's prefix is hot, you must spill to other replicas or that replica becomes the bottleneck.","Disaggregating prefill from decode stabilises TPOT and lets each phase scale on its own signal, at the price of KV-cache transfer and a second pool to operate; below a few replicas it is not worth it.","Headroom (idle capacity) buys tail-latency protection and absorbs slow scale-up; every point of headroom is paid GPU time, so the right level depends on how spiky traffic is and how long a cold replica takes to become ready.","Spreading replicas across zones improves availability but costs cross-zone traffic and sometimes loses access to the cheapest GPU capacity pool, which flips the decision for batch workloads."],"pitfalls":["Autoscaling on CPU or GPU utilization; the symptom is replicas reporting \"healthy\" and under 50% utilization while TTFT climbs into seconds because the KV cache or waiting queue is full.","Round-robin routing across replicas; the symptom is p99 latency far above p50 with no correlation to overall load, caused by long requests piling onto already-busy replicas.","Killing pods on scale-down without draining; the symptom is a burst of truncated streams and client retries every time the autoscaler steps down.","Treating a tensor-parallel replica as N independent GPUs in the capacity model; the symptom is a plan that promises N× the throughput a TP group actually delivers.","Scheduling a replica onto a node pool with the wrong GPU type or too little host RAM for weight loading; the symptom is CrashLoopBackOff with OOM during model load rather than at serving time.","No readiness gate that waits for the engine's warm-up; the symptom is a wave of errors and timeouts right after every scale-up or rollout because traffic hits a replica still loading weights."],"when":"You need a cluster the moment one replica cannot meet peak demand within the latency budget, when\nyou need to survive a node loss without an outage, or when you serve more than one model. Below\nthat, one well-tuned replica (or a hosted API) is simpler and cheaper. When you do build one, decide\nthe replica shape per model (TP degree, quantization, max context), the routing policy (least-loaded\nfirst, prefix affinity second), the scaling signal (queue depth and KV utilization, never CPU), and\nthe headroom. Measure a replica's capacity curve under load before writing any autoscaling rule, and\nkeep TTFT, TPOT, queue depth and KV usage on one dashboard. In interviews, expect to be asked why\nyou would not use the normal load balancer and autoscaler, and to reason from KV-cache memory and\nprefill/decode asymmetry to the answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the engine (TP degree, max sequences, max context), exposes its metrics, writes the router's selection logic and the autoscaler's queries, and load-tests each replica shape.","architect":"Decides pool boundaries per model, TP versus more replicas, whether to disaggregate prefill and decode, zone spreading, and the headroom policy that balances cost against tail latency.","fde":"Sizes a customer's cluster from their traffic shape and GPU inventory, explains why their existing Kubernetes autoscaler misbehaves with LLM pods, and sets up the right metrics and drain behaviour."},"check":{"q":"A team runs eight identical vLLM replicas behind an ordinary round-robin load balancer with\nCPU-based autoscaling. Under load, p50 latency is fine but p99 TTFT is 20× p50 and the autoscaler\nnever fires. Diagnose the cause and describe the fix, including what you would measure.","a":"Round-robin ignores that LLM requests vary 100× in length and occupy a replica for seconds to\nminutes, so long requests pile onto replicas that are already at their concurrency cap; those\nreplicas queue new arrivals, which is the p99 tail. CPU never rises because decode is GPU-bandwidth\nbound and the pods are waiting on the GPU, so a CPU rule sees an idle fleet. The fix is to route on\nengine state (least in-flight sequences, with prefix affinity when a prefix is hot) and to scale on\nengine metrics: waiting-queue length and KV-cache utilization, with a TTFT SLO as the alarm. Before\nchanging rules, load-test one replica to find the concurrency at which p95 TPOT still meets budget\nand treat that as its capacity; size the pool from peak tokens/second with headroom and one spare\nreplica, and add a readiness gate so new replicas take traffic only after warm-up."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is tensor parallelism normally kept inside a single node rather than spread across nodes?","options":["Kubernetes cannot schedule a pod across two nodes","It needs an all-reduce every layer for every token, which is only fast enough over intra-node interconnects","Cross-node tensor parallelism would duplicate the model weights on every node","The KV cache must be contiguous in memory, which a network cannot provide"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tensor parallelism slices each layer and exchanges partial results per layer per step; over a network that round-trip would dominate every decode step (rack-scale NVLink domains extend the boundary but do not change the reasoning). Pipeline parallelism is the cross-node option because it exchanges activations only at stage boundaries."},{"q":"Which signal is the best basis for autoscaling an LLM model pool?","options":["Average CPU utilization of the serving pods","GPU SM utilization reported by the driver","Engine queue depth and KV-cache utilization measured against the replica's load-tested capacity","Number of HTTP connections open on the load balancer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Admission is limited by KV-cache memory and the engine's scheduler, not by CPU; GPU utilization can be low while the cache is full of live sessions. Queue depth and cache usage reflect whether the replica can admit more work."},{"q":"Prefix-affinity routing improves cost; under what condition should the router ignore it?","options":["When the prompt is shorter than the model's context window","When the replica holding the prefix is in a different availability zone","When the preferred replica is at its concurrency cap, so spilling to a less-loaded replica avoids queueing delay","When the request uses streaming, since streamed responses cannot reuse a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cache hit saves prefill compute, but sending a request to a saturated replica trades that saving for queueing time and tail-latency damage. Affinity should be a preference with a spillover rule, not a hard constraint."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/shared-vs-isolated-model-serving-infrastructure","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Sheng et al., 2023 — S-LoRA: Serving Thousands of Concurrent LoRA Adapters","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"A GPU serving an LLM is efficient only when busy. Continuous batching reaches its throughput at\ntens of concurrent sequences; at two or three the GPU spends most of each decode step reading weights\nfor almost no output, so the cost per token can be an order of magnitude higher than at full batch.\nA tenant whose traffic peaks at five concurrent requests cannot fill a replica on their own. Pooling\nmany such tenants fills it, and because their peaks fall at different times, the pool needs capacity\nfor the peak of the sum, which is much less than the sum of the peaks. That is the economic pull\ntoward sharing, and it is strong: for a platform serving dozens of internal teams, dedicated\nreplicas per team can multiply the GPU bill several times for the same total tokens.\n\nThe naive conclusion, \"share everything\", fails the first time one tenant submits a batch job of\n50k-token documents. Those requests fill the KV cache, every other tenant's TTFT jumps, and the\nplatform has no way to say whose fault it is or to stop it, because the engine's scheduler is\nfirst-come-first-served by default (some engines offer a request-priority policy) and knows nothing\nabout tenants or fair shares. It fails again when a regulated tenant asks\nwhether their prompts ever shared a GPU with another company's, or when a tenant needs a different\nmodel, a longer context limit, a fine-tuned adapter or a different region. Sharing without\nisolation mechanisms is a cost optimisation that quietly removes fairness, blast-radius control and\ncontractual guarantees. The decision therefore is which of those properties each tenant actually\nneeds, and the cheapest level of isolation that provides them.","how":"### The isolation spectrum\nFrom cheapest to most isolated, each level buys a specific guarantee:\n\n1. **Shared pool, no tenant awareness.** Maximum utilization, no guarantees. Acceptable only for\n   one team's own traffic or a prototype.\n2. **Shared pool with logical isolation.** Per-tenant rate limits and token budgets at the\n   gateway, priority classes, fair-share admission so one tenant cannot hold more than a share of\n   in-flight sequences, and per-tenant metrics and chargeback. This buys fairness and attribution\n   while keeping the batching economics. It exists *because* the engine scheduler is FCFS or at\n   best priority-ordered, never tenant-fair; fairness must be enforced before requests reach it.\n3. **Reserved capacity inside a shared cluster.** A tenant gets a guaranteed minimum (their own\n   replicas, or a reserved share of the pool) and may burst into the shared remainder. This is the\n   self-hosted analogue of what hosted providers sell as provisioned throughput next to a\n   pay-as-you-go pool.\n4. **Dedicated replicas or node pool.** Separate engine processes, possibly separate GPUs, so a\n   tenant's KV cache and queue are physically theirs. Buys latency isolation and lets the tenant run\n   a different model, context limit or quantization. Costs idle capacity whenever the tenant is quiet.\n5. **Dedicated cluster, VPC or cloud account.** Separate control plane, network and identity. Buys\n   the strongest compliance story (data residency, no shared control plane, independent blast\n   radius) and the highest operating cost, because every platform component is duplicated.\n\n### The multiplexing arithmetic\nThe saving from sharing has a precise form. With tenant loads Lᵢ(t) measured in concurrent\nsequences and a replica holding C sequences within the latency budget:\n\n```\nisolated replicas = Σᵢ ( ceil(max_t Lᵢ(t) / C) + spareᵢ )\nshared replicas   = ceil( max_t Σᵢ Lᵢ(t) / C ) + spare\n```\n\nTwo effects separate them. Rounding: every isolated tenant pays for at least one whole replica (and\nits own spare) even if it peaks at a tenth of one. Multiplexing: the peak of the sum is lower than\nthe sum of the peaks whenever tenants peak at different times; for many independent tenants, the\naggregate's relative fluctuation shrinks roughly as 1/√N, so a large pool needs proportionally\nless headroom. The second effect is the one that flips. When tenants' peaks are correlated (all\nin one time zone, all driven by the same month-end process or the same upstream event), the peak\nof the sum approaches the sum of the peaks and sharing saves little beyond rounding and spares. So\nthe input to this decision is a per-tenant load time series, not a per-tenant average.\n\n### What is actually shared at each level\nSharing a *replica* means sharing GPU memory: tenants' KV caches sit in the same pool of blocks,\nand the batch holds their sequences side by side. Within a correct engine, one sequence cannot read\nanother's cache, so this is not a data-leak path in itself; the risks are side-channel timing,\nengine bugs and, more practically, prefix caching: if a cached prefix keyed only by its text can\nbe hit by another tenant's identical text, the cached KV is never returned to the caller, but the\nfaster TTFT of a hit is a timing side channel that can reveal that someone else recently sent that\nprefix. Cross-tenant cache sharing therefore has to be an explicit decision, and the usual answer is\nto salt the cache key with the tenant id (some engines accept a per-request cache salt). Sharing a *node* but not a replica removes\ncache co-residency and leaves shared host RAM, network and the node's failure domain. Sharing a\n*cluster* leaves shared control plane and shared operators. The compliance question is always \"what\nexactly is shared\", and the answer should be precise, because \"dedicated\" is sold at every level.\n\n### Multi-LoRA: sharing the base, isolating the behaviour\nWhen tenants need customised models, the expensive choice is a dedicated replica per fine-tune. The\ncheaper design keeps one shared base model and loads each tenant's LoRA adapter into the same\nengine; the adapters are small, swapped per request, and the base weights are shared. This gives\nbehavioural isolation (each tenant's outputs come from their adapter) with shared economics. The\nlimit is that all tenants must agree on the base model, context limit and quantization.\n\n### Logical isolation mechanics\nAt the gateway: per-tenant token-bucket limits in requests and tokens per minute, a concurrency cap\n(in-flight sequences per tenant), and a priority tag. At the pool: weighted fair queueing across\ntenants before admission, so a tenant with a backlog of batch work cannot starve interactive users,\nand the option to route a tenant's batch traffic to a separate low-priority pool. In observability:\nevery request carries a tenant id into metrics and traces so cost and tail latency can be attributed.\nWithout attribution you cannot even argue about whether a tenant deserves dedicated capacity.\n\n### How the decision is made\nScore each tenant on four questions. Does their traffic shape threaten others (long contexts,\nbursty batch jobs)? Do they need guarantees others would violate (latency SLO, data separation,\nregion)? Do they need a different model configuration? Would their dedicated capacity sit idle most\nof the day? A \"yes\" to any of the first three is a reason for separation; a \"no\" to the last means\nthat separation will be expensive. A contractual or regulatory requirement wins regardless of\ncost (and the cost goes on the contract); a traffic-shape problem is first attacked with logical\nisolation or a separate low-priority pool, and becomes dedicated capacity only when the tenant can\nfill it. Everyone else lives in the shared pool behind logical isolation. The platform ends up with a shared pool plus\na short list of exceptions, each with a documented reason.","example":"A worked comparison for a platform with ten internal product teams on one 70B model. Numbers are\nillustrative and come from the kind of measurements you would make yourself.\n\nEach team peaks at 20 concurrent sequences, but the peaks are spread over the day; the measured\npeak of the combined traffic is 80 concurrent sequences, not 200. A replica holds 48 concurrent\nsequences within the latency budget.\n\n- **Isolated per team:** every team needs its own replica, since no team's traffic can be combined\n  with another's. 10 replicas, each averaging perhaps 6 of 48 slots busy over the day.\n  Utilization near 12%, and that is before giving each team its own spare for N+1.\n- **Shared with logical isolation:** peak of the sum is 80, so ceil(80 / 48) = 2 replicas, plus\n  one for N+1 and headroom gives 3. The same 60 average busy slots now sit on 144 slots instead of\n  480, about 42% utilization (roughly 3.3x higher), and the bill is 3/10 = 30% of the isolated\n  design for the same tokens.\n- **Hybrid:** one team runs nightly document batches with 60k-token prompts. In the shared pool\n  those jobs would fill the KV cache during the batch window. Give that team a dedicated replica\n  for batch (or route their batch traffic to a low-priority pool), keep their interactive traffic\n  in the shared pool. Total: 4 replicas, fairness preserved. If one of the ten teams handles\n  regulated data and its compliance rule says \"no shared GPU\", none of this satisfies it: that\n  team needs level 4 or 5, at least two replicas of its own for N+1, and a cost line attached.\n\nThe arithmetic that matters is peak-of-sum versus sum-of-peaks, and whether a tenant's shape\nbreaks the shared pool. Whenever the second is true, the first stops mattering for that tenant.\nRun the same numbers with all ten teams peaking in the same hour: the combined peak is then close\nto 200 sequences, ceil(200 / 48) = 5 replicas plus a spare, and sharing saves 40% instead of 70%,\nalmost all of it from rounding and shared spares rather than multiplexing.","tradeoffs":["Sharing raises utilization and lowers cost per token, but it removes latency isolation; the balance flips when one tenant's traffic shape (long prompts, batch bursts) dominates the pool.","Logical isolation at the gateway preserves batching economics and gives fairness, but it cannot prevent cache contention once requests are admitted, so it does not satisfy hard latency SLOs.","Dedicated replicas give predictable latency and freedom to change model configuration, but capacity sits idle outside the tenant's peak; it flips to worthwhile when the tenant's own traffic can fill a replica most of the day.","Multi-LoRA sharing lets many customised models ride one base at shared cost, but constrains every tenant to the same base model, context and quantization, which breaks when one needs a different model.","Dedicated clusters or accounts give the strongest compliance story, yet duplicate every platform component and operator burden; they are justified by contract or regulation, rarely by performance."],"pitfalls":["Promising \"dedicated\" without stating the level; the symptom is a security review discovering the tenant's \"dedicated\" model shares a node, control plane or prefix cache with others.","Running a shared pool without per-tenant concurrency caps; the symptom is platform-wide TTFT spikes that line up with one tenant's cron schedule.","Keying a shared prefix cache on prompt text alone and not deciding whether cross-tenant hits are acceptable; the symptom is an auditor asking why tenant B's request was faster because tenant A had sent the same prompt.","No tenant id in metrics; the symptom is a cost-allocation argument nobody can settle and an isolation decision made on anecdotes instead of load shape.","Giving every tenant who asks their own replica; the symptom is a fleet averaging single-digit utilization and a GPU bill that scales with the number of customers rather than with tokens.","Treating isolation as permanent; the symptom is a tenant that outgrew or shrank below the threshold still paying (or still being subsidised) a year later."],"when":"Start shared with logical isolation: gateway limits, fair-share admission, per-tenant metrics. That\nis the default for internal platforms and for SaaS tiers without hard SLOs. Move a tenant to\nreserved or dedicated capacity when their traffic shape measurably damages others, when a contract\nspecifies latency or data-separation guarantees you cannot meet in the pool, or when they need a\ndifferent model configuration. Move to a dedicated cluster or account only for regulatory or\ncontractual reasons, and attach the cost to the contract. Measure per-tenant p95 TTFT, in-flight\nsequences and tokens, and pool utilization; revisit each exception quarterly. In interviews, be\nready to explain why continuous batching makes sharing cheap and why that same batching makes\nnoisy neighbours hard to contain after admission.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements tenant-tagged rate limits, concurrency caps and fair-share queueing at the gateway, and ensures tenant ids flow into engine metrics and traces for attribution.","architect":"Owns the isolation policy: which guarantees each tier of tenant gets, at which level of the spectrum, and the cost line that goes with every dedicated exception.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"we need our own model\" into the precise isolation level they need, prices it, and configures reserved or dedicated capacity inside their environment."},"check":{"q":"A SaaS company serves forty customers from one shared vLLM pool. One enterprise customer\ndemands \"dedicated infrastructure\" after a week of slow responses. How do you decide what to give\nthem, and what would you check first?","a":"First check whether the slowness is caused by them or by someone else: per-tenant p95 TTFT\nand in-flight sequences over the week, correlated with pool-wide KV utilization. If another tenant's\nbatch jobs caused it, the fix is logical isolation for everyone (concurrency caps, fair-share\nadmission, a low-priority batch pool), which solves the enterprise customer's problem without\ndedicated hardware. If their own traffic shape is the cause, or their contract names a latency SLO,\ngive them reserved capacity: a dedicated replica for their guaranteed minimum with burst into the\npool. Only if the demand is about data separation or residency do you move to a dedicated node\npool, cluster or account, and then the precise level of what is not shared goes into the contract\nalong with its cost. Dedicated capacity is reviewed against their utilization every quarter."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a shared model pool usually cost far less per token than dedicated replicas per tenant?","options":["Shared pools can use cheaper GPUs because the model is loaded once","Continuous batching only reaches full efficiency at high concurrency, and tenants' peaks rarely coincide, so capacity is sized for the peak of the sum","Dedicated replicas must each keep a separate copy of the training data","Shared pools skip the prefill phase for every tenant after the first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A GPU at low concurrency spends most of each decode step reading weights for little output. Pooling fills the batch and sizes capacity for combined peak rather than the sum of individual peaks, which is where the saving comes from."},{"q":"Per-tenant rate limits and concurrency caps at the gateway are in place, yet one tenant's long-context jobs still slow others. Why?","options":["Rate limits only count requests, never tokens","The gateway cannot see which tenant a request belongs to","Once admitted, sequences share the replica's KV cache and batch, so long contexts consume memory and bandwidth that gateway limits do not govern","Long-context requests bypass the gateway and reach the engine directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logical isolation controls admission, not what happens inside the batch; long sequences take many KV blocks and lengthen every step. Latency isolation needs separate replicas or routing long jobs to a separate pool."},{"q":"Which situation most justifies a fully dedicated cluster or cloud account rather than dedicated replicas?","options":["The tenant's p95 latency target is tighter than the pool's","The tenant wants a different context length limit","The tenant's contract requires data residency or no shared control plane","The tenant's traffic is the largest in the pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Latency and configuration needs are met by dedicated replicas inside a shared cluster. A separate cluster or account is justified by what must not be shared at all: region, control plane, operators and network."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cloud-ai-architecture","sources":["Official docs: Amazon Bedrock","Official docs: Azure OpenAI Service","Official docs: Google Cloud Vertex AI"],"why":"Calling a model vendor's public API from an enterprise application raises questions that have\nnothing to do with the model: who approved this vendor, where does the data go, which network path\ndoes the request take, how is it authenticated, where are the logs, who pays and under which\ncontract. Each of those is a review, and each review is slow. Meanwhile the company already has an\nanswer to every one of them for its cloud provider: IAM or Entra identities, private networking,\naudit logging, consolidated billing against a committed spend, and a signed compliance package.\n\nThe cloud AI platforms exist to let the model inherit those answers. A request to Bedrock is a\nSigV4-signed AWS API call that can travel over a VPC endpoint, with opt-in model invocation logging\nto CloudWatch Logs or S3, billed on the AWS invoice. An\nAzure OpenAI deployment is a resource in a resource group with Entra RBAC, a private endpoint and\ndiagnostic settings. A Vertex AI call is a Google Cloud API call under a project's IAM, VPC Service\nControls and Cloud Audit Logs. From the security reviewer's point of view, the model becomes \"just\nanother cloud service\", which is the point.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"it is the same model, use the vendor API and save the complexity\", fails on\nthe review calendar rather than on technology, and later on data terms (training opt-outs, retention,\nresidency) that the cloud contracts fix in writing. It also fails on capacity: committed cloud\ncustomers can buy provisioned throughput in their own region, which a public pay-as-you-go API does\nnot offer in the same form. The opposite naive alternative, \"self-host everything on our GPUs\", fails\nfor most teams on model quality and operations cost, which is why the managed route is the default\nin large organisations.","how":"### The common shape\nAll three platforms present the same architectural layers, and your design decisions live in each:\n\n1. **Identity.** No API keys from the model vendor. Bedrock uses IAM principals and policies per\n   model; Azure OpenAI uses Entra ID (keys exist but are the weaker option); Vertex uses Google\n   Cloud IAM and service accounts. Decision: which workload identities may call which models.\n2. **Network.** Private connectivity so traffic never crosses the public internet: VPC endpoints\n   (PrivateLink) for Bedrock, Private Endpoints for Azure OpenAI, Private Service Connect and VPC\n   Service Controls for Vertex. Decision: where the endpoint sits and which networks can reach it.\n3. **Model catalog and endpoint.** Bedrock offers models from several vendors (Amazon's own,\n   Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others) behind one API, with the Converse API as the model-agnostic\n   interface. Azure OpenAI hosts OpenAI models, and you create a named *deployment* of a model\n   version with a capacity setting; the wider Azure AI Foundry catalog (since rebranded Microsoft\n   Foundry; these product names change often) adds other vendors' models.\n   Vertex serves Gemini natively and partner models (Anthropic, Meta, Mistral and others) through\n   Model Garden. Decision: which models, which versions, pinned how.\n4. **Capacity model.** Two modes everywhere, with different names. Pay-as-you-go shares a regional\n   pool under quotas (requests and tokens per minute per model per region per account or\n   subscription). Reserved capacity buys guaranteed throughput: Bedrock Provisioned Throughput in\n   model units, Azure Provisioned Throughput Units (PTU), Vertex Provisioned Throughput. Reserved\n   capacity exists *because* the shared pool cannot promise throughput at peak; you pay for\n   dedicated model capacity in exchange for predictability. Batch modes exist for offline work at a\n   discount. Decision: which workloads run on shared quota and which need reserved capacity.\n5. **Region.** Models are available per region, and quotas are per region. Each platform has added\n   ways to route across regions when one is full: Bedrock cross-region inference profiles, Azure\n   \"Global\" and \"Data Zone\" deployment types that process within a wider geography, and a global\n   endpoint option on Vertex for some models. These trade residency precision for availability.\n   Decision: which regions satisfy the data-residency rule, and whether cross-region routing is\n   permitted.\n6. **Governance and logging.** Invocation logging to the cloud's log services, content filters\n   (Bedrock Guardrails, Azure's built-in content filtering, Vertex safety settings), and the data\n   terms: all three state that customer prompts and outputs are not used to train the base models.\n   Azure's abuse-monitoring may retain prompts for a limited period unless an exemption is granted,\n   which matters for regulated data and must be checked against current docs.\n7. **Adjacent building blocks.** Managed retrieval (Bedrock Knowledge Bases, Vertex AI Search and\n   RAG tooling, Azure AI Search), agent frameworks, evaluation and fine-tuning services. They are\n   convenient and deepen lock-in; treat each as a separate build-versus-buy decision.\n\n### Where the differences bite\n- **Model lag.** New models and features usually reach the vendor's own API first and the cloud\n  platforms days to months later, sometimes with missing features. If the roadmap depends on the\n  newest model, that lag is a real cost.\n- **Capacity semantics.** Azure's quota is a TPM allocation per model per region per subscription\n  that you split across deployments; Bedrock's on-demand quotas are per model per region per\n  account; Vertex has moved Gemini pay-as-you-go toward a dynamically shared quota rather than\n  fixed per-project limits. The practical consequence is the same: plan capacity per model per\n  region and request increases early, since increases are tickets, not API calls.\n- **SDK shape.** Bedrock has its own SDK (boto3) with two call styles: `InvokeModel`, whose\n  request body is each vendor's native format, and the Converse API, one message format across\n  models; vendor SDKs can also use Bedrock as a backend; Azure OpenAI uses the OpenAI SDK with an Azure client where\n  `model` means the deployment name (Azure has also added a versionless \"v1\" endpoint usable from\n  the standard OpenAI client, so check which style the current docs recommend); Vertex uses the Google Gen AI SDK or vendor SDKs with a Vertex\n  backend. An internal gateway or an abstraction such as LiteLLM usually hides this so that\n  application code is not rewritten when a model moves.\n- **Version pinning and retirement.** Each platform retires model versions on its own schedule;\n  Azure deployments can be set to auto-upgrade or pinned, Bedrock and Vertex pin by model id. A pin\n  is not forever: when a pinned version reaches its retirement date you must migrate (or, on Azure,\n  you may be upgraded), so the retirement calendar is part of the plan.\n  Unplanned upgrades change behaviour under your evals, so pinning plus a scheduled re-evaluation\n  is the safe pattern.\n\n### Choosing the capacity mode\nReserved capacity is a fixed hourly or monthly price for a throughput ceiling; pay-as-you-go is a\nper-token price with no ceiling guarantee. The comparison is the same arithmetic as owning GPUs:\n\n```\non-demand cost  = tokens actually served × price per token\nreserved cost   = units × price per unit-hour × hours (paid whether used or not)\nbreak-even utilization u* = reserved cost ÷ (tokens the reservation could serve at full use × on-demand price)\n```\n\nAbove u*, reserved is cheaper per token; below it, you are paying for a guarantee. The number that\nis hard to get is \"tokens the reservation could serve\", because each platform's unit (model unit,\nPTU) delivers a throughput that depends on the model and on the input/output mix: prompt-heavy\ntraffic and generation-heavy traffic consume a unit differently. Each platform publishes sizing\nguidance or a calculator, but the reliable figure comes from a load test of your own traffic mix\nagainst a small reservation. Two further facts usually decide it: a latency SLO at peak can justify\nreserved capacity below u*, since the alternative is 429s at the worst moment; and commitment\nterms (hourly, monthly, longer) mean a forecast error is paid for the length of the term.\n\n### The reference architecture\nApplication → internal AI gateway (auth, routing, quotas, logging, prompt and response capture) →\nprivate endpoint → cloud AI platform in the approved region → optional cross-region or second\nprovider fallback. The gateway is where you keep the option to move between platforms, which is the\nanswer to the second-layer lock-in that these services create.","example":"The same one-turn request through all three platforms. Notice what differs: Bedrock has no key and\nsigns with the IAM identity in the environment; Azure needs an endpoint, an API version and the\n*deployment* name; Vertex authenticates with Application Default Credentials and a project/location.\nModel identifiers are placeholders to look up in each catalog.\n\n```python\nimport os, boto3\nfrom openai import AzureOpenAI\nfrom google import genai\n\nQUESTION = \"Summarise our refund policy in one sentence.\"\n\ndef via_bedrock(model_id: str) -> str:\n    rt = boto3.client(\"bedrock-runtime\", region_name=os.environ[\"AWS_REGION\"])  # IAM creds, SigV4\n    r = rt.converse(\n        modelId=model_id,                                   # or a cross-region inference profile id\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"text\": QUESTION}]}],\n        inferenceConfig={\"maxTokens\": 200},\n    )\n    return r[\"output\"][\"message\"][\"content\"][0][\"text\"]\n\ndef via_azure(deployment_name: str) -> str:\n    client = AzureOpenAI(\n        azure_endpoint=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT\"],   # https://<resource>.openai.azure.com\n        api_key=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY\"],            # prefer Entra ID token auth in prod\n        api_version=os.environ[\"AZURE_OPENAI_API_VERSION\"],\n    )\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=deployment_name,                               # deployment, not the model family name\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": QUESTION}], max_tokens=200,\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\ndef via_vertex(model_name: str) -> str:\n    client = genai.Client(vertexai=True, project=os.environ[\"GCP_PROJECT\"],\n                          location=os.environ[\"GCP_LOCATION\"])  # ADC supplies the identity\n    r = client.models.generate_content(model=model_name, contents=QUESTION)\n    return r.text\n```\n\nIn production none of these calls live in application code; they sit behind the gateway, which\ntags each request with tenant, feature and region so that quota use and cost can be attributed and\na fallback can be configured without touching the application.\n\nThe decision the gateway's telemetry feeds is the capacity mode. A worked version with\nillustrative prices (not any platform's list price): a reservation costs $30,000 a month and your\nload test shows it sustains about 1.5 billion tokens a month of your mix at full use; the same\ntokens on pay-as-you-go cost $40 per million, so full use would cost $60,000 on demand. Break-even\nutilization is 30,000 ÷ 60,000 = 50%. If the workload keeps the reservation 70% utilised on\naverage, reserved costs $30,000 against $42,000 on demand; at 30%, reserved costs $30,000\nagainst $18,000, and only a peak-latency SLO justifies it. The usual design reserves for the base\nand lets the burst above it spill to a pay-as-you-go deployment, which the gateway does by trying\nthe reserved endpoint first and falling over on a 429.","tradeoffs":["You inherit the cloud's identity, network, logging and compliance perimeter, but you accept model and feature lag behind the vendor's own API; the balance flips when the product depends on the newest model capabilities.","Reserved capacity (PTU, provisioned throughput, model units) buys predictable throughput for peak, but it is paid whether used or not; it is worth it only when steady load fills most of it or an SLO makes shared-pool 429s unacceptable.","Consolidated billing against committed spend simplifies procurement and can discount the model bill, but it ties model choice to one cloud's catalog and makes a multi-cloud model strategy harder.","Managed adjacent services (knowledge bases, agents, guardrails) accelerate delivery, but every one adopted deepens lock-in and narrows the exit path; the trade flips when the component is commodity and replaceable behind an interface.","Cross-region and global routing options raise availability, but they loosen the residency guarantee; where regulation names a region, they must be disabled."],"pitfalls":["Passing the model family name instead of the deployment name to Azure OpenAI; the symptom is a 404 \"deployment not found\" even though the model is clearly available in the region.","Sizing capacity in one region and discovering the model is not offered there or the quota is lower; the symptom is a launch delayed by a quota-increase ticket.","Leaving auto-upgrade or default model versions in place; the symptom is a silent behaviour change that breaks evals or output parsing with no deploy on your side.","Enabling cross-region inference or global deployment types on a workload with a residency requirement; the symptom is an audit finding months later rather than an error at request time.","Calling the platform from application code directly; the symptom is a multi-week rewrite when a model moves clouds or a second provider is added for fallback.","Assuming the cloud's data terms cover abuse-monitoring retention and human review; the symptom is a compliance review rejecting a workload that handles regulated data until the exemption is arranged."],"when":"Reach for a cloud AI platform when the organisation already runs on that cloud, when identity,\nprivate networking and compliance scope are review gates, when spend commitments can absorb the\nmodel bill, or when you need reserved capacity in a chosen region. Prefer the vendor's direct API\nwhen you need the newest models or features first and your data terms are satisfied by the vendor's\ncontract. Prefer self-hosting when open-weight models meet the bar and volume makes it cheaper, or\nwhen the data cannot leave your own hardware. Measure quota headroom per model per region, 429 and\nthrottle rates, and time-to-new-model when a release matters. In interviews, the expected depth is\nexplaining the identity, network, capacity and region layers and how a gateway keeps the exit open.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires the gateway to each platform's SDK, pins model versions, handles each platform's throttling and error shapes, and tags requests for cost and quota attribution.","architect":"Chooses platform versus direct API versus self-host per workload, places endpoints inside the network and identity perimeter, and decides reserved versus shared capacity per region.","fde":"Deploys the solution inside the customer's existing cloud account, works through their quota, region and private-endpoint constraints, and documents which data terms apply."},"check":{"q":"A bank on Azure wants a customer-facing assistant on a frontier model, with EU data residency,\na p95 latency target and a launch in six weeks. Walk through the architecture decisions and the\nrisks you would raise.","a":"Use Azure OpenAI deployed in an EU region, reached through a Private Endpoint from the\napplication's VNet, with Entra ID identities and diagnostic logging to the bank's workspace. Check\nmodel availability in that region first, then request quota early; for the latency target, size\nprovisioned throughput for peak and keep a pay-as-you-go deployment for overflow, or accept 429s\nwith queueing if the SLO allows. Use regional deployment types (regional Standard and regional\nProvisioned) and avoid Global types; an EU Data Zone deployment keeps processing inside the EU and\nmay be acceptable, but only if legal confirms that the wider geography satisfies the residency rule. Pin the model version and schedule\nre-evaluation before any upgrade. Confirm the abuse-monitoring retention terms and apply for the\nexemption if required. Put all calls behind a gateway so a second region or provider can be added\nas fallback. The biggest risks are quota lead time, model availability in the region, and\nbehaviour drift from version upgrades."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main reason enterprises route model calls through Bedrock, Azure OpenAI or Vertex AI rather than the model vendor's public API?","options":["The cloud platforms serve the models with lower latency","The model inherits the cloud account's identity, private networking, logging, billing and compliance perimeter","The cloud platforms always offer newer model versions","The cloud platforms do not enforce rate limits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The technical model is the same; what changes is that security, procurement and compliance are already solved for the cloud provider. Model versions usually lag, and quotas still apply."},{"q":"Why do all three platforms sell a reserved or provisioned capacity mode alongside pay-as-you-go?","options":["Reserved capacity uses a different, faster model build","Pay-as-you-go cannot be used from a private endpoint","The shared regional pool is governed by quotas and cannot guarantee throughput at peak, so predictability has to be bought as dedicated capacity","Reserved capacity is required for logging and audit features"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shared pools give best-effort throughput under per-region quotas; a workload with a latency SLO at peak needs capacity that is reserved for it, which is what PTU, provisioned throughput and model units provide."},{"q":"A workload with a strict single-country data-residency rule is deployed with a cross-region or global routing option enabled. What is the consequence?","options":["Requests fail immediately with a residency error","Latency improves and nothing else changes","Requests may be processed in another region, violating the rule without any runtime error","The quota is halved because two regions share it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cross-region and global modes are designed to route to wherever capacity exists; they do not know your legal constraints. The violation surfaces in an audit, not in an error, so it must be prevented by configuration."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/api-provider-quotas-and-regional-limits","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI rate limits","Official docs: Anthropic rate limits","Official docs: Azure OpenAI quotas and limits","Official docs: Amazon Bedrock quotas"],"why":"A provider's GPU fleet is finite and shared by everyone. Without limits, one customer's traffic\nspike would degrade every other customer, and the provider could not size its fleet. Rate limits\nare the provider's admission control; quotas are the allocation of that control per customer. They\nare set per model because models have different cost and capacity, per region because the fleet is\nregional, and per account or subscription because that is the billing and abuse boundary. Tiers\nthat rise with spend exist because payment history is the provider's best signal that a customer is\nreal and predictable.\n\nFrom the application side, the naive model is \"the API is infinite, just call it; retry on error\".\nIt fails in three ways. First, a 429 under sustained demand is not transient: retries add load to a\nlimiter that is already rejecting you, so the retry storm raises latency for every request without\nincreasing throughput. Second, the limit is measured in tokens, not requests, and several major\nproviders (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Bedrock among them) document that they count `max_tokens`\nagainst the token budget at admission, so a request with `max_tokens=4096` that produces 50 tokens\nstill holds 4,096 tokens of budget when it is admitted (some providers credit the unused part back\nwhen the response completes, others do not); a team that never set `max_tokens` deliberately can\nfind its effective capacity is a fraction of what the quota implies. The exact rule differs per\nprovider and has changed over time, so read the current docs for the one you use.\nThird, quotas are regional, and the model the product depends on may not be available, or may have\na lower quota, in the only region the data-residency rule allows. A launch has been delayed by a\nquota ticket more often than by a model's quality. Treating quota as a planned resource like GPU\nmemory, with measurement, headroom and a growth process, is what this topic is about.","how":"### The units\n- **RPM** (requests per minute): the simplest cap, binding for workloads with many small requests.\n- **TPM** (tokens per minute): the cap that usually binds for LLM traffic. Some providers count\n  input and output together; others keep separate input-TPM and output-TPM budgets, which matters\n  because long-prompt RAG traffic and long-output generation traffic exhaust different budgets.\n- **TPD / RPD** (per day): present at lower tiers on some providers, a budget rather than a rate.\n- **Concurrency**: some platforms cap simultaneous in-flight requests rather than, or in addition\n  to, rates.\n- **Per-model, per-region, per-account.** Azure OpenAI allocates TPM per model per region per\n  subscription and you split it across deployments; Bedrock's on-demand quotas are per model per\n  region per account and are raised through the service-quotas process; vendor APIs set limits per\n  organisation per model by usage tier; Vertex has moved toward dynamically shared quota for some\n  models, where the fixed per-project cap gives way to a pool shared across customers.\n\n### How counting works\nProviders implement limits as token buckets or sliding windows: the budget refills continuously, a\nrequest is admitted if the bucket holds enough, otherwise it is rejected with 429 and usually a\n`Retry-After` (or `retry-after-ms`) header saying when to try again. On providers that reserve output,\nthe token cost is estimated at admission from the prompt length plus `max_tokens` (or a default\nwhen absent), *because* the provider must reserve capacity before it knows the real output length;\nsome then adjust the accounting when the response completes, and others count only actual output\nagainst an output-token limit. Response headers report the remaining budget,\nfor example OpenAI's `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens` family and Anthropic's\n`anthropic-ratelimit-tokens-remaining` family, which a gateway can read to slow down before the\nfirst 429 rather than after it.\n\n### The ceiling a quota actually gives you\nEvery limit converts into a request ceiling, and the lowest one binds:\n\n```\nmax RPM from TPM  = TPM quota ÷ tokens charged per request\ntokens charged    = prompt tokens + max_tokens     (on providers that reserve output)\nbinding ceiling   = min(RPM quota, max RPM from TPM, concurrency cap ÷ mean seconds per request × 60)\n```\n\nWith a 400k TPM quota and 1,000-token prompts, `max_tokens=4000` charges 5,000 tokens a request,\nso the ceiling is 80 requests per minute even if answers average 300 tokens. Setting\n`max_tokens=800` charges 1,800 and lifts the ceiling to about 220 per minute, with no change to the\nquota or the bill. The concurrency term is Little's Law: at a cap of 50 in-flight requests and 15\nseconds per long generation, you cannot exceed 200 requests per minute whatever the token quota\nsays. Running this calculation per model per region is the core of quota planning.\n\n### Quota versus capacity\nA quota is a promise about *your* admission, not about the pool's availability. When the regional\npool is saturated, requests fail with overload or capacity errors (Anthropic's 529, Bedrock's\nthrottling exceptions, Azure 429s on a deployment that is under its TPM) even though you are\nunder quota. Provisioned or reserved capacity exists for this gap: it buys dedicated throughput that\ndoes not compete with the pool, which is why it is priced by the hour rather than by the token.\n\n### Regional limits\nQuotas are per region, which cuts both ways. If residency rules allow, a workload can spread across\nseveral regions or several Azure deployments and multiply its effective quota. If residency names\none region, the quota in that region is the ceiling and the plan must be built around it, including\nlead time for increase requests. Model availability is also regional: the newest model may reach\nsome regions months after others. Cross-region inference profiles, global and data-zone deployment\ntypes and global endpoints are the providers' tools for borrowing capacity from elsewhere; using\nthem is a residency decision as much as a capacity one.\n\n### The client-side design\n1. **Measure demand in the provider's units.** Peak tokens per minute in and out, peak requests\n   per minute, per model, per region, from your own telemetry, with `max_tokens` included in the\n   input-side estimate when the provider reserves it.\n2. **Limit yourself below the quota.** A gateway-level token bucket at roughly 80–90% of each\n   quota smooths bursts, so the provider never sees a storm and the first rejection happens in your\n   queue, where you control priority.\n3. **Queue with priority and bound the wait.** Interactive traffic ahead of batch; shed or defer\n   when the queue exceeds the latency budget rather than letting everything time out.\n4. **Retry correctly.** Honour `Retry-After`, add jitter, cap attempts, and open a circuit breaker\n   when the failure rate shows the pool is overloaded, since retries cannot create capacity.\n5. **Spread and fall back.** Weighted routing across regions, deployments, accounts or providers\n   where allowed, with the fallback order chosen by residency and quality constraints. Spreading\n   across accounts is legitimate when they map to real organisational boundaries; creating accounts\n   purely to multiply a limit can breach a provider's terms, so check them and prefer a quota\n   increase or reserved capacity.\n6. **Buy reserved capacity for the base load** and use pay-as-you-go for burst, with batch APIs for\n   offline work because they have separate limits and typically a lower price.\n7. **Operate the quota.** Alert on remaining-budget headers and 429 rates; file increases before\n   growth, not during the incident; keep a table of quota per model per region in the runbook.","example":"A gateway-side limiter that budgets tokens the way the provider does (reserving `max_tokens`),\nroutes across a pool of deployments, honours `Retry-After`, and refuses to retry into an overloaded\npool. Deployment names and quotas are placeholders.\n\n```python\nimport time, random, threading\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    def __init__(self, per_minute: int, share: float = 0.85):\n        self.cap = per_minute * share; self.tokens = self.cap; self.t = time.time(); self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    def take(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        with self.lock:\n            now = time.time(); self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + (now - self.t) * self.cap / 60); self.t = now\n            if self.tokens >= n: self.tokens -= n; return True\n            return False\n    def give(self, n: int):                      # refund a reservation that was never sent\n        with self.lock: self.tokens = min(self.cap, self.tokens + n)\n\nclass Deployment:\n    def __init__(self, name, tpm, rpm):\n        self.name, self.tpm, self.rpm, self.open_until = name, TokenBucket(tpm), TokenBucket(rpm), 0.0\n\npool = [Deployment(\"eu-west-a\", tpm=300_000, rpm=1_800), Deployment(\"eu-west-b\", tpm=150_000, rpm=900)]\n\ndef pick(need: int):\n    for d in random.sample(pool, len(pool)):   # random order spreads load; budget only the one used\n        if d.open_until > time.time() or not d.rpm.take(1): continue\n        if d.tpm.take(need): return d\n        d.rpm.give(1)                          # token budget short: return the request slot\n    return None\n\ndef call(send, prompt_tokens, max_tokens, attempts=4):\n    need = prompt_tokens + max_tokens          # count max_tokens, as the provider does at admission\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        d = pick(need)\n        if d is None:\n            time.sleep(min(2 ** attempt, 8) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)); continue   # wait in our queue\n        status, headers, body = send(d.name)\n        if status == 200: return body\n        if status == 429:                      # our estimate drifted or the pool is busy: back off\n            time.sleep(float(headers.get(\"retry-after\", 2 ** attempt)) * random.uniform(1.0, 1.5))\n        elif status in (503, 529):             # overloaded: trip this deployment for a while\n            d.open_until = time.time() + 30\n        else:\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"{d.name}: {status}\")\n    raise RuntimeError(\"quota exhausted: shed or defer this request\")\n```\n\nThe limiter admits only when both the request budget and the token budget of the chosen deployment\nhave room, which is the condition the provider will check, and it debits only the deployment the\nrequest is actually sent to (debiting every candidate would silently burn the others' budgets).\nRunning it at 85% of quota keeps a margin for estimation drift. A production version would also\nrefund the unused part of `max_tokens` when the response reports its real usage, if the provider\ndoes the same.","tradeoffs":["Self-limiting below quota prevents 429 storms and lets you prioritise, but it leaves a slice of paid-for quota unused; the margin should shrink as your token estimates get better.","Spreading across regions or deployments multiplies quota and availability, but it weakens the residency guarantee and splits prefix-cache and provisioned-capacity benefits across endpoints.","Reserved capacity removes 429 and overload risk for the base load, but it is billed by the hour whether used or not; it pays when steady load fills most of it or an SLO forbids shed traffic.","Setting `max_tokens` tightly frees quota and lowers reservation waste, but a value below what real answers need truncates output; tune it per endpoint from observed output-length percentiles.","Batch APIs give separate limits and a lower price, but hours of turnaround; they flip from useful to useless when a workload is interactive or has a deadline inside the batch window."],"pitfalls":["Retrying 429s immediately and without a cap; the symptom is latency rising across every endpoint while throughput stays flat, because retries are consuming the budget that fresh requests need.","Leaving `max_tokens` at a large default; the symptom is hitting TPM at a fraction of the token volume the quota suggests, visible as 429s with low measured output tokens.","Planning capacity in one region without checking model availability and quota there; the symptom is a launch waiting on a quota-increase ticket.","Treating a quota as a capacity guarantee; the symptom is overload errors during a provider-wide peak while your dashboards show you well under quota.","Ignoring remaining-budget headers; the symptom is learning about exhaustion from the first 429 instead of throttling smoothly before it.","Enabling cross-region or global routing to fix a quota problem on a residency-bound workload; the symptom is an audit finding rather than a runtime error."],"when":"This is relevant to every workload on a hosted model API. Plan quotas when you choose the model and\nregion, not at launch: convert peak demand into the provider's units, compare with the current\nallocation per model per region, and file increases with weeks of lead time. Put a self-limiter,\npriority queue and correct retry logic in the gateway from day one; add spreading across regions or\ndeployments when residency allows and demand exceeds one region's quota; buy reserved capacity when\nthere is a steady base load or an SLO at peak. Measure 429 and overload rates, remaining budget,\nqueue wait time, and actual versus reserved output tokens. In interviews, expect questions on why\nretries do not fix sustained 429s and why `max_tokens` affects throughput before it affects cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements token-aware limiting, priority queueing and `Retry-After` handling in the gateway, tunes `max_tokens` per endpoint and surfaces remaining-budget headers as metrics.","architect":"Decides the region, deployment and provider topology per workload, when to buy reserved capacity, and how residency constraints bound the spreading options.","fde":"Audits a customer's quotas per model per region before go-live, drives the increase requests early, and explains overload errors that occur while they are under quota."},"check":{"q":"A team on a hosted API has a quota of 400k TPM for their model in the one region their\nresidency rule allows. Their load test shows 429s starting at about 120k measured tokens per\nminute. Explain what is probably happening and how you would raise effective capacity without\nbreaking the residency rule.","a":"The provider reserves `max_tokens` at admission; if requests set `max_tokens` at 4,000 while\nreal answers average a few hundred tokens, each request reserves far more than it uses, so the\nprovider's limiter counts the full 400k as consumed while only about 120k tokens are actually\nprocessed (with prompts around 1,300 tokens, each request charges about 5,300 but uses about\n1,600, a ratio near 0.3). First, set `max_tokens` from\nobserved output-length percentiles per endpoint, which can roughly triple effective capacity on\nits own. Second, add a gateway token bucket at 85% of quota with a priority queue so interactive\ntraffic is admitted first and batch work is deferred rather than retried. Third, move offline work\nto the batch API, which has separate limits. Fourth, file a quota increase with usage evidence,\nand if a peak SLO exists, buy provisioned capacity in the same region for the base load. Spreading\nto other regions is off the table because of residency, so the plan is efficiency, prioritisation\nand reserved capacity in-region."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does retrying immediately after a 429 fail to improve throughput under sustained demand?","options":["429 responses are cached by the provider for one minute","The limiter is already rejecting at the quota, so retries consume budget that new requests need and only add latency","Retries are billed at double the token price","The provider blocks the account after repeated 429s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 429 under sustained load means the budget is exhausted; another request does not add capacity, it competes for the same refill. Back off with jitter, honour Retry-After, and prioritise in your own queue."},{"q":"Why does an overly large `max_tokens` reduce effective throughput on a tokens-per-minute quota?","options":["Larger outputs always take longer to generate","The provider charges for `max_tokens` whether used or not","Providers that reserve output count `max_tokens` against the budget at admission, so unused reservation still counts for that minute","Requests with large `max_tokens` are routed to a slower pool"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Billing is on actual tokens, but a limiter that reserves before it knows the output length uses `max_tokens` (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Bedrock document this; check your provider). Setting it from real output-length percentiles recovers the wasted budget."},{"q":"A residency-bound workload exceeds the quota available in its region. Which option is valid?","options":["Enable the provider's global routing so excess traffic is served elsewhere","Spread traffic across accounts in several regions","Improve efficiency, prioritise traffic, use batch APIs for offline work, and request reserved capacity or a quota increase in that region","Disable rate limiting in the client so requests are not delayed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Global or multi-region routing violates residency silently. The in-region toolkit is tighter token reservation, prioritisation, separate batch limits, and reserved capacity or increases with lead time."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/hybrid-on-prem","sources":["Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: Kubernetes"],"why":"Three forces push inference on-prem. Regulation and contracts: health, financial, defence and\npublic-sector data often may not be processed outside a named facility, jurisdiction or the\ncustomer's own account, and \"the provider promises not to train on it\" does not satisfy that rule.\nPhysics and connectivity: a factory, ship, hospital or retail site may need inference near the\nmachines generating the data, or have links that are slow, metered or intermittent. Economics: a\nworkload that runs flat-out all day on open-weight models can cost less on amortised owned GPUs\nthan on per-token prices, once utilization is high enough.\n\nTwo forces push the other way. The strongest models are available only as hosted APIs, and most\nproducts want them for at least some tasks. Elasticity: owned GPUs are a fixed quantity bought on a\nprocurement cycle measured in months, so a traffic spike or a new use case cannot be met by\n\"buying more\" this week. On-prem also means owning the operations: drivers, firmware, CUDA\nstacks, hardware failures, spares, power and cooling, security patching of the serving stack.\n\nThe naive answers both fail. \"Everything on-prem\" leaves teams on last year's open-weight model,\nunable to serve a launch spike, and paying a platform team for work the cloud would have done.\n\"Everything in the cloud\" fails the first compliance review of the regulated workload and the\nfirst site with a bad link. Hybrid is the admission that different requests have different\nconstraints and the infrastructure should route accordingly, which turns the question into a\npolicy problem: which request goes where, enforced by a gateway, with the application unaware.","how":"### What \"on-prem\" covers\nThe term spans a spectrum: your own datacenter; colocation space with your hardware; a private\ncloud; a customer's cloud account or VPC (cloud hardware, customer control); cloud-provider\non-premises extensions that place the provider's managed stack in your facility (offerings such as\nAWS Outposts, Azure's on-premises stack and Google Distributed Cloud are examples of this\ncategory); and fully air-gapped sites with no external connectivity at all. The control, cost and\noperational burden differ at each point, so \"on-prem\" in a requirement must be pinned down before\nanything is designed. The deployment module covers the customer-VPC and air-gapped cases in depth\nfrom the forward-deployed perspective; this lesson covers the architecture that spans both sides.\n\n### The routing policy\nEvery request carries attributes the gateway can read: the data classification of its inputs,\nthe latency budget, the model capability required, the tenant and the site. The policy maps those\nto a target:\n\n- **Restricted data** → on-prem pool only, never bursts, no fallback to cloud. The rule is\n  enforced at the gateway *because* enforcing it in each application means every application is\n  a compliance risk.\n- **Internal data, frontier capability needed** → cloud platform in the approved region, through\n  private connectivity.\n- **Public or synthetic data, bulk volume** → on-prem when capacity is free (it is already paid\n  for), cloud burst when the on-prem queue exceeds a wait threshold.\n- **Site-local, low latency or offline** → the site's own small pool, with a degraded mode when\n  the central systems are unreachable.\n\nClassification comes from the data source (document labels, database column tags, tenant\ncontract), not from inspecting the prompt at request time, since inspection is a detector with a\nmiss rate and the rule must hold at the boundary regardless.\n\n### A uniform API surface\nApplication code should not know which side served it. On-prem engines (vLLM, TGI and others)\nexpose OpenAI-compatible endpoints, and hosted platforms are reached through their SDKs; the\ngateway presents one interface and holds the policy, the credentials for each side, the quotas and\nthe telemetry. This is what makes moving a workload from one side to the other a configuration\nchange. The exception is behaviour: an open-weight model on-prem and a frontier model in the cloud\nanswer differently, so prompts and evals are per route, and a workload that may be served by either\nside must pass its eval on both.\n\n### Bursting and backflow\nCloud burst works like an overflow queue: when the on-prem pool's queue wait exceeds a threshold,\neligible requests (eligibility is the classification again) go to the cloud. For workloads allowed\non both sides, on-prem first with cloud overflow is the right default, because the owned cost is\nalready sunk while every cloud token is a marginal cost. Backflow is the reverse movement: deferrable\neligible work (batch jobs, re-indexing, evaluation runs) is pulled back onto owned GPUs whenever they\nwould otherwise idle, for example overnight, so the fixed pool stays busy instead of the cloud bill\ngrowing.\n\n### Operating the owned side\nA fixed pool is only cheaper when busy, so on-prem capacity should not be sized for the peak of all\ntraffic. The sizing has two parts: restricted work has nowhere else to go, so its *peak* (plus a\nspare for a node failure) must fit on-prem; burst-eligible work first fills the gaps the restricted\nload leaves (nights, weekends, the spare's idle hours), and its peaks go to the cloud. GPUs beyond\nthe restricted requirement are justified only by eligible load that is present nearly all the\ntime, *because* only load that is always there keeps an extra GPU above break-even utilization;\neligible load that comes and goes is cheaper rented. The owned side needs a hardware lifecycle\n(procurement lead time, spares, replacement cadence, typically a three-to-five-year amortisation\nfor the TCO model), a serving-stack lifecycle (engine upgrades, CVE patching, driver and CUDA\ncompatibility), monitoring unified with the cloud side, and a model lifecycle (which open-weight\nmodels, which quantization, evaluated against which cloud baseline). Connectivity between sides is\nprivate (VPN, dedicated links or private endpoints) and secrets for each side are held only where\nthey are used, so that a compromised on-prem node does not hold cloud credentials and vice versa.\n\n### Economics in one line\nOwned cost per token = (amortised hardware + power + space + people) ÷ tokens actually served, so\nit falls with utilization; hosted cost per token is roughly flat. The crossover utilization is the\nbreak-even, covered in its own lesson; the hybrid pattern's job is to keep the owned side above it\nby giving it all the eligible work.","example":"A gateway routing policy that enforces classification, routes for capability and bursts only\neligible traffic. Thresholds and model names are placeholders.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass DataClass(Enum):\n    RESTRICTED = \"restricted\"   # may never leave the owned environment\n    INTERNAL = \"internal\"       # cloud allowed in the approved region, via private link\n    PUBLIC = \"public\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Request:\n    data_class: DataClass\n    needs_frontier: bool        # task requires a capability only the hosted model has\n    latency_budget_ms: int\n    site: str | None = None     # set for site-local traffic\n\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS = {\"central\": 0, \"plant-7\": 0}   # refreshed from engine metrics\nBURST_THRESHOLD_MS = 800\n\ndef route(r: Request) -> str:\n    if r.data_class is DataClass.RESTRICTED:\n        if r.needs_frontier:\n            raise PermissionError(\"restricted data cannot use the hosted frontier model\")\n        return \"onprem:central\"                          # no burst, no fallback, by policy\n    if r.site and r.latency_budget_ms < 300:\n        return f\"onprem:{r.site}\"                        # site-local pool, degraded mode if central is down\n    if r.needs_frontier:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # private endpoint, approved region\n    if ONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] > BURST_THRESHOLD_MS and r.latency_budget_ms < 5_000:\n        return \"cloud:eu-region\"                         # interactive eligible burst only;\n                                                         # deferrable work waits for owned GPUs\n    return \"onprem:central\"                              # owned capacity is sunk cost: keep it busy\n\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.RESTRICTED, False, 2000)))        # onprem:central\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.INTERNAL, True, 2000)))           # cloud:eu-region\nONPREM_QUEUE_WAIT_MS[\"central\"] = 1500\nprint(route(Request(DataClass.PUBLIC, False, 2000)))            # cloud:eu-region (burst)\n```\n\nThe policy refuses rather than degrades when restricted data needs a capability only the cloud\nhas; that refusal is the signal to procure a better on-prem model, not a bug to route around. The\norder of the checks is the policy: classification first, so no later rule (latency, capability,\nburst) can move restricted data.\n\nSizing the owned pool, with illustrative numbers in replicas of one model: restricted traffic\npeaks at 3 replicas on weekday afternoons and falls to 1 overnight; eligible interactive traffic\nruns at 2 replicas during the day and peaks at 6; an overnight batch could use 4 replicas for 8\nhours. Own 3 + 1 spare = 4 for the restricted peak. During the day, the spare and any restricted\nslack take eligible interactive work and the rest of its peak bursts to the cloud. Overnight,\nrestricted needs 1 and the batch takes the other 3, so the pool stays busy around the clock.\nBuying 2 more replicas for the daytime eligible base would make sense only if those 2 also stayed\nbusy overnight and at weekends; if not, they sit idle for over half the week and the cloud is\ncheaper for that load.","tradeoffs":["Owned GPUs lower cost per token at high utilization, but they are a fixed quantity with months of lead time; the trade flips against ownership when load is spiky or still growing fast.","On-prem satisfies residency and air-gap rules, but confines those workloads to open-weight models that typically trail the frontier; when the task needs frontier quality, the choice is a better on-prem model or a policy change, not a routing trick.","Bursting to the cloud absorbs peaks without over-buying hardware, but every burst path is a data path that must be gated by classification, and prompts must be evaluated on both models.","A uniform gateway interface makes migration a configuration change, but it hides behavioural differences between routes, which is why evals and prompts must stay per route.","Cloud-provider on-premises extensions reduce the operations burden of owned hardware, but keep the provider's pricing and lock-in inside your building."],"pitfalls":["Sizing on-prem for the peak of all traffic, including work that could burst to the cloud; the symptom is a GPU fleet averaging well under a quarter utilization while the finance model assumed near-full.","Classification by prompt inspection instead of by source; the symptom is a restricted document found in cloud logs because a detector missed it.","One prompt tuned on the cloud model and reused on the on-prem model; the symptom is a quality regression that only appears during bursts or on-prem fallback.","No spare hardware or replacement plan; the symptom is weeks of reduced capacity after a single GPU or node failure.","Cloud credentials stored on on-prem nodes for \"convenience\"; the symptom is a security review finding that an on-prem compromise exposes the cloud account.","Treating a customer VPC deployment as \"on-prem\" in the TCO model; the symptom is a cost estimate that omits the customer's cloud bill and the provider's egress and support terms."],"when":"Reach for hybrid when a named class of data or sites cannot use hosted APIs *and* other workloads\nneed frontier models or elastic capacity. Stay fully hosted when no residency or connectivity\nconstraint exists and volume is below the break-even. Go fully on-prem only when policy forbids\nevery hosted path and open-weight models meet the quality bar. Measure owned-pool utilization,\nburst volume and its cost, per-route quality on shared evals, and the number of policy refusals,\nwhich tells you where the on-prem model is falling short. In day-to-day work this shows up as the\ngateway's policy table and the quarterly review of what each side costs; in interviews, expect to\ndefend why classification is enforced at the gateway and why on-prem is sized for the restricted\npeak plus only the always-present base of eligible work rather than for total peak.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the gateway policy and burst logic, runs OpenAI-compatible engines on the owned side, and maintains per-route prompts and evals so either side passes the same test suite.","architect":"Defines the data classes and their permitted targets, sizes the owned pool to the restricted peak plus the always-present eligible base, owns the TCO and break-even model, and signs off on the connectivity and secrets design.","fde":"Deploys the on-prem or customer-VPC side inside the customer's environment, maps their data classifications to routes, and sets up burst eligibility and monitoring that the customer's security team accepts."},"check":{"q":"A hospital group wants an assistant over patient records (must stay on-site), a general\nstaff assistant, and overnight batch summarisation of public research papers. They own eight\nGPUs. Design the hybrid layout and say what you would measure to know it is working.","a":"Patient-record traffic is restricted: it runs on the owned pool with an open-weight model,\nno burst, no fallback, enforced by the gateway from the record system's classification, and the\nmodel is evaluated on clinical tasks before launch. The staff assistant needs frontier quality and\ntouches internal but not restricted data, so it goes to a cloud platform in an approved region\nover a private endpoint. The research batch is public data: it runs on the owned pool overnight\nwhen the restricted workload is quiet, bursting to the cloud batch API if it cannot finish by\nmorning. Size the owned pool for the restricted workload's peak (it cannot burst) with one GPU\nor node of spare, and let the overnight batch fill the idle hours, not for the combined peak of all\nthree workloads.\nMeasure owned-pool utilization (target high, since cost per token depends on it), queue wait and\nburst volume, per-route eval scores, and policy refusals, which show where the on-prem model is\ninsufficient."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should data classification for routing be derived from the data source rather than from inspecting the prompt?","options":["Prompt inspection is too slow for interactive latency budgets","A detector has a miss rate, while source-level labels make the restriction hold at the boundary regardless of prompt content","Prompts are encrypted before they reach the gateway","Source labels are required by the serving engine to allocate KV cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are absolute; a classifier that misses one restricted document has already violated them. Labels attached to the record system, tenant or document travel with the request and are enforced deterministically."},{"q":"Which sizing rule keeps owned GPUs economical in a hybrid design?","options":["Size on-prem for peak so the cloud is never needed","Fit the restricted workload's peak, add capacity only for eligible load present nearly all the time, and send eligible peaks to the cloud","Size on-prem for the restricted workload's average load and send everything else to the cloud","Size on-prem to match the cloud quota in the approved region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Owned cost per token falls with utilization, so the pool must be busy, but restricted work cannot burst, so its peak sets a floor. Filling the remaining capacity with the base of eligible work keeps the pool full, while elastic cloud capacity absorbs eligible peaks that would otherwise sit idle on owned hardware; sizing restricted work at its average would drop requests at its peak."},{"q":"A restricted-data workload needs a capability only the hosted frontier model has. What should the gateway do?","options":["Route to the cloud once with logging so the exception is auditable","Strip identifying fields from the prompt and route to the cloud","Refuse the request and surface it as a signal to improve the on-prem model or change policy","Route to the cloud only during off-peak hours when scrutiny is lower"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The restriction is on the data, not on the time or on identifiers that a detector might miss. The correct response is a policy refusal, which becomes evidence for procuring a stronger on-prem model or formally revisiting the rule."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/edge-ai-deployment","sources":["Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Official docs: llama.cpp","Official docs: ONNX Runtime"],"why":"A cloud call costs a network round trip, needs connectivity, uploads the user's data and bills per\ntoken. For many workloads none of that matters. For some it is disqualifying: a keyboard\nsuggestion or voice assistant that waits 400 ms for a server feels broken; a field technician in a\nbasement has no signal; a camera stream from a factory line cannot be shipped to a datacenter for\nevery frame; a health app's users will not accept their data leaving the phone; and a product with\na hundred million installs cannot pay per-token prices for every interaction. Each of those is a\nreason to run the model where the data is.\n\nThe naive answer, \"ship the model we run in the cloud\", fails on arithmetic. A 70B-parameter model\nin fp16 is roughly 140 GB of weights; a phone gives an app a few gigabytes at most, and even a\nlaptop with 16 GB of unified memory comfortably holds only a 4-bit model of about 7–8B parameters\n(a 13–14B model is a squeeze) once the OS, other apps and the KV cache are counted. Decode speed is bound by memory bandwidth, which on a phone is a small fraction of a\ndatacenter GPU's, so the same model that streams at 100 tokens per second in the cloud would crawl\non device even if it fit. Thermal limits add a third constraint: sustained inference throttles the\nchip and drains the battery. The opposite naive answer, \"quantize everything to 4-bit and hope\",\nfails on quality: compression without evaluation produces models that pass a demo and fail in the\nfield. Edge deployment is therefore a budgeting and engineering discipline, not a flag on the cloud\nmodel.","how":"### Which edge\n\"Edge\" covers two different deployments. **On-device** means the user's phone, laptop, browser or\nan embedded controller: you do not own the hardware, it is shared with other apps, and the fleet is\nheterogeneous. **On-site edge servers** are a small box or rack you do own at a store, plant,\nhospital or cell site (a Jetson-class module, a workstation GPU, a telco edge node): fewer devices,\nknown hardware, a few users each, and the same latency and connectivity benefits. The second is\ncloser to a tiny on-prem cluster and is planned like one; the rest of this lesson is mostly about\nthe first, where the constraints are hardest.\n\n### Start from the device budget\nThree numbers bound the design. **Memory**: weights plus KV cache plus activations must fit in what\nthe platform grants an app, with margin for the app itself. Weight bytes ≈ parameters × bits per\nweight ÷ 8, so a 3B model at 4-bit is about 1.5 GB and an 8B model about 4 GB, before the cache.\n**Bandwidth**: each decode step reads all the weights once, so tokens per second is bounded by\nmemory bandwidth ÷ model bytes; as an illustrative order of magnitude, a device with tens of GB/s\nof memory bandwidth and a 2 GB model cannot exceed a few tens of tokens per second, whatever the\ncompute. **Power and thermal**: sustained throughput is lower than burst throughput because the\nchip throttles, so plan for sustained figures measured on warm devices, and battery cost per\ninteraction is a product metric on phones and laptops.\n\nThe prefill/decode split from the datacenter still applies, with a twist. Decode is bandwidth-bound\nas above, but prefill is compute-bound, and on a phone compute is scarce too, so time to first\ntoken grows with prompt length: TTFT ≈ prompt tokens ÷ prefill tokens per second. A 2,000-token\nprompt (system prompt plus a retrieved document) on a device that prefills a few hundred tokens a\nsecond waits several seconds before the first word appears, which is why on-device features keep\nprompts short, reuse a cached system-prompt prefix across turns, and use the NPU or GPU for prefill\nwhere the runtime supports it. Plan both numbers: TTFT from prompt length, streaming speed from\nbandwidth.\n\n### Make the model fit\n- **Choose a small model first.** Down to about 4 bits per weight, a larger quantized model often\n  beats a smaller one at the same byte budget, so 4-bit is a common sweet spot. Below roughly 3 bits\n  quality usually falls off sharply, so a large model that only fits at 2-bit is typically beaten by\n  a small language model (hundreds of millions to a few billion parameters) at 4-bit, especially one\n  trained or distilled for the task. The crossover is model- and task-dependent; measure it.\n- **Quantize**, typically to 4-bit or 8-bit weights, with activations kept at higher precision.\n  Post-training quantization methods (GPTQ, AWQ and the k-quant families used by llama.cpp) cut\n  memory and raise bandwidth-bound speed roughly in proportion to the bit reduction. The quality\n  cost must be measured per model and task; it is small for many models at 8-bit and 4-bit but\n  can be severe for the smallest models.\n- **Distil** a large model's behaviour into a small one for the specific task, which is how most\n  production on-device models are made task-competent.\n- **Prune and adapt.** Structured pruning removes capacity the task does not need; LoRA adapters\n  let one base model serve several device features with small per-feature deltas.\n\n### Match the runtime to the silicon\nDevice accelerators are heterogeneous: CPU vector units, mobile GPUs, NPUs on phones and laptops,\nApple's unified-memory GPU and Neural Engine, NVIDIA Jetson-class embedded GPUs. Runtimes exist to\nmap one model to many of them: llama.cpp with its GGUF format (CPU and GPU back ends across\ndesktop and mobile), ONNX Runtime (execution providers per accelerator, including mobile), Core ML\n(Apple), LiteRT (formerly TensorFlow Lite) and ExecuTorch (mobile and embedded from the TensorFlow\nand PyTorch ecosystems), TensorRT (NVIDIA embedded and desktop GPUs), and WebGPU-based runtimes\nfor in-browser inference. The runtime decision is made *per device class* because the fastest\npath on each is different, and the test matrix must cover real devices, not a developer laptop.\n\n### Hybrid by default\nA small on-device model handles what it can; requests that need more capability, more context or\nmore knowledge escalate to the cloud. The escalation rule is explicit (confidence threshold, task\ntype, user setting) and is itself a privacy decision, since escalation uploads data. On-device\nfirst with cloud fallback is the shape most consumer platforms have converged on, and it preserves\nthe latency and offline benefits for the common case.\n\n### Lifecycle on a fleet you do not control\nModel files are distributed like app assets: downloaded on Wi-Fi, versioned, delta-updated, with\nintegrity checks. A device may have any of several model versions at once, so telemetry (opt-in,\naggregated) must carry the model version, and the server-side fallback must accept requests from\nold clients. Capability detection at install time decides which model variant, if any, a device\ngets; low-end devices get cloud-only mode. Rollouts are staged by device class because a model\nthat fits one phone can crash another.\n\n### Security on device\nAnything on the device belongs to the user, including the weights, the prompts and any guardrail\nlogic. Treat model weights as extractable, keep anything proprietary in the cloud model, and never\nrely on on-device prompts or filters for decisions that matter to the server; re-check on the\nserver side when the request escalates.","example":"Running a quantized GGUF model locally with llama.cpp through its Python binding, with a budget\ncheck first. The model file name is a placeholder; parameters and quantization are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport os, time\nfrom llama_cpp import Llama\n\ndef fits(model_path: str, n_ctx: int, kv_bytes_per_token: int, budget_bytes: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Weights on disk approximate weights in memory for GGUF; add the KV cache for n_ctx.\"\"\"\n    weights = os.path.getsize(model_path)\n    need = weights + n_ctx * kv_bytes_per_token\n    print(f\"weights {weights/1e9:.2f} GB + cache {n_ctx*kv_bytes_per_token/1e9:.2f} GB vs budget {budget_bytes/1e9:.1f} GB\")\n    return need <= budget_bytes\n\nMODEL = \"small-chat-3b.Q4_K_M.gguf\"          # ~3B params at 4-bit: roughly 1.5-2 GB on disk\nN_CTX = 2048\n# kv_bytes_per_token = 2 * n_layers * n_kv_heads * head_dim * bytes; read these from the model card\nassert fits(MODEL, N_CTX, kv_bytes_per_token=2 * 28 * 8 * 128 * 2, budget_bytes=4 * 1024**3)\n\nllm = Llama(model_path=MODEL, n_ctx=N_CTX, n_threads=os.cpu_count() or 4,\n            n_gpu_layers=-1, verbose=False)   # offload all layers to Metal/CUDA/Vulkan if present\n\nt = time.time()                      # timing below includes prompt processing, not just decode\nout = llm.create_chat_completion(\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Reply in one sentence.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise why the pump alarm fired.\"}],\n    max_tokens=64,\n)\ntext = out[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]\nn_out = out[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\nprint(text); print(f\"{n_out/(time.time()-t):.1f} tok/s on this device\")\n# Measure sustained tok/s after several minutes on a warm device, not this first burst.\n```\n\nThe `fits` check is the on-device version of the capacity question: weights plus cache against\nthe budget the platform grants. The printed tokens-per-second figure is the number that decides\nwhether streaming feels responsive, and it will drop as the device heats up.","tradeoffs":["On-device inference removes network latency, cloud cost and data upload, but caps model quality at what fits in the device's memory and bandwidth; the trade flips when the task needs frontier capability or broad knowledge.","Quantization to 4-bit roughly halves memory and raises speed versus 8-bit, but the quality loss grows as models get smaller; measure on the task before shipping.","A hybrid escalation path recovers quality for hard cases, but every escalation is a network dependency and a privacy event, so the rule must be visible to the user and the product.","Per-device runtimes extract the most from each accelerator, but multiply the build, test and update matrix; a single portable runtime is slower and simpler.","Running on the user's hardware makes your marginal compute cost near zero, but makes rollouts, debugging and telemetry slower and partial compared with a server you control; for a small or internal user base the cloud bill it saves may not repay that engineering."],"pitfalls":["Measuring only tokens per second and ignoring prompt length; the symptom is a feature that streams quickly once started but sits blank for seconds whenever a long document is in context.","Benchmarking on a developer laptop; the symptom is a model that is fast in the demo and crawls or crashes on the median user's device.","Measuring burst throughput; the symptom is responsiveness collapsing after a minute of use as the device throttles thermally.","Forgetting the KV cache in the memory budget; the symptom is out-of-memory kills on long conversations even though the weights \"fit\".","Shipping a quantized model without task evals; the symptom is a quality regression reported from the field that the cloud model never showed.","Relying on on-device guardrails or prompts for policy; the symptom is users bypassing them by editing the app or its model files.","No model-version field in telemetry and requests; the symptom is a bug that reproduces on some devices and cannot be attributed to the variant they run."],"when":"Reach for edge deployment when latency must be below a network round trip, when the device is\noften offline, when the data must not leave it, or when per-interaction cloud cost at your install\nbase is untenable, and when a small or distilled model can meet the quality bar on the task. Do not\nreach for it when the task needs frontier reasoning or large context, or when the device matrix is\ntoo fragmented to test. Measure sustained tokens per second on target devices, memory high-water\nmarks with the KV cache, task-eval scores per quantization level, escalation rate to the cloud, and\ncrash rates by device class. In interviews, expect to size a model to a device from parameter\ncount and bit width and to explain why decode speed follows memory bandwidth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Quantizes and evaluates candidate models, integrates the runtime for each target platform, implements the memory check and the escalation path, and builds the device test matrix.","architect":"Sets the on-device versus cloud split per feature, the privacy rule for escalation, the model distribution and versioning scheme, and which capabilities stay server-side for security.","fde":"Fits a customer's workload to their actual devices (plant controllers, handhelds, kiosks), proves sustained performance on site, and designs the offline and degraded modes they need."},"check":{"q":"A logistics company wants a package-scanning assistant on handheld devices with 6 GB of RAM,\nof which the app may use about 3 GB, working in warehouses with poor connectivity. Walk through\nhow you would choose and validate the model.","a":"Budget first: 3 GB must hold weights, the KV cache for the longest expected interaction and\nthe app. A 3B-class model at 4-bit is about 1.5–2 GB of weights (k-quant formats spend a little\nmore than 4 bits per weight), and a 2k-token cache for a model of that shape is a few hundred MB,\nleaving room for the app; an 8B model at 4-bit (about 4 GB) does not fit. Then quality: distil or fine-tune the\nsmall model on the scanning tasks and evaluate it against the cloud model on a task set, at the\nexact quantization that will ship. Then speed: measure sustained tokens per second on the real\nhandheld after thermal steady state, since decode is bound by its memory bandwidth, and check the\nhigh-water memory mark on long sessions. Design offline-first with queued escalation to the cloud\nwhen connectivity returns for cases below a confidence threshold. Stage the rollout by device\nclass, carry the model version in telemetry, and keep any policy enforcement on the server."},"quiz":[{"q":"What primarily bounds decode tokens per second for an LLM on a phone?","options":["The number of CPU cores available to the app","Memory bandwidth divided by the bytes of weights read per step","The size of the KV cache in the longest conversation","Network latency to the model's update server"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each generated token requires reading all the weights once; a phone's memory bandwidth is a small fraction of a datacenter GPU's, so the model's byte size divided into that bandwidth gives the ceiling. Quantization helps precisely because it shrinks the bytes read."},{"q":"Why is a purpose-built small model at 4-bit usually better on device than a large model squeezed to 2–3 bits to fit the same memory?","options":["Small models do not need a KV cache","Large models cannot be quantized below 8-bit","Quality degrades sharply below about 4 bits per weight, so the extra parameters do not make up for it, while a small model distilled for the task keeps its quality at the same byte budget","Small models run on the CPU, which is always faster than the NPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At a fixed memory budget the question is where quality per byte is highest. Around 4 bits a larger model often wins, but pushing it to 2–3 bits degrades it sharply, whereas a distilled small model at 4-bit keeps task performance. The crossover still has to be measured per task."},{"q":"Which safeguard belongs on the server even when the model runs entirely on device?","options":["The tokenizer, so that vocabulary stays consistent","The model's quantization tables","Policy enforcement for decisions that matter, since on-device prompts, filters and weights can be modified by the user","The KV cache, to save device memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Everything on the device is under the user's control and can be extracted or altered. Guardrails that protect the business or other users must be re-checked server-side when requests escalate or produce consequential actions."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/capacity-planning","sources":["Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"A classic web service has fairly uniform requests: if one pod handles 500 RPS at p99 under 100 ms, ten\npods handle about 5,000. Teams carry that habit into LLM serving and it breaks in three ways.\n\nFirst, cost per request is wildly uneven. A 200-token classification and a 30,000-token document\nsummary are both \"one request\", yet the second processes 150 times the input tokens (and more than\nthat in attention work, which grows faster than linearly with context) and holds GPU memory for far\nlonger. An RPS figure says nothing about which mix you will get.\n\nSecond, a request occupies a GPU for seconds, not milliseconds. Generation produces output tokens one at\na time, so a 500-token answer at 30 ms per token holds a batch slot and its KV cache for 15 seconds.\nWhat saturates a replica is the number of sequences *in flight at once*, and that is bounded by KV-cache\nmemory and by the per-token latency you promised, not by arrivals per second.\n\nThird, the units that limit you come from different places. A self-hosted engine runs out of KV memory\nor decode bandwidth; a managed API throttles you on requests per minute and tokens per minute; your\nwebsocket tier and session store care about open conversations. A plan written in only one unit\nmisses the constraint that actually binds. The naive fixes, averaging tokens/sec over a day or adding\nGPUs until the alerts stop, either under-provision the peak or pay for idle hardware without knowing\nwhich.","how":"### 1. Describe the workload as distributions, not averages\nCapacity starts from traffic, not hardware. Collect, per use case: peak concurrent active users, how\noften each sends a message, how many model calls one message triggers (an agent may make several),\ninput tokens per call (system prompt + history + retrieved context) and output tokens per call. Record\np50 and p95 for the token counts, and the peak-to-average ratio of traffic over a day and a week. You\nneed the tails because a long-context minority can consume most of the KV memory while being a small\nshare of requests.\n\n### 2. Convert between the three units\n- **Requests/sec (λ):** active users × messages per user per second × calls per message.\n- **Tokens/sec:** input TPS = λ × mean input tokens; output TPS = λ × mean output tokens. Keep them\n  separate *because* they hit different resources: input is prefill, compute-bound and parallel; output\n  is decode, bandwidth-bound and sequential per request. Planning on \"total tokens\" hides which one binds.\n- **Concurrent in-flight sequences (L):** by Little's Law, L = λ × W, where W is mean time in the system.\n  For one call, W ≈ queue wait + TTFT + output tokens × TPOT (time per output token). This is the number\n  that must fit into batch slots and KV memory.\n- **Concurrent sessions:** open conversations, most of them idle between turns. Sessions × duty cycle\n  (fraction of wall time a session has a call running) ≈ in-flight sequences. Sessions size the\n  connection tier, session store and prefix-cache retention; in-flight sequences size the GPUs.\n\nLittle's Law holds for any stable system in steady state, whatever the arrival or service\ndistribution, which is *why* it is the safe bridge between units. It does not tell you queueing delay;\nthat is the job of the queueing-theory lesson.\n\nOne subtlety makes LLM planning circular: W depends on load. TPOT rises as the batch grows, so more\nin-flight sequences make each call longer, which makes L larger still. Break the loop by using the\nTPOT and TTFT measured *at the concurrency you plan to run*, not at batch 1; if you plug in an\nunloaded TPOT, the plan undercounts in-flight sequences exactly when it matters. A useful identity\nfor checking the result: a replica running B sequences at TPOT t produces about B ÷ t output tokens\nper second, so in-flight sequences, TPOT and output tokens/sec must agree with each other.\n\n### 3. Measure capacity per replica at the SLO\nPer-replica capacity is not a spec-sheet number. Run a load test with a realistic token mix and sweep\nconcurrency. Throughput rises with batch size while per-request TPOT and TTFT degrade; the usable\ncapacity is the highest concurrency (and its tokens/sec) where p95 TTFT and p95 TPOT still meet the\nlatency budget, sometimes called goodput. Cross-check it against KV memory: max sequences ≈ free\nmemory after weights ÷ (KV bytes per token × mean context length). Whichever is lower, the memory bound\nor the latency knee, is the replica's capacity, and you should know which one it is because the fixes\ndiffer (more memory or GQA/quantized cache vs. more replicas).\n\n### 4. Size, then add headroom\nReplicas = max over the binding units: peak in-flight sequences ÷ (per-replica sequence capacity ×\ntarget utilization), and peak input tokens/sec ÷ per-replica prefill capacity at that load, rounded\nup. Usually one dominates, but a prefill-heavy mix (long RAG contexts, short answers) can be bound by\nprefill while sequence slots sit free. The\ntarget utilization sits below 100% *because* queueing delay grows sharply as utilization approaches\none, and the count is increased again for N+1 failure tolerance, rolling deploys and autoscaler lag (GPU nodes and model\nloading can take minutes). Finally, plan over a horizon at least as long as your procurement or\nquota lead time: if reserved GPUs take a quarter to arrive and traffic grows 10% a month, the plan\nyou act on today must cover the peak expected a quarter from now, not today's. For a managed API, the same numbers become requests-per-minute and\ntokens-per-minute quotas per region and per model; check whether your provider counts the requested\n`max_tokens` against the token quota at admission time, because some do, which makes generous\n`max_tokens` settings consume quota you never use.","example":"```python\nimport math\n\ndef plan(active_users, msgs_per_user_min, calls_per_msg, in_tok, out_tok,\n         ttft_s, tpot_s, replica_max_seqs, target_util=0.7, spare=1):\n    rps = active_users * msgs_per_user_min / 60 * calls_per_msg     # λ\n    in_tps, out_tps = rps * in_tok, rps * out_tok\n    w = ttft_s + out_tok * tpot_s                                   # seconds per call\n    in_flight = rps * w                                             # Little's Law L = λW\n    duty = in_flight / active_users                                 # share of sessions busy\n    replicas = math.ceil(in_flight / (replica_max_seqs * target_util)) + spare\n    return dict(rps=rps, in_tps=in_tps, out_tps=out_tps, w=w,\n                in_flight=in_flight, duty=duty, replicas=replicas)\n\n# Support agent at peak: 1,200 open sessions, one message per user per minute,\n# 3 model calls per message, 3,000 input / 250 output tokens per call.\n# Load test (illustrative): one replica holds 64 sequences at p95 TPOT <= 30 ms.\nprint(plan(1200, 1, 3, 3000, 250, ttft_s=0.5, tpot_s=0.03, replica_max_seqs=64))\n# {'rps': 60.0, 'in_tps': 180000.0, 'out_tps': 15000.0, 'w': 8.0,\n#  'in_flight': 480.0, 'duty': 0.4, 'replicas': 12}\n```\nReading the result: 60 calls/sec, each lasting about 8 s (0.5 s TTFT + 250 × 30 ms), means 480\nsequences in flight, so only 40% of the 1,200 sessions are busy at any instant. At 64 sequences per\nreplica, 7.5 replicas would run at 100%; at a 70% target that is 11, plus one spare gives 12.\nNow cross-check memory: for a Llama-3-8B-shaped model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, fp16) the\ncache is 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB per token, so 64 sequences × 3,250 tokens ≈ 27 GB, which\nfits beside ~16 GB of weights on an 80 GB GPU. Then check decode consistency: 480 sequences over 12\nreplicas is 40 per replica, and 40 ÷ 0.03 s ≈ 1,330 output tokens/sec per replica, about 16,000\nacross the fleet, slightly above the 15,000 demanded because part of each call's 8 s is TTFT rather\nthan decode; if the numbers disagreed badly, one of the inputs would be wrong. Then check prefill: 180,000 input tokens/sec across 12\nreplicas is 15,000 per replica, which the load test must confirm; if the 2,000-token system prompt is\nshared, prefix caching removes most of that work.","tradeoffs":["A higher target utilization cuts GPU cost, but queueing delay rises steeply near saturation; it flips toward low utilization when traffic is bursty or the TTFT budget is tight.","Planning to p95 token counts instead of means over-provisions on quiet days but protects the tail; means are acceptable only when context length is tightly capped.","Larger per-replica batches raise tokens/sec per GPU but lengthen TPOT for every user; interactive chat favours smaller batches, offline batch jobs favour the largest batch memory allows.","Static provisioning for peak is simple and fast to respond, but wastes money when peak-to-average is high; autoscaling saves cost only if scale-up time is shorter than the ramp of your traffic."],"pitfalls":["Planning in RPS only; the symptom is a capacity plan that looks fine until one tenant starts sending long documents and KV-cache preemptions spike while RPS is unchanged.","Using the engine's peak tokens/sec from a benchmark at huge batch; the symptom is that real interactive traffic misses its TPOT SLO at a fraction of that throughput.","Forgetting agent fan-out, counting user messages instead of model calls; the symptom is provider 429s on requests-per-minute at a user load the plan said was safe.","Sizing to the daily average; the symptom is queue depth and TTFT climbing every weekday morning while the dashboard shows modest mean utilization.","Load-testing with fixed-length synthetic prompts; the symptom is a replica that met its SLO in the test but misses it in production, where the long tail of contexts fills KV memory sooner.","Treating open sessions as concurrent load; the symptom is a GPU fleet several times larger than needed because idle conversations were counted as in-flight sequences."],"when":"Do this before any launch, before signing a reserved-capacity or provisioned-throughput commitment, and\nwhenever the traffic mix changes: a new feature with longer context, an agent that adds tool-calling\nsteps, a large tenant onboarding. Keep the model as a living spreadsheet or script fed by production\ntelemetry: arrivals, input and output token histograms, TTFT and TPOT percentiles, in-flight sequences,\nKV-cache utilization and provider throttle counts. If you consume only managed APIs, the plan is shorter\nbut still needed, because quotas are per region and per model and raising them can take days. In\ninterviews, expect \"how many GPUs for N users\" questions; the strong move is to ask for the token\ndistribution and SLO first, then reason through Little's Law and a measured per-replica capacity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments token counts, TTFT, TPOT and in-flight sequences per route, and runs the load test that finds the replica's knee at the latency SLO.","architect":"Owns the capacity model, headroom policy and the choice between reserved capacity, autoscaling and managed API quotas, including regional failover capacity.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"5,000 users\" into calls/sec and token mix from their real documents, and files quota increase requests in their cloud account before go-live, not after."},"check":{"q":"Traffic stays at 40 RPS, but after a release p95 TTFT doubles and the engine reports frequent\npreemptions. Nothing changed in hardware. What happened, and how do you confirm and fix it?","a":"RPS is unchanged, so look at the work per request. Likely the release raised input or output\ntokens per call: more retrieved chunks, longer history, or a higher `max_tokens`. Longer outputs raise W,\nso in-flight sequences L = λW rise even at constant λ; longer contexts raise KV bytes per sequence. Both\npush the replica past its memory bound, causing preemption and queueing. Confirm by comparing input and\noutput token histograms and in-flight sequence counts before and after. Fix by trimming context, capping\noutput, enabling prefix caching for shared prompts, or adding replicas sized from the new token mix."},"quiz":[{"q":"A service receives 20 calls/sec and each call takes 6 s end to end. How many sequences are in flight on average?","options":["20, because concurrency equals arrival rate","3.3, because 20 divided by 6","120, by Little's Law L = λW","It cannot be estimated without the arrival distribution"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Little's Law gives L = λ × W = 20 × 6 = 120 for any stable system in steady state, whatever the distribution. The distribution matters for queueing delay and tails, not for the mean in-flight count."},{"q":"Why should a capacity plan keep input and output tokens/sec separate instead of summing them?","options":["Providers bill them at the same rate, so splitting adds nothing","Input tokens drive compute-bound prefill while output tokens drive bandwidth-bound, sequential decode that holds GPU memory longer","Output tokens are always fewer, so they can be ignored","Input tokens are cached and therefore free after the first request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The two phases hit different resources. A summary workload is prefill-heavy, a long-generation workload is decode-heavy and occupies batch slots longer; summing them hides which constraint binds. Only shared prefixes, not all input, benefit from caching."},{"q":"A chat product has 10,000 open sessions, but each session has a model call running 5% of the time. What should size the GPU fleet?","options":["10,000 concurrent sequences, since every session might send at once","About 500 in-flight sequences at peak, plus headroom for bursts and failures","The number of sessions divided by the number of GPUs","Requests per second alone, since sessions are irrelevant to GPUs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPUs are occupied by in-flight sequences, which here are sessions × duty cycle ≈ 500. Open sessions still size connections and session storage, and the plan adds headroom because peaks exceed averages."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/queueing-theory","sources":["Kleinrock, 1975 — Queueing Systems, Volume 1: Theory","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Harchol-Balter, 2013 — Performance Modeling and Design of Computer Systems"],"why":"The naive capacity model is division: a replica handles 4 requests per second, peak traffic is 40, so\nbuy 10 replicas. That model assumes requests arrive evenly spaced and each takes exactly the average\ntime. Neither is true. Users arrive in bursts that no one coordinates, and LLM service times are\nwildly variable because output length varies: one request generates 20 tokens, the next generates\n2,000. When three long requests land together, everything behind them waits, and the server cannot\n\"bank\" the idle time it had a minute earlier to pay for the burst now.\n\nThe consequence is that latency is not flat until capacity and then a cliff; it bends upward long\nbefore 100% utilization. Teams that size to the mean see fine dashboards at 60% load and then\nmysterious p99 explosions at 85%, with no code change. Queueing theory exists to quantify that curve:\nhow much waiting a given utilization buys, how variability makes it worse, and how pooling, admission\ncontrol and headroom make it better. It is also the bridge between the throughput numbers the serving\nteam measures and the latency numbers the product team promises. Without it you cannot answer \"how\nmuch headroom do we need\" with anything better than a guess.","how":"### Vocabulary\n- **λ (lambda)**: arrival rate, requests per second.\n- **μ (mu)**: service rate of one server, so mean service time is 1/μ.\n- **c**: number of servers (for an LLM, think *concurrency slots*, not machines; see below).\n- **ρ = λ / (c·μ)**: utilization, the fraction of time servers are busy. Must stay below 1 or the\n  queue grows without bound.\n- **W, Wq, L**: mean time in system, mean time waiting in queue, mean number of requests in system.\n\n### Little's law: L = λ·W\nThe one law that holds for almost any stable system, regardless of arrival or service distribution:\nthe average number of requests in flight equals the arrival rate times the average time each spends\ninside. It holds *because* every request that enters eventually leaves, so long-run averages must\nbalance. It is the most useful formula in capacity work: at 50 req/s and 6 s mean latency, you have\n300 requests in flight on average, so you need at least 300 concurrent slots across the fleet before\nany headroom. It also runs backwards: if a gateway caps in-flight requests at L and latency is W, the\nbest throughput you can see is L/W.\n\n### Why waiting explodes: the 1/(1−ρ) term\nFor the simplest model, one server with random (Poisson) arrivals and exponential service times\n(M/M/1), mean time in system is W = 1/(μ − λ) and queue wait is Wq = ρ/(μ − λ). Rewrite it as\nWq = (service time) × ρ/(1 − ρ). At ρ = 0.5 you wait one service time; at 0.9, nine; at 0.99,\nninety-nine. The denominator is the reason headroom is not waste: the last 10% of utilization costs\nmore latency than the first 80%.\n\n### Variability: Kingman's approximation\nReal systems are not exponential. For a single server with general distributions (G/G/1), Kingman's\nformula approximates the wait as:\n\n```\nWq ≈ ( ρ / (1 − ρ) ) × ( (Ca² + Cs²) / 2 ) × mean_service_time\n```\n\nwhere Ca and Cs are the coefficients of variation (std dev / mean) of inter-arrival times and service\ntimes. This splits the problem into three levers: utilization, *arrival* variability (bursts, retry\nstorms, batch jobs released on the hour) and *service* variability (output-length spread, tool calls,\ncache hits vs misses). LLM workloads often have Cs around or above 1 (an exponential has exactly 1),\nand agentic traffic with tool calls and long-tail outputs can push it higher; when Cs > 1 their queues\nare worse than a textbook M/M/1 at the same utilization. Measure Cs from your own traffic rather than\nassuming it. Capping `max_tokens` or splitting long jobs onto a separate queue lowers Cs and so reduces\nlatency for everyone else.\n\n### Many servers: M/M/c and pooling\nWith c servers sharing one queue, the Erlang C formula gives the probability an arriving request must\nwait and the mean wait. Two results matter in practice. First, larger pools tolerate higher\nutilization at the same wait, *because* a burst is more likely to find some server free; this is\nstatistical multiplexing. Second, one shared queue beats several separate ones of the same total\ncapacity, because separate queues can have one pool idle while another is backed up. Every dedicated\nper-tenant or per-model pool pays this tax.\n\n### Mapping it onto LLM serving\nAn inference replica with continuous batching is not one server; it serves many sequences at once,\nand each sequence slows slightly as the batch grows. A workable approximation is to treat each replica\nas roughly `max_concurrent_sequences` servers whose service time is the measured latency *at that\nconcurrency*. The queue is wherever requests wait for a slot: the engine's waiting list, the gateway,\nor a provider's rate limiter. Measure service time and its variance from real traffic, not from a\nsingle-request benchmark, because per-request speed depends on load.\n\n### Tails, not means\nSLOs are written on p95 or p99, and the tail of queue wait grows faster than the mean. In M/M/c the\nwait has a simple shape: a request waits at all with probability P(wait) (Erlang C), and if it waits,\nthe extra delay is exponential with rate (c·μ − λ). So\n\n```\nP(Wq > t) = P(wait) × exp(−(c·μ − λ)·t)      →   t_p = ln(P(wait) / (1 − p)) / (c·μ − λ)\n```\n\nThe rate (c·μ − λ) is the *spare* service capacity; as it shrinks toward zero, every percentile\nstretches in proportion. In the 16-slot example below, p99 queue wait is about 3 s at 75% utilization\nand about 22 s at 95%, while the mean goes from 0.2 s to 3.9 s. Planning on the mean hides most of the\ndamage an SLO will see.\n\n### Open versus closed load, and queue order\nThe formulas above assume an *open* system: arrivals keep coming regardless of how slow you are, which\nis true of public traffic. A *closed* system has a fixed population that waits for each response before\nsending the next (a load test with N virtual users, or an agent that calls the model, then a tool, then\nthe model again). Closed systems self-throttle, because slow responses delay the next request, so a\nclosed-loop load test reports gentler latency than real open traffic at the same throughput. Queue\norder matters too: FIFO lets one 2,000-token job delay many 20-token ones, and priority classes or\nshortest-expected-job-first scheduling cut mean wait for interactive traffic, at the price of possible\nstarvation for the long jobs unless they are aged up or given reserved capacity.","example":"A fleet exposes 16 concurrency slots in total; a request holds a slot for 4 s on average. Erlang C\nshows how waiting behaves as arrival rate rises, and what pooling buys. Exponential service is an\nidealization; LLM traffic whose service-time variance exceeds the exponential's (Cs > 1) will wait\nlonger than this at every row.\n\n```python\nfrom math import factorial\n\ndef mmc(lam, mu, c):\n    \"\"\"M/M/c: returns (utilization, P(wait), mean queue wait Wq, mean time in system W).\"\"\"\n    a = lam / mu                      # offered load, in busy servers\n    rho = a / c\n    if rho >= 1:\n        return rho, 1.0, float(\"inf\"), float(\"inf\")\n    top = a**c / factorial(c) / (1 - rho)\n    p_wait = top / (sum(a**k / factorial(k) for k in range(c)) + top)   # Erlang C\n    wq = p_wait / (c * mu - lam)\n    return rho, p_wait, wq, wq + 1 / mu\n\nmu = 1 / 4.0                          # 4 s mean service per slot\nfor lam in (2.0, 3.0, 3.5, 3.8, 3.95):\n    rho, pw, wq, w = mmc(lam, mu, c=16)\n    print(f\"lam={lam}/s rho={rho:.2f} P(wait)={pw:.2f} Wq={wq:.2f}s L={lam*w:.1f}\")\n# lam=2.0/s rho=0.50 P(wait)=0.01 Wq=0.00s L=8.0\n# lam=3.0/s rho=0.75 P(wait)=0.20 Wq=0.20s L=12.6\n# lam=3.5/s rho=0.88 P(wait)=0.51 Wq=1.02s L=17.6\n# lam=3.8/s rho=0.95 P(wait)=0.78 Wq=3.90s L=30.0\n# lam=3.95/s rho=0.99 P(wait)=0.94 Wq=18.84s L=90.2\n\nprint(mmc(3.5, mu, 16)[2], mmc(1.75, mu, 8)[2])   # pooled 16 slots: ~1.0 s; split 2x8: ~2.5 s each\n```\n\nRead the output: raising load from 3.0 to 3.8 req/s (27% more traffic) raises mean queue wait from\n0.2 s to 3.9 s, about 20×. Splitting the same 16 slots into two dedicated pools of 8 at the same total\ntraffic more than doubles the wait. L follows Little's law throughout.","tradeoffs":["Running at high utilization lowers cost per request, but queueing delay grows as ρ/(1−ρ); the trade flips toward headroom as soon as a latency SLO, not throughput, is the binding requirement.","Dedicated pools per tenant or model give isolation and predictable noisy-neighbour behaviour, but lose pooling efficiency; they win only when isolation or compliance is worth the extra capacity.","Larger batches raise throughput per GPU but lengthen each request's service time, so the optimal concurrency cap depends on whether your SLO is on time-to-first-token or total completion time.","Separating long jobs onto their own queue cuts service variability for interactive traffic, at the cost of a second pool that can sit idle; it pays off when output-length spread is large."],"pitfalls":["Sizing capacity to mean traffic; the symptom is p99 latency that is fine at off-peak and spikes sharply during ordinary daily peaks with no deploy involved.","Benchmarking service time with one request at a time; under real concurrency each request is slower, so the model underestimates ρ and the fleet saturates earlier than predicted.","Letting clients retry immediately on timeout; retries raise λ exactly when the system is already saturated, visible as request rate climbing while successful completions stay flat.","Load testing with a fixed pool of virtual users that wait for each reply; the closed loop hides the knee, so the test passes at a rate where open production traffic shows a p99 cliff.","Treating an unbounded queue as safety; requests wait past their useful deadline, so the system does work nobody is waiting for, seen as high latency together with high client-side cancellation."],"when":"Reach for queueing reasoning whenever someone proposes a utilization target, a replica count, a\nconcurrency cap or a per-tenant pool. Use Little's law first, because it needs no distribution\nassumptions; use M/M/c or Kingman to sanity-check headroom, then validate with a load test that\nreplays real request-length distributions. Measure arrival rate, slot occupancy, queue depth and\nqueue wait separately from service time, since only the split tells you whether to add capacity or\nreduce variability. In interviews, explaining why 90% utilization is dangerous, and deriving\nin-flight concurrency from rate times latency, signals real production experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets engine concurrency caps and gateway queue limits, and instruments queue wait separately from model latency so saturation is visible before users report it.","architect":"Chooses utilization targets, pooling versus per-tenant isolation, and admission control, using the utilization-latency curve to justify headroom in the capacity budget.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their batch job at 9 a.m. slows the interactive assistant, and proposes spreading arrivals or a separate queue rather than buying more GPUs."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant runs at 60% average utilization with acceptable p99. Marketing plans a launch\nthat should add 40% traffic. Leadership asks whether latency will rise about 40%. What do you say?","a":"No: queueing delay is non-linear. 60% × 1.4 is 84% utilization, and the ρ/(1−ρ) factor goes from\n1.5 to about 5.25, so queue wait could rise three to four times even before accounting for bursts, and\nLLM service-time variance makes it worse (Kingman). Service time itself may also lengthen if batches\ngrow. I would compute required in-flight slots with Little's law at the new rate, add capacity to keep\nutilization near the current level, put a concurrency cap and fast rejection at the gateway, and\nvalidate with a load test replaying real output-length distributions before launch."},"quiz":[{"q":"A service handles 20 req/s with a mean latency of 3 s. How many requests are in flight on average?","options":["About 7, because 20 divided by 3 is roughly 7","60, by Little's law L = λ·W","It cannot be known without the service-time distribution","20, because that is the arrival rate per second"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Little's law holds for any stable system regardless of distributions: L = 20 × 3 = 60. The distribution matters for waiting time and tails, not for this average."},{"q":"Two teams each run a dedicated pool of 8 identical slots at 85% utilization. What happens if they merge into one pool of 16?","options":["Waiting time is unchanged because total capacity and load are unchanged","Waiting time doubles because each request now competes with twice as many others","Mean waiting time drops, because a burst in one team's traffic can use slots the other is not using","Utilization falls to about 42% because the slots are now shared"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pooling is statistical multiplexing: one shared queue never leaves a slot idle while requests wait elsewhere. Utilization stays 85%, but the probability and length of waits fall."},{"q":"Kingman's approximation separates utilization from variability. Which change shrinks the variability term (Ca² + Cs²)/2 rather than utilization?","options":["Raising the batch size so each replica processes more tokens per step","Capping output length or routing long jobs elsewhere to reduce service-time variability","Adding retries with no backoff so failed requests finish sooner","Moving to faster GPUs so the mean service time drops"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reducing service-time spread lowers Cs, so waiting falls even at the same utilization. Bigger batches and faster GPUs act on capacity and mean service time, i.e. on ρ, not on the variability term; immediate retries raise arrival burstiness (Ca) and make waiting worse."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/latency-budgets","sources":["Dean & Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"A modern AI request is a pipeline, not a call. A RAG answer may pass through an edge proxy, an auth\ncheck, an input moderation classifier, a query rewrite with a small model, an embedding call, a vector\nsearch, a reranker, prompt assembly, a queue in front of the model, prefill, decode, an output\nguardrail and a logging hop. Each was built by someone who measured it alone and found it \"fast\nenough\". Added together, and at the tail rather than the median, they blow through any product target.\n\nThe naive approach is to set a single end-to-end timeout and hope. That fails in three ways. First, it\ngives no one a number to design against, so a 400 ms reranker gets added without anyone noticing it\nconsumed a fifth of the budget. Second, a single outer timeout fires too late: the request has already\nburned GPU time, and every downstream step kept working after the user gave up. Third, percentiles do\nnot add. If ten parallel calls each have a 1% chance of being slow, the request is slow about 10% of the\ntime (1 − 0.99¹⁰ ≈ 9.6%); with 100 fan-out calls it is about 63%. Tail behaviour in a chain or fan-out\nis worse than any single component's tail, so budgets must be set on percentiles and verified end to\nend, not assembled from component medians.","how":"### 1. Start from the experience, not the system\nPick the metric the user actually feels. For streamed chat that is time-to-first-token (TTFT) plus a\nreadable token rate; for a voice agent it is time to first audio; for an API that returns JSON it is\ntotal completion time; for a background agent it may be minutes per task. Fix the percentile (p95 or\np99) and the load at which it must hold. A target with no percentile or no load is not a budget.\nStreamed output needs two numbers, not one: a TTFT budget for the wait before anything appears, and a\ntime-per-output-token (inter-token latency) budget for the stream itself, because a fast first token\nfollowed by a stuttering stream still feels broken, and the two are bought with different levers\n(prompt length and queueing for TTFT, batch size and model size for per-token time).\n\n### 2. Decompose the critical path\nDraw the path and mark which steps are sequential and which run in parallel. Only the critical path\nconsumes budget; parallel branches cost the slowest branch, not the sum. This is where the biggest wins\nusually hide: running moderation in parallel with retrieval instead of before it removes its cost from\nthe critical path entirely, at the price of occasionally doing retrieval work for a request you then block.\n\n### 3. Allocate, with a reserve\nGive each step a number derived from measured p95 (or p99) under realistic load, and hold back a\nreserve of 10–20% (an illustrative rule of thumb) for network jitter, retries and growth. For the model\nstep, split the budget into queue wait, prefill (grows with prompt length) and decode (time per output\ntoken × tokens). Prompt length is therefore a latency decision as well as a cost one: adding 20 retrieved\nchunks lengthens prefill and TTFT. Queue wait is the most load-sensitive line in the whole budget, because\nit grows as ρ/(1−ρ) with utilization (see queueing theory); an allocation measured at 50% load is only\nvalid up to the utilization you tested, so state that utilization alongside the budget.\n\n### 4. Enforce with deadlines, not just timeouts\nTurn the budget into a **deadline**: an absolute time by which the response must be produced, created\nat the edge and propagated with the request (gRPC deadlines do this natively; over HTTP, pass a header\nwith the remaining time). Each step sets its timeout to the smaller of its own allocation and what is\nleft minus what later steps need. This matters *because* a step that started late should get less time,\nnot the same time, and a downstream service should stop working once the caller has given up.\n\n### 5. Degrade instead of failing\nClassify steps as required or optional. When the remaining budget is short, skip the optional ones\n(query rewrite, reranking, a second retrieval hop) rather than squeezing them into a timeout they will\nmiss. Retry only when the remaining budget exceeds the attempt's p95; otherwise the retry has a large\nchance of timing out and adds load exactly when the system is struggling. Hedged requests (send a second copy after the p95 delay and take the first\nresponse) cut tails for idempotent reads at a modest extra cost.\n\n### 6. Verify end to end\nUse distributed tracing so each span reports its duration against its allocation, and alert on the\nend-to-end percentile, not on component averages. Budgets drift as features land; review them like any\nother interface contract.","example":"An interactive RAG assistant with a target of p95 TTFT ≤ 2.0 s at peak load. Allocation (illustrative,\nfrom measured p95s): edge and TLS 100 ms, gateway and auth 30 ms, input moderation 120 ms, query rewrite\n300 ms (optional), embedding 50 ms, vector search 80 ms, rerank 200 ms (optional), prompt assembly 20 ms,\nmodel queue plus prefill 800 ms, reserve 300 ms. Sum: 2.0 s. Moving moderation in parallel with\nretrieval frees 120 ms. With 400 output tokens at 25 ms per token, the full answer takes another 10 s,\nwhich is acceptable streamed but not if an output guardrail must see the whole answer before display.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nclass Deadline:\n    def __init__(self, seconds): self.end = time.monotonic() + seconds\n    def remaining(self): return self.end - time.monotonic()\n\nasync def stage(fn, budget, dl, keep=0.0, optional=False, fallback=None):\n    avail = dl.remaining() - keep           # leave room for the stages after this one\n    if optional and avail < budget:\n        return fallback                     # skip an optional step rather than squeeze it\n    try:\n        return await asyncio.wait_for(fn(), max(0.0, min(budget, avail)))\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        if optional:\n            return fallback\n        raise\n\nPREFILL_P95 = 0.8                           # measured queue + prefill at peak\n\nasync def first_token(query, remaining_s, rewrite, retrieve, rerank, generate):\n    dl = Deadline(remaining_s)              # from the propagated deadline header, not a fresh 2.0 s\n    q = await stage(lambda: rewrite(query), 0.30, dl, keep=0.15 + PREFILL_P95,\n                    optional=True, fallback=query)\n    docs = await stage(lambda: retrieve(q), 0.15, dl, keep=PREFILL_P95)\n    docs = await stage(lambda: rerank(q, docs), 0.20, dl, keep=PREFILL_P95,\n                       optional=True, fallback=docs[:5])\n    return await stage(lambda: generate(q, docs), dl.remaining(), dl)\n```\n\n`remaining_s` comes from the deadline set at the edge, so upstream delay is already subtracted. On a\nnormal request about 1.87 s is left (2.0 s minus 130 ms of edge and gateway) and the rewrite runs. If the\ngateway queue ate an extra 700 ms, only 1.17 s is left; the rewrite needs its 0.30 s plus the 0.95 s kept\nfor retrieval and generation, so it is skipped automatically, and generation's 0.8 s share stays\nprotected. Creating `Deadline(2.0)` inside this function instead would silently ignore that upstream\ndelay, which is exactly the bug deadline propagation exists to prevent.","tradeoffs":["Tight per-step allocations make regressions visible early, but over-precise budgets create churn; they flip to a burden when the pipeline changes weekly and owners spend time renegotiating numbers.","Skipping optional steps protects latency at the cost of answer quality; this is right for chat and wrong for workflows where a wrong answer costs more than a slow one.","Hedged requests cut tail latency but add load, roughly the fraction of requests that exceed the hedge delay; they backfire on saturated systems or non-idempotent calls.","Streaming hides decode time behind reading time, but forces output guardrails to work on partial text or to hold tokens back, which reintroduces latency."],"pitfalls":["Adding component p95s or medians and calling it the end-to-end p95; the symptom is a dashboard where every service is green while users report slowness.","Retrying inside a budget that cannot afford another attempt; visible as retry counts rising together with timeouts, and more GPU work per successful request.","Budgeting the model step from a single-request benchmark; under load, queue wait and prefill grow, seen as TTFT p95 degrading at peak hours while prompt sizes are unchanged.","Enforcing the deadline only at the edge and never cancelling downstream work; the user sees a timeout while the model keeps decoding, visible as GPU or token spend on requests whose clients already left.","Letting retrieval chunk counts creep upward without re-budgeting prefill; symptom is TTFT rising after a \"quality\" change that never touched serving."],"when":"Write a latency budget for any user-facing AI path with more than two hops, and before adding a new\nstep such as a reranker, guardrail or tool call. Measure p95 and p99 per span under peak load, the\nend-to-end percentile, and the share of requests that took a degraded path. Skip formal budgets for\noffline batch jobs, where throughput and cost per task matter more than per-request latency. In\ninterviews, decomposing a target into steps, pointing out that percentiles do not add, and proposing\ndeadline propagation with optional-step degradation is the expected answer to \"make this feel fast\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements deadline propagation, per-stage timeouts and degradation paths, and adds tracing spans so each step's latency is compared against its allocation.","architect":"Owns the end-to-end target and its split, decides what runs in parallel, which steps are optional, and when a slow component must be redesigned rather than tuned.","fde":"Accounts for the customer's network, proxies and on-prem retrieval systems, which often consume budget the vendor's demo never had to pay."},"check":{"q":"Your voice agent must start speaking within 1 s at p95. Measured p95s: speech-to-text 300 ms,\nretrieval 250 ms, LLM TTFT 600 ms, text-to-speech first audio 200 ms. What do you change?","a":"The sequential sum of p95s is 1.35 s, and the true p95 of the sum is usually somewhat lower than that\nsum for independent steps, but still clearly over budget. Options: stream speech-to-text and start retrieval on partial\ntranscripts or run it in parallel with endpointing; cut prompt length or use a smaller or\nprefix-cached model to reduce TTFT; stream LLM tokens into TTS sentence by sentence so first audio\ndepends on the first clause, not the full answer; and speak a short acknowledgement while retrieval\ncompletes. Then verify the end-to-end p95 with tracing under load, keeping a reserve for network\njitter."},"quiz":[{"q":"A request fans out to 50 parallel services, each with a 1% chance of exceeding its p99. Roughly how often does the request wait on at least one slow call?","options":["About 1%, since each service meets its p99","About 50%, one percent per service added linearly","About 40%, because 1 − 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.39","Never, because parallel calls hide each other's latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The request waits for its slowest branch. The chance that all 50 are fast is 0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 0.61, so about 39% of requests hit at least one tail. Linear addition overestimates; ignoring fan-out underestimates."},{"q":"Why propagate an absolute deadline instead of giving every stage a fixed timeout?","options":["Fixed timeouts are not supported by most HTTP clients","A stage that starts late gets only the time actually left, and downstream services stop working once the caller has given up","Deadlines make each stage run faster by raising its priority","Deadlines remove the need to measure per-stage latency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed timeouts let each stage spend its full allocation even after earlier stages overran, so the total exceeds the target and abandoned work keeps consuming capacity."},{"q":"The remaining budget is 300 ms and the model call's p95 is 700 ms. What should the pipeline do?","options":["Retry the previous slow stage once to recover time","Call the model anyway with a 300 ms timeout","Take the degraded path: skip or shortcut steps, fall back, or fail fast with a clear response","Extend the deadline because the model step is required"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 300 ms timeout on a 700 ms p95 call fails a large share of the time while still consuming GPU time. Degrading or failing fast keeps the SLO honest and avoids wasted work."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/scalability","sources":["Amdahl, 1967 — Validity of the Single Processor Approach to Achieving Large Scale Computing Capabilities","Gunther, 2007 — Guerrilla Capacity Planning"],"why":"The intuitive model of scaling is \"twice the machines, twice the throughput\". It holds for perfectly\nindependent work and almost nothing else. Every real system has a part that cannot be parallelized: a\nsingle primary database, a global rate limit, a lock around a shared index, a coordinator that every\nrequest touches. Amdahl showed that if a fraction of the work is serial, speedup is capped at one over\nthat fraction no matter how many workers you add. Worse, distributed systems also pay a coordination\ncost that grows with the square of the worker count, because workers must exchange state with each other\n(cache invalidation, consensus, connection fan-in). That cost produces *retrograde* scaling: throughput\npeaks and then falls.\n\nAI systems add their own non-linear limits. A model replica needs enough accelerator memory for its\nweights plus a useful KV cache, so capacity comes in large, indivisible steps (one or several whole GPUs\nfor a large model); a new replica can take minutes to start because weights must be loaded; a hosted API has a\ntokens-per-minute quota that no amount of client hardware raises; and caches that make inference cheap\n(KV and prefix caches) are local to one replica, so spreading traffic evenly destroys their hit rate.\nWithout a scalability model, teams discover these walls during a launch, after adding hardware that\ndid not help.","how":"### Vertical vs horizontal\n**Scaling up** (bigger GPU, more memory, tensor parallelism across GPUs in one node) raises what one\nreplica can do and is often the only option for a model that does not fit smaller hardware. It has a\nceiling and usually a price per unit that rises with size. **Scaling out** (more replicas behind a load\nbalancer) raises total throughput and fault tolerance, but only for work that can be partitioned and\nonly until a shared component saturates. For LLM serving, tensor or pipeline parallelism is scale-up of\none model instance; data-parallel replicas are scale-out.\n\n### Amdahl and the Universal Scalability Law\nAmdahl's law: speedup(N) = 1 / ((1 − p) + p/N), with p the parallel fraction. The ceiling is\n1/(1 − p): if 5% of each request's time is spent holding one shared lock or primary, no fleet size gets\npast 20×, and 64 workers already give only about 15×. Gunther's Universal\nScalability Law (USL) adds coherency:\n\n```\nC(N) = N / (1 + α(N − 1) + β·N(N − 1))\n```\n\nα is contention (queueing on a shared resource), β is coherency (pairwise coordination). With β = 0\nthis is exactly Amdahl rewritten (α = 1 − p), and capacity flattens toward 1/α. With β > 0,\ncapacity peaks at N* = √((1 − α)/β) and declines after. The value of the model is diagnostic: fit α and\nβ from a few load-test points, and they tell you whether to remove a serialization point (α) or reduce\ncross-node chatter (β), and roughly where adding nodes stops paying.\n\n### Where AI systems stop scaling\n- **Batch is the first axis, inside one replica**: with continuous batching, a replica's throughput\n  rises steeply with concurrent sequences, because each weight read from memory is shared by the whole\n  batch, while each request's per-token latency rises more slowly. The useful operating point is the\n  largest batch that still meets the per-token latency target; adding replicas before reaching it\n  buys GPUs you are not using.\n- **GPU memory per replica**: concurrency per replica is bounded by KV-cache memory, so throughput per\n  replica plateaus; you scale by adding replicas, not by pushing more requests into one.\n- **Provider quotas**: tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute limits per key, per model and per region\n  form a hard ceiling; scaling past it means quota increases, more regions, or multiple providers.\n- **State and affinity**: prefix caches and conversation state live on a replica. Pure round-robin loses\n  the cache hit rate; affinity routing (by session or prefix hash) keeps it but makes load uneven.\n- **Shared stores**: vector indexes, metadata databases, and the logging pipeline that records every\n  prompt are classic α sources once the model tier scales.\n- **Elasticity lag**: GPU nodes and model loads take minutes, so autoscaling on CPU utilization reacts\n  too late. Scale on leading signals such as queue depth, in-flight requests or KV-cache utilization, and\n  keep warm capacity for the time it takes a new replica to become ready.\n\n### Designing for it\nKeep the request tier stateless and push state into stores designed to scale; partition by tenant or\nkey where work is independent; make the serial parts small and fast; add backpressure so overload\nproduces fast rejection instead of collapse; and test scaling explicitly rather than assuming it.","example":"A retrieval service is load-tested with N workers sharing one vector index and metadata database. The\nmeasurements are illustrative; the fitting method is standard USL linearization.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\nN = [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 24]                 # workers\nX = [40, 78, 148, 262, 410, 485]         # measured req/s\n\n# USL: X(N) = X1*N / (1 + a(N-1) + b*N(N-1)).  Linearise: X1*N/X(N) - 1 = a(N-1) + b*N(N-1)\nrows = [(n - 1, n * (n - 1), X[0] * n / x - 1) for n, x in zip(N, X)]\ns11 = sum(u * u for u, v, y in rows); s12 = sum(u * v for u, v, y in rows)\ns22 = sum(v * v for u, v, y in rows)\nt1 = sum(u * y for u, v, y in rows); t2 = sum(v * y for u, v, y in rows)\ndet = s11 * s22 - s12 * s12              # 2x2 least squares, no intercept\na = (t1 * s22 - t2 * s12) / det\nb = (s11 * t2 - s12 * t1) / det\n\nusl = lambda n: X[0] * n / (1 + a * (n - 1) + b * n * (n - 1))\nprint(f\"contention a={a:.3f} coherency b={b:.5f} peak near N={sqrt((1 - a) / b):.0f}\")\nfor n in (16, 32, 48, 64, 96):\n    print(f\"N={n:3d} predicted {usl(n):4.0f} req/s efficiency {usl(n) / (X[0] * n):.0%}\")\n# contention a=0.026 coherency b=0.00069 peak near N=38\n# N= 32 predicted 514 req/s ...  N= 64 predicted 473 req/s ...  N= 96 predicted 394 req/s\n```\n\nThe fit says throughput tops out near 38 workers at roughly 520 req/s (per-worker efficiency is\nalready down to about a third), and doubling from 32 to 64\nworkers would *lower* throughput. Raising capacity here means attacking β (for example, sharding the\nindex so workers stop coordinating on one store) or α (caching hot metadata), not buying replicas.","tradeoffs":["Scale-out buys fault tolerance and near-linear throughput for independent work, but adds coordination; it flips to a loss once coherency cost dominates, visible as a USL peak.","Affinity routing preserves prefix and session caches, cutting cost and TTFT, but creates hot replicas; it stops paying when traffic is dominated by a few large tenants or sessions.","Keeping warm GPU capacity hides cold-start minutes during spikes, but you pay for idle hardware; it is worth it when spikes are frequent and the latency SLO is strict.","Sharding a shared store removes a contention point, but cross-shard queries and rebalancing add complexity; it is premature while the store is far from saturation."],"pitfalls":["Load-testing one replica and multiplying by N; the symptom is a launch where adding replicas leaves throughput flat because a shared store or quota was the real limit.","Autoscaling GPU replicas on CPU or GPU utilization; visible as replicas arriving after the spike has already caused timeouts, because model loading takes minutes.","Round-robin load balancing across replicas with prefix caching; symptom is cache hit rate falling as replicas are added, and TTFT and cost per request rising with scale.","Ignoring provider quotas in the scaling plan; seen as 429 errors at a traffic level far below what the application tier could handle."],"when":"Use this reasoning before any launch, migration or \"just add replicas\" proposal. Run load tests at\nseveral sizes (at least four or five points), fit a USL curve, and find which shared resource sets α\nand β. Track per-replica throughput as you scale, cache hit rate, queue depth and quota headroom.\nDo not invest in elaborate scale-out architecture for workloads that fit comfortably on one replica\nwith headroom; simplicity is cheaper. In interviews, naming the specific shared bottleneck and the\nsignal you would autoscale on beats generic \"make it stateless and horizontal\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes stateless services, adds affinity routing and backpressure, and runs multi-size load tests so scaling claims come from measurements rather than assumptions.","architect":"Decides partitioning keys, scale-up versus scale-out for models, multi-region and multi-provider strategy, and where shared state lives so it does not cap growth.","fde":"Finds the customer-side ceilings, such as an on-prem search cluster or a single API key's quota, that will limit rollout long before the vendor's platform does."},"check":{"q":"Going from 8 to 16 inference replicas raised throughput by only 15%, and GPU utilization per\nreplica fell. What do you investigate, and in what order?","a":"Falling per-replica utilization means replicas are waiting on something shared, so the GPU tier is\nnot the bottleneck. I would trace one request's critical path under load and look for the component\nwhose latency rose: a vector store or metadata DB (contention), a gateway or rate limiter, a provider\nquota producing 429s, or connection pools sized for 8 replicas. I would also check load balancing:\nround-robin can collapse prefix-cache hit rates, increasing prefill work. Then fit α and β from load\ntests at several sizes to confirm whether the limit is a serial point or coordination cost before\nredesigning."},"quiz":[{"q":"In the Universal Scalability Law, what does a positive coherency coefficient β imply?","options":["Throughput grows linearly but with a lower slope","Throughput peaks at some N and then declines as more workers are added","Throughput plateaus at 1/β and stays flat","The system is limited by its serial fraction only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β models pairwise coordination cost growing roughly with N², so beyond N* = √((1 − α)/β) each added worker costs more than it contributes. Contention alone (α) gives a plateau, not a decline."},{"q":"Why is CPU utilization a poor autoscaling signal for GPU inference replicas?","options":["CPU metrics are not exposed inside containers","GPU replicas never use the CPU","It does not track the real constraint, and new replicas take minutes to load, so scaling must react to leading signals like queue depth","Autoscalers can only read GPU metrics"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Inference load shows up in queue depth, in-flight requests and KV-cache utilization. Because model loading is slow, a lagging signal adds capacity after users have already timed out."},{"q":"A team adds replicas and sees prefix-cache hit rate fall. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Larger fleets use smaller cache sizes per replica by default","Requests sharing a prefix are spread across replicas, so each replica's local cache sees fewer repeats","Prefix caching only works with a single replica","More replicas increase prompt lengths"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefix caches are local to a replica. Without prefix- or session-aware routing, traffic spreads thinly, so each cache sees fewer repeats; affinity routing restores the hit rate."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-optimization","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"AI features have a cost structure unlike most software: every request has a marginal cost, often cents,\nthat scales directly with usage and with design choices such as context size and model tier. A feature\nthat is cheap in a pilot can become the largest line on the cloud bill at production volume, and\ngross margin on an AI product can be negative if no one looks.\n\nThe naive responses both fail. \"Switch to the cheapest model\" saves money per call but often lowers task\nsuccess, which pushes work into retries, escalations and human review, so cost per *successful* task can\nrise. \"Optimize the GPU kernels\" or \"negotiate a discount\" works on the smallest lever first: a 20%\nprice cut is dwarfed by not sending 60% of the tokens in the first place. Cost work also fails when it\nis done blind: without attribution by feature and request type, teams optimize the endpoint they happen\nto be looking at, not the one that dominates spend. The discipline exists to apply effort where the\nmoney actually is, in the order that yields the most per unit of risk, with quality held constant by\nmeasurement rather than by hope.","how":"### 0. Measure and attribute first\nTag every model call with feature, tenant, model and request type, and record tokens by type (fresh\ninput, cached input, output, reasoning). Rank spend by feature and look at the cost distribution: often a\nfew features and a long tail of large requests dominate. Pair cost with an outcome metric (task success,\nacceptance rate) so every later change can be judged on cost per successful task.\n\n### 1. Do not call the model\nCheapest token is the one never sent. Exact-match response caches for repeated queries (keyed on\nnormalized input plus the version of every input that affects the answer), deterministic code paths for\ncases rules handle reliably, and skipping steps that do not change the outcome. Semantic caches (reuse\nan answer for a *similar* question) save more but can return a wrong answer for a subtly different\nquestion, so they need a strict threshold and evaluation.\n\n### 2. Send fewer tokens\nInput usually dominates RAG and agent cost. Retrieve fewer, better chunks (reranking lets you cut top-k),\ntrim system prompts and tool schemas, compact long histories, and stop pasting entire tool outputs into\ncontext. Turn on prompt caching for stable prefixes: put fixed instructions and schemas first and\nvariable content last *because* caching only matches identical prefixes. Cap output with concise\nformats and `max_tokens`, since output tokens are usually priced higher than input. Two output sources\nare easy to miss: reasoning (\"thinking\") tokens, which are billed as output on most providers even when\nnot shown, so a reasoning budget or effort setting is a cost control; and agent loops, where every extra\nstep re-sends the growing context, so cost per task grows faster than linearly with step count and\ncutting one redundant tool round-trip can save more than trimming the prompt.\n\n### 3. Use a cheaper model where it is good enough\nRoute easy requests to a smaller model and escalate hard or low-confidence ones (routing or cascading).\nDistill or fine-tune a small model for a narrow, high-volume task. The gain is large because price per\ntoken differs by one to two orders of magnitude across model tiers, but routing errors cost quality, so\nmeasure success per route.\n\n### 4. Buy capacity more cheaply\nUse asynchronous batch APIs for work that can wait (many providers discount them), committed-use or\nreserved capacity for steady baseline load, spot or preemptible instances for interruptible batch jobs,\nand self-hosting only where volume keeps GPUs highly utilized.\n\n### 5. Tune serving\nFor self-hosted models: raise utilization with continuous batching and right-sized concurrency, quantize\nweights or the KV cache where evals allow, enable prefix caching, right-size GPUs, and scale down idle\nreplicas. These matter most once the fleet is large.\n\n### Guardrails on the process\nLevers compound multiplicatively, so estimate each one on the base left after the previous ones, not on\nthe original bill: a 50% context cut and a 50% routing saving give 75%, not 100%. They also interact;\nrouting traffic away from the large model shrinks the value of the large model's prompt cache. Each change ships behind an eval and, ideally, an A/B or shadow comparison; the acceptance criterion is\ncost per successful task at equal or better quality and within the latency budget.","example":"A document-QA feature (all figures illustrative): 1M requests per month, 8,000 input tokens per request\n(1,500 fixed instructions plus 6,500 retrieved context), 500 output tokens, on a large model at $3 per\nmillion input and $15 per million output. Cost per request is $0.024 + $0.0075 = $0.0315, or $31,500 a\nmonth. Apply levers in order, each confirmed by the eval suite:\n\n1. **Exact-match cache** on normalized question plus document version: measured 10% hit rate. Billable\n   requests fall to 900k: $28,350.\n2. **Prompt caching** of the 1,500-token fixed prefix, read at $0.30 per million (a 90% discount; the\n   discount and any cache-write premium vary by provider and are ignored here): saves 1,500 × $2.70/M ≈\n   $0.004 per request, giving $0.02745 per request and $24,705 a month.\n3. **Rerank and cut context** from 6,500 to 3,000 tokens (eval shows no drop): saves $0.0105 per\n   request, giving $0.01695 and $15,255.\n4. **Route 60% of requests** (simple lookups) to a small model at $0.25/$1.25 per million, about\n   $0.00175 per request, escalating 10% of those to the large model: blended $0.0088 per request,\n   about $7,960 a month.\n\nTotal reduction is roughly 75%, and none of it came from GPU tuning or price negotiation. Step 4 is the\nriskiest: if escalation is triggered by low confidence that misses wrong answers, success drops and the\nsaving is illusory, so the success rate per route is the number to watch.","tradeoffs":["Smaller models and routing cut cost sharply, but add a routing error rate; the trade flips when failures are expensive (refunds, compliance) and cost per successful task rises.","Aggressive context trimming saves input tokens and latency, but can drop the one chunk the answer needed; it stops paying when retrieval recall is already marginal.","Batch APIs and spot capacity are cheaper, but add delay or interruption; they suit offline work and are wrong for anything on an interactive path.","Semantic caching avoids calls for similar queries, but risks serving a wrong cached answer; it fits stable FAQ-like traffic and fails where small wording changes alter meaning."],"pitfalls":["Optimizing without attribution; the symptom is a successful saving on one endpoint while total spend keeps rising because another feature dominated all along.","Measuring cost per call instead of per successful task; visible as falling per-call cost alongside rising retries, escalations or human-review volume.","Breaking prompt caching by putting timestamps, user names or request IDs at the start of the prompt; the cached-token share drops to near zero after a prompt change.","Keying a response or semantic cache only on the question text; one tenant or user is served an answer built from another's documents, found as a data-exposure incident rather than a cost metric. Include tenant, permission scope and source versions in the key.","Shipping a cheaper configuration without an eval gate; quality complaints or lower acceptance rates appear weeks later and are hard to trace to the change."],"when":"Start cost work once a feature has real traffic and its spend is material, or earlier if the cost model\nshows negative unit economics at target volume. Begin with attribution, then work down the lever list,\nestimating savings from traces before building anything. Measure cost per successful task, cached-token\nshare, tokens per request by type, routing mix and quality per route. Avoid premature optimization in\nprototypes, where learning what users need is worth more than a cheaper token. In interviews, ordering\nthe levers and insisting on an eval gate is what separates practitioners from people who only say\n\"use a smaller model\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements caches, prompt-prefix ordering, context trimming and routing, and wires per-call cost and success metrics so each change can be evaluated.","architect":"Sets the cost target per unit of work, decides which levers the platform provides centrally (gateway caching, routing, batch lanes), and owns the quality bar changes must meet.","fde":"Finds customer-specific waste, such as oversized documents or redundant calls in their integration, and proves savings against the customer's own acceptance metrics."},"check":{"q":"Finance asks you to cut a RAG assistant's model spend by half within a month without hurting\nquality. How do you approach it?","a":"First attribute spend: tokens by type per feature and request class, and the cost distribution,\nso I know whether input context, output length, a few heavy tenants or an agent loop dominates. Then\nestimate each lever from traces: exact caching for repeats, prompt caching with a stable prefix,\nreranking to cut retrieved context, output caps, and routing easy queries to a smaller model. I would\nship them in order of savings per unit of risk, each behind the eval suite and a shadow or A/B check,\ntracking cost per successful answer, not per call. Price negotiation and GPU tuning come last."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which metric best guards against a cost optimization that secretly degrades the product?","options":["Cost per API call","Total monthly token count","Cost per successful task, measured alongside a quality eval","Average output length per response"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Per-call cost falls when a cheaper model fails more often, but retries, escalations and human review then push cost per success up. Tying cost to successful outcomes exposes that trade."},{"q":"A prompt change moved the current date and user name to the top of the system prompt. What cost effect is most likely?","options":["Output tokens increase because the model writes longer answers","Prompt-cache hits collapse, because the prefix now differs across users and days","Nothing, since the total prompt length is unchanged","Batch API discounts no longer apply"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prompt caching matches identical prefixes. Putting variable content first makes the prefix differ per user and per day, so the instructions after it are billed at the full input price again; total length being unchanged is irrelevant."},{"q":"In what order should levers generally be tried for an API-based RAG feature?","options":["Negotiate price, tune GPU kernels, then trim prompts","Switch everything to the smallest model, then fix quality later","Attribute spend, avoid calls, cut tokens, route to cheaper models, then buy capacity more cheaply","Add semantic caching first because it removes the most calls"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attribution shows where the money is; avoiding calls and tokens is low-risk and often large; routing has higher quality risk; cheaper capacity (batch lanes, commitments) only applies to deferrable or steady load, so it comes after removing work. Semantic caching is not first because it carries a wrong-answer risk and its hit rate is unknown until measured."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-modeling","sources":["Official docs: provider pricing pages (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google)"],"why":"LLM spend is metered per token, so cost is set by the shape of each request, not by servers running.\nTwo features that look alike on a product roadmap can differ in cost by a hundred times: a one-shot\nclassification with a 300-token prompt versus a 20-turn agent that pastes tool results into its context\neach step. Teams that estimate cost as \"requests per day × a typical price\" are routinely surprised,\nbecause they miss three things.\n\nFirst, input and output are priced differently, and models that reason emit billed tokens the user never\nsees. Second, conversation and agent loops re-send everything each turn, so a long session is not N\ntimes a short one but closer to N². Third, the cost distribution is heavy-tailed: a small share of long\ndocuments, runaway agent loops or retries can dominate the invoice. A cost model makes these visible\n*before* launch, which is when you can still change the design (cap turns, cache prefixes, route to a\nsmaller model) instead of explaining an invoice. It is also the input to break-even analysis, pricing\nand unit economics: none of those are possible without a defensible cost per request.","how":"### The per-call formula\n```\ncost = fresh_input_tokens × p_in\n     + cached_input_tokens × p_cached\n     + output_tokens × p_out            # includes reasoning/thinking tokens where billed\n     + non-model costs (embeddings, rerank, vector search, tool APIs, guardrail models)\n```\nSome providers also charge a premium to *write* a prompt-cache entry, a discount for asynchronous batch\nprocessing, or different rates above a context-length threshold. Pricing structures are vendor- and\nmodel-specific and change often, so a cost model should read prices from configuration, never hard-code\nthem.\n\n### Why output costs more\nPrefill processes the whole prompt in one parallel pass, which uses GPU compute efficiently. Decode\nproduces output one token at a time, and each step reads the full weights and KV cache for very little\narithmetic, so a GPU produces far fewer output tokens per second than it ingests input tokens. Providers\nprice that: output is commonly several times the input price. The design consequence is that verbosity\nis expensive, and a reasoning model that \"thinks\" for thousands of tokens can cost more than a larger\nmodel that answers directly.\n\n### Tokens are not words, and not portable\nToken counts depend on the tokenizer. The same text yields different counts on different model\nfamilies, and code, non-English text and JSON tend to tokenize less efficiently than English prose. A\nper-word estimate built on one model is wrong on another; measure with the target model's tokenizer.\n\n### Multi-turn growth\nIn a chat or agent loop, turn t sends the system prompt, tool schemas and every earlier message. If each\nturn adds d tokens, the total input over T turns is about T·prefix + d·T²/2. The quadratic term dominates\nlong sessions. Two levers attack it: prompt (prefix) caching, which bills repeated prefixes at a\ndiscount, and context compaction, which shrinks the history itself. They act differently: caching\nleaves the T² shape intact but multiplies its coefficient by the cached-to-fresh price ratio (0.1 in the\nexample below), while compaction caps how large the history can grow, turning the quadratic back into\nroughly linear growth once the cap is reached. Caching also only helps if the next turn arrives within\nthe cache lifetime; human think time between turns can exceed it, so measure the cached-token share\nrather than assuming it.\n\n### Self-hosted token cost\nFor your own GPUs, the \"price\" is derived: cost per token = (GPU $/hour) / (tokens produced per hour at\nthe utilization you actually achieve). The utilization term is decisive. A fleet that is busy a third\nof the time pays three times as much per token as the same fleet kept full.\n\n### Model the distribution\nBuild the model from logged traces: for each feature, the distribution of input, cached and output\ntokens and of turns per session. Report p50, p95 and p99 cost per request and the share of spend from the\ntop 1% of requests. For the monthly forecast the right statistic is the true mean from that distribution\n(forecast = Σ over features of volume × mean cost per request), and it is usually well above the cost of\nthe \"typical\" request people reason from. For risk, the mean alone hides the runaway sessions that\nbudgets and step caps must bound, so the forecast should state how much of it comes from the tail.","example":"Illustrative prices: $3 per million input tokens, $15 per million output, $0.30 per million cached input\n(cache-write premiums ignored). Substitute your provider's current sheet.\n\n```python\nP_IN, P_OUT, P_CACHED = 3.00, 15.00, 0.30          # $ per million tokens (illustrative)\n\ndef call_cost(fresh_in, out, cached_in=0):\n    return (fresh_in * P_IN + cached_in * P_CACHED + out * P_OUT) / 1e6\n\ndef agent_run(turns, prefix=4_000, delta=600, out=200, cache=False):\n    \"\"\"Each turn re-sends the history: prefix + all earlier tool results and outputs.\"\"\"\n    total, history = 0.0, prefix\n    for t in range(turns):\n        reused = history - delta - out if (cache and t > 0) else 0   # last turn's prompt\n        total += call_cost(history - reused, out, reused)\n        history += delta + out\n    return total\n\nprint(f\"single RAG call: ${call_cost(6_000, 400):.4f}\")              # $0.0240\nfor turns in (4, 12, 24):\n    print(turns, round(agent_run(turns), 3), round(agent_run(turns, cache=True), 3))\n# 4 turns: $0.074 vs $0.036 cached | 12: $0.338 vs $0.101 | 24: $1.022 vs $0.228\n\ngpu_hr, tok_per_s = 4.00, 1_500                     # illustrative $/GPU-hour, aggregate output tok/s\nfor util in (0.9, 0.3):\n    print(util, round(gpu_hr / (tok_per_s * 3600 * util) * 1e6, 2), \"$/M output tokens\")\n# 0.9 -> $0.82/M ; 0.3 -> $2.47/M\n```\n\nGoing from 4 to 24 turns is 6× the turns but about 14× the cost without caching; prefix caching cuts the\n24-turn run by more than 4×. The self-hosted number shows why utilization, not GPU list price, decides\nwhether owning hardware is cheap.","tradeoffs":["A detailed per-feature model catches design choices that will be expensive, but costs time to build and maintain; for a prototype a spreadsheet of token counts per call is enough until traffic is real.","Prompt caching cuts repeated-prefix cost sharply, but only pays when prefixes are byte-identical and reused within the cache lifetime; it flips to a small loss where writes carry a premium and reuse is rare.","Reasoning models raise output tokens per task, but can lower total cost when they replace several retries or a multi-step chain; measure cost per *successful* task, not per call.","Self-hosting lowers marginal token cost at high utilization, but turns cost fixed; it flips to more expensive than an API when traffic is low or spiky."],"pitfalls":["Estimating from a typical or hand-picked request instead of the logged distribution; the symptom is an invoice several times the forecast, driven by a long tail of large documents or long agent sessions.","Forgetting hidden reasoning tokens or tool-schema tokens in the prompt; visible as billed input or output counts far above what the visible text suggests.","Reusing a token-per-word ratio from one model family on another; the forecast drifts after a model switch even though the traffic did not change.","Ignoring non-model costs such as embeddings, reranking, guardrail models and trace storage; total spend per request comes out well above the LLM line item."],"when":"Build a cost model before committing to any feature design that calls a model in a loop, ingests\nuser-supplied documents, or will run at volume; rebuild it from real traces after launch. Use it to set\nmax-turn and max-token limits, to decide where prompt caching and smaller models pay off, and as the\ninput to pricing and break-even decisions. Measure tokens by type (fresh, cached, output, reasoning) per\nfeature and per request, and watch the p95 and p99, not only the mean. In interviews, deriving the\nquadratic growth of agent cost and the utilization term in self-hosted cost shows real understanding.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Logs token counts by type for every call, tags them with feature and request IDs, and writes the cost function that dashboards and budget guards share.","architect":"Uses the model to choose between designs, set turn and context limits, and decide where caching, smaller models or self-hosting change the economics.","fde":"Builds a cost estimate from the customer's real document sizes and usage volumes, so the commercial proposal survives contact with production traffic."},"check":{"q":"A support agent averages 8 turns, but finance's forecast assumed cost = 8 × a single-call cost.\nThe invoice is 4× the forecast. Explain, and say what you would change.","a":"Each turn re-sends the system prompt, tool schemas and full history, so input tokens grow with\nthe turn number and total input grows roughly with the square of turns; multiplying a single call by 8\nundercounts badly. The tail also matters: a few 30-turn sessions dominate. I would rebuild the model from\ntraces with token distributions per turn, enable prompt caching for the stable prefix, compact or\nsummarize history past a threshold, cap turns and tokens per session, and route simple tickets to a\nsmaller model, then track p95 cost per resolved ticket rather than per call."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do hosted providers typically price output tokens higher than input tokens?","options":["Output tokens are larger than input tokens in bytes","Output is generated sequentially in decode steps that use the GPU far less efficiently than the parallel prefill of input","Output tokens must be checked by a safety model that input tokens skip","Input tokens are cached for free on every request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill processes the prompt in one parallel, compute-efficient pass; decode yields one token per step and is memory-bandwidth bound, so each output token consumes more GPU time."},{"q":"An agent's turns each add about the same number of tokens to history. How does total input cost scale with the number of turns, without caching or compaction?","options":["Linearly, since each turn adds the same number of tokens","Roughly quadratically, because each turn re-sends all earlier turns","Logarithmically, because providers discount long prompts","It stays constant after the first turn"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Turn t's prompt contains all t earlier increments, so total input is a sum of an arithmetic series, proportional to T². Each turn adding a constant amount is exactly what makes the total quadratic."},{"q":"A self-hosted GPU costs the same per hour regardless of load. What most changes its cost per token?","options":["The model's parameter count only","The provider's published per-token price","The utilization actually achieved, since cost per token is hourly cost divided by tokens produced per hour","Whether the prompt uses JSON output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fixed hourly cost spread over fewer tokens raises the per-token price; a fleet at 30% utilization pays three times more per token than the same fleet at 90%."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/unit-economics-per-successful-task","sources":[],"why":"Model providers bill per token, so dashboards naturally show cost per call or per request. That number\nanswers the wrong question. The business does not buy calls; it buys resolved tickets, extracted\ninvoices, merged pull requests, qualified leads. Between a call and a resolved task sit retries, tool\nerrors, verification passes, escalations to a human and, worst, wrong outputs that nobody caught and\nthat cost a refund, a compliance incident or a customer.\n\nOptimizing cost per call therefore rewards the wrong design. Swapping to a smaller model halves the per-\ncall cost and can triple the cost per resolved task once escalations are counted. Agents make the gap\nwider: a run that loops for 40 steps and then fails has consumed real money and produced nothing. A\nsecond failure is definitional: \"success\" that is not measured against explicit acceptance criteria\nbecomes whatever makes the metric look good, such as \"the model returned something\". Unit economics per\nsuccessful task exists to put cost and outcome in one ratio, measured the same way every time, so that\nmodel choices, retry policies and human-in-the-loop design can be compared on the only basis the\nbusiness cares about: what does one good outcome cost, and is it less than it is worth?","how":"### Define success first\nWrite down what counts as a successful task in terms the customer would accept: ticket resolved and\nnot reopened within a set window, extraction matching the source on all required fields, code change\npassing tests and merged. Decide how success is measured: automated checks, sampled human review, or\na downstream signal. A metric without a fixed definition drifts toward whatever is easy to count.\nDownstream signals arrive late (a ticket counts as resolved only once the reopen window has passed), so\ncompute the metric on cohorts of tasks whose window has closed, or this week's number will look better\nthan it is.\n\n### Count all the cost\nFor a period or a cohort of tasks, sum:\n- model tokens for every attempt, including failed and abandoned ones, and reasoning tokens;\n- non-model calls (retrieval, tools, guardrails, verifier or judge models);\n- human time for review, escalation and correction, at a loaded hourly rate;\n- the expected cost of undetected errors (rate of silent failure × cost per incident);\n- amortized fixed costs if the comparison is about the whole product rather than a design change.\n\n### Divide by successes\n```\ncost_per_successful_task = total_cost / tasks_meeting_acceptance\n```\nFor reasoning about designs, a simple expected-value model helps. If each attempt costs c and succeeds\nwith probability p, and every failure is reliably detected and retried, the expected AI cost per success\nis c/p. That ratio already shows why a model at 4× the price with 1.5× the success rate is not 4× worse.\nReal systems add two terms that usually dominate: the share of failures handed to humans, and the share\nof failures *not detected* at all.\n\n### Compare with value\nValue per task is what the business gains: the human handling cost avoided, revenue per conversion, or\ntime saved. Margin per task = value − fully loaded cost. Break-even success rate is the p at which that\nmargin reaches zero; it tells you how good the system must be before automation pays. For the common\ncase of a human fallback, the baseline is the all-human cost h. With one attempt at cost c, a detector\nthat catches a fraction d of failures, and a cost e per silent error, automation is cheaper when\n\n```\nc + (1 − p)·(d·h + (1 − d)·e)  <  h     →     p  >  1 − (h − c) / (d·h + (1 − d)·e)\n```\n\nWith perfect detection (d = 1) this reduces to p > c/h, which is why cheap models clear the bar\neasily when failures are always caught; as e grows and d falls, the required p climbs fast.\n\n### Caveats that change the answer\nAttempts are not independent: if a model fails on a hard input, retrying the same model often fails\nagain, so the c/p retry math is optimistic. Detection is not free or perfect: a verifier adds cost and\nmisses some failures. And the mix of tasks shifts over time, so recompute per task category, not one\nglobal number.","example":"A ticket-triage agent where a human handles anything the agent cannot (all figures illustrative): human\ncost $4.00 per ticket, a verifier costing $0.005 catches 80% of agent failures, and a failure that slips\nthrough costs $20 in rework and goodwill.\n\n```python\ndef per_task(c, p, k=1, verify=0.005, detect=0.8, human=4.00, error=20.00):\n    \"\"\"Expected fully loaded cost of one task: up to k attempts; a caught failure is retried\n    (then sent to a human after k); a missed failure costs `error`. Independence is optimistic.\"\"\"\n    cost, reach, solved, ai = 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0\n    for _ in range(k):\n        ai += reach * (c + verify)\n        cost += reach * (1 - p) * (1 - detect) * error   # silent failure reaches the customer\n        solved += reach * p\n        reach *= (1 - p) * detect                         # caught failure: try again\n    cost += ai + reach * human\n    return cost, solved, ai / solved\n\nfor name, c, p in ((\"small\", 0.02, 0.60), (\"large\", 0.08, 0.90)):\n    for k in (1, 3):\n        cost, solved, ai_per_win = per_task(c, p, k=k)\n        print(f\"{name} k={k}: ${cost:.3f}/task automated {solved:.1%} AI spend/success ${ai_per_win:.3f}\")\n# small k=1: $2.905/task automated 60.0% AI spend/success $0.042\n# small k=3: $2.442/task automated 85.3% AI spend/success $0.042\n# large k=1: $0.805/task automated 90.0% AI spend/success $0.094\n# large k=3: $0.529/task automated 97.8% AI spend/success $0.094\n```\n\nOn AI spend per success the small model looks more than twice as good. Fully loaded, it costs about\n3.6× more per task (k=1), because its silent failures and escalations dominate. Retries help only with\n*detected* failures, and every extra attempt is another chance for a failure to slip past the verifier:\nfor the small model, going from k=1 to k=3 cuts human escalation from $1.28 to about $0.13 per task but\n*raises* the silent-error term from $1.60 to about $2.28, which now dominates its bill. That is why\nimproving detection is often worth more than adding retries, or even than improving the model.\n\nBecause a human finishes every caught failure, nearly every task ends in a good outcome here, so cost\nper task is close to cost per successful task; the silent failures are the exception, charged through\nthe error term. Against the all-human baseline of $4.00, the break-even formula above gives\np > 1 − 3.975 / 7.2 ≈ 0.45 for the small model, so at 60% it does beat doing everything by hand, but\nby a thin margin that a small rise in silent-error cost would erase.","tradeoffs":["A stricter success definition makes the metric trustworthy, but lowers the headline number and costs review effort; a looser one flips into self-deception once customers start reopening tasks.","Adding a verifier costs money on every task, but pays back whenever the cost of a silent failure is much larger than the verification cost; for low-stakes tasks it can be pure overhead.","More retries raise automation rate, but each retry costs tokens and latency and helps only when failures are detected and not strongly correlated across attempts.","Including amortized fixed costs gives the true product margin, but blurs comparisons between design options; use marginal cost to choose designs and fully loaded cost to price."],"pitfalls":["Reporting cost per call as unit cost; the symptom is a \"cheaper\" model rollout followed by more escalations and a higher total support bill.","Counting only successful runs' tokens; visible as unit cost that ignores agent runs that looped and failed, so finance totals never reconcile with the metric.","Defining success as \"completed without error\"; the metric looks excellent while customer reopen rates or downstream correction volume quietly climb.","Treating the retry math as independent; real success after retries stays well below the predicted rate because the same hard inputs keep failing."],"when":"Use this metric whenever you compare models, prompts, retry policies or human-in-the-loop designs, and\nwhenever you price an AI feature or argue for its ROI. Compute it per task category and per tenant\nrather than globally, track it weekly next to success rate and escalation rate, and validate the\nsuccess definition with sampled human review. It is less useful during early prototyping, before there\nis a stable task definition. In interviews, asking \"per successful what?\" when someone quotes a cost\nnumber is the mark of someone who has run an AI product.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments each task end to end with an ID so every attempt, tool call, verifier result and escalation is attributed to one outcome.","architect":"Chooses model tier, retry budget, verifier and human-fallback design by comparing fully loaded cost per successful task against task value.","fde":"Agrees the success definition and the value per task with the customer, so the ROI case uses their numbers and survives their finance review."},"check":{"q":"Model X costs $0.01 per attempt with 70% success; model Y costs $0.05 with 92%. Failures go to a\nhuman at $3. Which is cheaper per task, and what assumption would you challenge?","a":"With one attempt and perfect detection, X costs 0.01 + 0.30 × 3 = $0.91 per task and Y costs\n0.05 + 0.08 × 3 = $0.29, so Y is about three times cheaper despite the 5× price. The human fallback\ndominates. I would challenge perfect detection: if some failures are not caught, the cost of each silent\nerror must be added, and that term usually favors the more accurate model further. I would also check\nwhether retrying X helps or whether its failures correlate on hard inputs, and compute the numbers per\nticket category."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is cost per API call a misleading unit cost for an AI feature?","options":["API calls are billed monthly rather than per call","It ignores failed attempts, retries, human fallback and undetected errors, which can dominate the cost of a good outcome","Token prices change too frequently to measure per call","It double-counts cached input tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The business pays for outcomes. A cheaper call that fails more often moves cost into retries, escalations and error handling, so cost per successful task can rise while cost per call falls."},{"q":"Each attempt costs c, succeeds with probability p, and failures are always detected and retried. What is the expected AI cost per success?","options":["c × p","c / p","c / (1 − p)","c + p"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number of attempts until success is geometric with mean 1/p, so expected cost per success is c/p. c/(1 − p) inverts the wrong probability: it is the expected cost until the first *failure*, a common mix-up."},{"q":"A verifier catches 80% of failures. Adding more retries mainly reduces which cost term?","options":["The cost of silent failures that reach the customer","The share of tasks escalated to a human after caught failures","The per-attempt token cost","The verifier's own cost per attempt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retries only act on failures the verifier detects, so they shift work from human escalation to automation. Missed failures never trigger a retry, and each extra attempt is another chance of a silent miss, so the silent-error term does not fall and can even grow; it needs better detection."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/cost-allocation-by-tenant-and-feature","sources":["Official docs: FinOps Foundation (FinOps Framework)"],"why":"A hosted model invoice arrives as one number per model per month. A self-hosted GPU fleet costs the\nsame per hour whether one tenant or fifty used it. Meanwhile the product has pricing tiers, enterprise\ncontracts and a roadmap of features, each with a very different cost profile. A tenant who uploads\n500-page contracts and runs long agent sessions can cost a hundred times more than one asking short\nquestions on the same plan. Without allocation, that tenant looks as profitable as everyone else until\ngross margin turns negative.\n\nThe naive approaches fail predictably. Splitting spend evenly across tenants or by seat count\nsubsidizes heavy users with light ones and hides the problem. Allocating only the direct API cost\nignores shared infrastructure, which for self-hosted or RAG-heavy systems can be most of the spend.\nReconstructing attribution later from logs fails because the tenant and feature were never recorded at\ncall time, and retrofitting the tags across many services is slow. Allocation exists to answer, with\nnumbers that reconcile to the invoice: what does each tenant cost us, what does each feature cost, and\nwhere is the margin?","how":"### 1. Tag at the point of spend\nRoute all model, embedding and reranker calls through one gateway or client library that requires\n`tenant_id`, `feature` (or workflow) and a task or trace ID on every call, and rejects or flags calls\nmissing them. Record per call: model, tokens by type (fresh input, cached input, output, reasoning),\nlatency, and for self-hosted models the GPU-seconds or token-seconds consumed. Propagate the tags\nthrough agents, tools and background jobs as trace attributes, *because* the call that spends money is\noften several hops away from the request that caused it.\n\n### 2. Price direct costs per call\nCompute cost at write time from a versioned price table, so historical rows keep the price that applied\nthen. Apply the cache, batch and long-context rules your provider actually bills. Where possible, use\nseparate provider API keys or projects per environment or major feature so the invoice can be split at\nthe source as a cross-check.\n\n### 3. Allocate shared costs with explicit drivers\nShared pools need a driver that approximates causation:\n- **GPU fleet**: GPU-seconds or token-seconds per request; concurrent batching makes exact\n  attribution impossible, so tokens weighted by phase (prefill vs decode) is a common approximation.\n  For long-context traffic, KV-cache occupancy (context tokens × seconds resident) is often the fairer\n  driver, because memory rather than compute is what limits how many other requests fit alongside it:\n  a 100k-token session that decodes slowly crowds out many short ones while using little arithmetic.\n- **Shared prompt-cache prefixes**: the first request pays the cache write and later ones get the cheap\n  read. Charging each call its literal billed price rewards whoever arrives second; for a prefix shared\n  across tenants (a common system prompt), pricing every call at the blended rate is usually fairer.\n- **Commitments and discounts**: reserved capacity or committed-spend discounts should be allocated at\n  the effective (amortized) rate, not list price, and an unused commitment is idle capacity under\n  another name.\n- **Vector store**: stored vectors for storage-bound costs, query count for compute-bound costs.\n- **Embedding re-indexing**: to the tenant whose data was re-embedded.\n- **Idle and headroom capacity**: either spread proportionally to usage, or kept as a visible\n  platform-overhead line. Spreading makes tenant totals add up; keeping it separate shows the cost of\n  over-provisioning instead of hiding it.\n\nWrite the driver choice down; it is a policy decision, and changing it changes everyone's numbers.\nThe same goes for platform-caused waste: retries after a provider 5xx or a bad deploy are usually\nbetter charged to platform overhead than to the tenant who happened to be calling.\n\n### 4. Reconcile and track the unallocated share\nEach month, allocations must sum to the invoices. Track the unattributed share (untagged calls, shared\njobs) as a KPI and drive it down, because a large unattributed bucket makes every per-tenant number\nsuspect.\n\n### 5. Use it: showback, chargeback, pricing\n**Showback** reports cost per tenant and feature to teams and account owners; **chargeback** actually\nmoves the cost onto internal teams' budgets. Passing cost on to external customers is a separate\npricing decision (usage-based tiers, overages) that this data informs. Most products start with showback, then use the data to set tier\nlimits, overage pricing, per-tenant quotas, and to flag tenants whose cost exceeds their revenue.","example":"A month of usage: hosted API calls priced at the gateway, plus a shared GPU fleet and vector store\nbilled as lump sums (PostgreSQL; numeric columns).\n\n```sql\nWITH usage AS (\n  SELECT COALESCE(tenant_id, 'UNATTRIBUTED') AS tenant_id,\n         COALESCE(feature,   'UNATTRIBUTED') AS feature,\n         COALESCE(SUM(api_cost_usd), 0)::numeric AS direct_usd,  -- priced per call at the gateway\n         SUM(gpu_seconds)::numeric       AS gpu_s,       -- self-hosted calls\n         SUM(retrieval_queries)::numeric AS rq\n  FROM usage_events\n  WHERE ts >= DATE '2026-09-01' AND ts < DATE '2026-10-01'\n  GROUP BY 1, 2\n),\npools AS (\n  SELECT SUM(amount_usd) FILTER (WHERE pool = 'gpu_fleet')    AS gpu_usd,\n         SUM(amount_usd) FILTER (WHERE pool = 'vector_store') AS vec_usd\n  FROM shared_invoices WHERE month = DATE '2026-09-01'\n),\nalloc AS (\n  SELECT u.tenant_id, u.feature, u.direct_usd,\n         COALESCE(p.gpu_usd * u.gpu_s / NULLIF(SUM(u.gpu_s) OVER (), 0), 0) AS gpu_usd,\n         COALESCE(p.vec_usd * u.rq / NULLIF(SUM(u.rq) OVER (), 0), 0)       AS vector_usd\n  FROM usage u CROSS JOIN pools p\n)\nSELECT tenant_id, feature,\n       ROUND(direct_usd, 2) AS direct, ROUND(gpu_usd, 2) AS gpu, ROUND(vector_usd, 2) AS vector,\n       ROUND(direct_usd + gpu_usd + vector_usd, 2) AS total\nFROM alloc\nORDER BY total DESC;\n```\n\nIllustrative result: a $30,000 GPU fleet where tenant A's contract-review feature used 45% of\nGPU-seconds gets $13,500 of it; added to $6,200 of direct API spend, A's total is about $19,700 against a\n$12,000 monthly contract. The even-split view would have shown A as profitable. The `UNATTRIBUTED` row\nshould be small; if it is 15% of spend, fix tagging before trusting any tenant number.","tradeoffs":["Fine-grained drivers (GPU-seconds by phase, per-query vector cost) are fairer, but cost engineering effort; coarse token-proportional splits suffice while shared costs are a small share of spend.","Spreading idle capacity across tenants makes totals reconcile, but hides over-provisioning; keep it separate when the platform team needs to see and own utilization.","Chargeback changes behavior because internal teams pay from their own budget, but disputes over drivers follow; showback is the better start until the numbers are trusted.","Per-tenant API keys or dedicated pools make attribution exact, but lose pooling efficiency and add quota management; worth it for large enterprise tenants, not for a long tail of small ones."],"pitfalls":["Tagging only the user-facing call; the symptom is a large unattributed bucket made of agent tool calls, background re-embeddings and retries that lost their tenant context.","Repricing history with today's price table; visible as last quarter's per-tenant costs changing after a provider price update.","Allocating shared GPU cost by request count; long-document tenants look cheap while short-query tenants are overcharged, and cost per tenant does not track their token volume.","Never reconciling with invoices; per-tenant totals drift away from actual spend and finance stops trusting the dashboard."],"when":"Set up tagging before the first multi-tenant launch; it is cheap at the start and painful to retrofit.\nFormal allocation of shared pools becomes worthwhile once shared infrastructure is a material part of\nspend or pricing decisions depend on per-tenant cost. Measure unattributed share, cost per tenant\nagainst revenue per tenant, cost per feature per active user, and cost per successful task by tenant.\nIn interviews, explaining how to attribute shared GPU cost under batching, and why idle capacity is a\npolicy choice, shows depth beyond \"add a tenant label\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enforces required tenant and feature tags in the gateway or client, propagates them through traces and jobs, and computes per-call cost from a versioned price table.","architect":"Chooses allocation drivers, decides between shared and dedicated pools per tier, and makes per-tenant cost an input to pricing, quotas and isolation design.","fde":"Gives a customer a defensible view of what their usage costs, and spots integration patterns such as oversized uploads that will push them past their contract."},"check":{"q":"Your platform shares one self-hosted model fleet across 40 tenants with continuous batching. How\ndo you allocate the fleet cost, and what do you do with idle capacity?","a":"Exact per-request GPU time is not observable under batching, so I would use a driver that\napproximates causation: tokens per request weighted by phase, since prefill and decode cost differently,\nor measured token-seconds from the engine, recorded per call with tenant and feature tags. Fleet cost is\nsplit proportionally to that driver. Idle and headroom capacity is a policy choice: spread it\nproportionally so tenant totals reconcile to the invoice, or report it as platform overhead so\nutilization stays visible. I would document the choice, reconcile monthly, and track the unattributed\nshare."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should tenant and feature tags be attached at the gateway on every model call rather than reconstructed later?","options":["Providers require tenant IDs in every API request","The call that spends money is often a tool or background step several hops away, and its tenant context is lost if not propagated at call time","Tags reduce token usage by shortening prompts","Logs cannot store more than one identifier per request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Agents, retries and background jobs issue calls far from the originating request. If context is not propagated and recorded when the call is made, the spend ends up unattributed."},{"q":"Allocating a shared GPU fleet by number of requests per tenant tends to do what?","options":["Allocate cost accurately, since every request uses the same GPU time","Undercharge tenants with long documents and overcharge tenants with many short requests","Overcharge tenants who use prompt caching","Make allocations fail to sum to the invoice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPU time scales with tokens processed, not request count. A request driver ignores size, so heavy long-context tenants are subsidized by light ones. Totals still reconcile; only fairness suffers."},{"q":"What is the main argument for reporting idle GPU capacity as a separate platform line rather than spreading it?","options":["It makes per-tenant totals sum exactly to the invoice","Tenants are not allowed to be billed for idle capacity","It keeps the cost of over-provisioning visible to the team that controls utilization","It removes the need for an allocation driver"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Spreading idle cost hides it inside every tenant's number. A separate line makes utilization a visible, owned cost; the trade-off is that tenant totals then do not add up to the invoice by themselves."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/quality-latency-cost-optimization","sources":["Chen et al., 2023 — FrugalGPT: How to Use Large Language Models While Reducing Cost and Improving Performance"],"why":"The usual history: a prototype is built on the largest model with a long, example-stuffed prompt. It works,\nit ships, and then three things happen at once. The bill grows linearly with traffic; p95 latency of several\nseconds makes the interface feel broken; and when someone finally runs an eval, the \"best\" model turns out\nto be no better on this task than one an order of magnitude cheaper, because the task saturates.\n\nThe naive strategies each fail on one axis. \"Use the best model for everything\" ignores that quality is\ntask-relative and plateaus, while cost and latency are paid on every single request. \"Use the cheapest\nmodel\" ignores that a wrong answer has a cost too: a retry, a human escalation, a lost customer. The right\ndenominator is cost per *successful* task, and on that measure the cheap model can be the expensive one.\n\"Tune one axis at a time\" is the most common failure: a team trims the prompt to cut cost, quality quietly\ndrops, and nobody notices until a customer does, because quality was never a number on a dashboard.\n\nThe axes are coupled by mechanism, not by accident. More reasoning tokens means better answers on hard\nproblems and more decode time and spend. More retrieved context means more grounding and more prefill. A\nbigger model means more capability and more memory bandwidth per token. You therefore need a method that\nholds all three in view, makes the exchange rate between them explicit, and is re-run whenever a model,\nprice or prompt changes.","how":"### Make all three measurable\nNothing below works without numbers for each axis on the *same* set of requests.\n\n- **Quality** is a task-specific eval: a golden set with a pass criterion (exact match for classification,\n  a rubric or LLM judge for open text, human grading for the ambiguous slice). It is defined first\n  *because* every cost or latency cut is a bet that quality survives, and bets need a scoreboard.\n- **Latency** must be the latency the user feels: time-to-first-token for streamed chat, end-to-end time\n  for a classifier or a tool call, and always a percentile (p95 or p99), not a mean. Means hide the tail,\n  and the tail is what users remember.\n- **Cost** is cost per successful task, including retries, fallbacks, judge or verifier calls, cache\n  misses and the downstream cost of failures. Per-request cost is what the provider bills; per-successful\n  task is what the business pays.\n\n### Where latency and cost actually come from\nBefore pulling levers, decompose each axis, *because* the decomposition tells you which lever can move it:\n\n```\ncost_per_call ≈ n_in_uncached·p_in + n_in_cached·p_cached + n_out·p_out\nTTFT          ≈ network + queueing + prefill(n_in)\nend_to_end    ≈ TTFT + n_out · TPOT          (TPOT = time per output token)\n```\n\nPrefill processes the whole prompt in parallel, so input tokens are cheap in time; decode emits output\ntokens one at a time, so `n_out · TPOT` usually dominates end-to-end latency. Output tokens are also\ntypically priced several times higher than input tokens. With an illustrative TPOT of 20 ms, a\n300-token answer spends about 6 s in decode alone, whatever the prompt length; cutting the answer to\n100 tokens saves about 4 s, while halving a 2,000-token prompt saves a fraction of a second of prefill.\nThis is why output caps and terse formats are often the strongest latency lever, and why prompt trimming\nmainly buys cost and TTFT. Agents and chains multiply all of it by the number of sequential calls.\n\n### Cost per success, and when quality *is* cost\nIf each attempt succeeds with probability *q* and a verifier lets you retry until success, expected\nattempts are `1/q`, so `cost_per_success = (c_call + c_verify) / q`. That assumes independent attempts; in practice the\nsame hard input tends to fail again, so retries recover less than `1/q` predicts. If failures instead go to a human,\n`cost_per_task = c_call + (1 − q) · c_human`. Illustratively, a $0.001 model at 86% with a $2 human\nescalation costs 0.001 + 0.14 × 2 ≈ $0.28 per task; a $0.018 model at 94% costs 0.018 + 0.06 × 2 ≈ $0.14.\nWhen failure is expensive, the quality axis collapses into the cost axis and the \"expensive\" model is the\ncheap one. When failure is cheap (a user rephrases), the axes stay separate and the frontier below matters.\n\n### Know which axis each lever moves\n| Lever | Quality | Latency | Cost | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bigger model tier | up | up | up | coarsest lever; quality gain saturates per task |\n| Shorter prompt / less context | risk | down (prefill) | down | every token is paid on every call |\n| Capped or terser output | neutral if instructed | down (decode) | down | decode is sequential, so output length often dominates total time |\n| Reasoning / thinking budget | up on hard tasks | up a lot | up a lot | wasted on easy tasks; gate by difficulty |\n| Prompt caching (shared prefix) | none | down (TTFT) | down (input) | removes work instead of trading it |\n| Response / semantic cache | none on hits | near zero on hits | near zero on hits | only for repeat queries; staleness risk |\n| Routing / cascade | ≈ large model | up for escalated requests | down on average | depends on escalation rate |\n| Batch / async endpoints | none | up (minutes to hours) | down sharply | for non-interactive work only |\n| Fine-tune / distill small model | up for small model | down | down | front-loaded effort; shifts the frontier |\n| Quantization, speculative decoding | small risk | down | down | serving-side; measure per model |\n| Streaming | none | perceived down | none | changes perception, not the number |\n\nTwo groups matter. Most levers move you *along* the frontier: you give up some of one axis for another.\nA few, caching, distillation, fine-tuning and task redesign, *shift* the frontier, because they remove work\nor make a cheaper model genuinely better at this task. When no point on the current frontier satisfies the\nconstraints, only the second group can help; more tuning of the first group cannot.\n\n### Build the frontier\nRun every candidate configuration against the same eval set and a replay of real traffic, and record the\ntriple (quality, p95 latency, cost per successful task). Configuration A is *dominated* if some B is at\nleast as good on all three and strictly better on one; discard the dominated ones. What remains is the\nPareto frontier.\n\nRespect measurement noise when declaring dominance. A pass rate on *n* items has a standard error of about\n`√(q(1−q)/n)`; at q = 0.92 and n = 200 that is roughly 2 points, so 0.92 and 0.93 are a statistical tie.\nTreat configurations within the noise band as equal on quality (compare them on the same items with a\npaired test, or grow the eval) and let latency and cost decide. Latency percentiles need the same care:\na p95 from a few hundred requests is itself noisy.\n\nThe point of the exercise is that it turns a three-way argument into a short list and makes\nthe exchange rate visible: \"moving from B to C buys two points of quality for $0.003 per task and 450 ms\nof p95\". People can argue about whether that trade is worth it; they can no longer argue about facts.\n\n### Choose under constraints\nIn practice two axes are constraints and one is the objective. Either *maximize quality subject to\np95 ≤ L and cost ≤ C*, or *minimize cost subject to quality ≥ Q and p95 ≤ L*. The quality floor is nearly\nalways a product requirement rather than a tunable, so the second form is the common one. Pick the\nfeasible frontier point with the best objective. If the feasible set is empty, stop tuning and reach for a\nfrontier-shifting lever.\n\n### Do it per task, not per application\nA single application contains features with different frontiers. Intent classification saturates on a\nsmall model; multi-document synthesis does not. Splitting traffic by task and optimizing each separately is\nusually the single largest win, which is why routing comes before most other work.\n\n### Keep measuring\nQuality drifts when the provider updates a model or the input mix changes; prices fall; traffic mix\nshifts; a prompt edit makes the model chattier. Re-run the frontier on a cadence and on every change to\nprompt, model or retrieval, and alert on cost per task and p95 in production, not just on total spend.","example":"A small, runnable version of the frontier step. The measurements are illustrative numbers for a\nsupport-ticket triage task, not benchmarks; the structure is what matters.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    name: str\n    quality: float        # pass rate on the task's golden set, 0..1\n    p95_ms: int           # end-to-end p95 on a traffic replay\n    cost_per_task: float  # USD per successful task, retries and judge calls included\n\nconfigs = [\n    Config(\"large, long prompt\",     0.94, 3800, 0.0180),\n    Config(\"large, trimmed prompt\",  0.93, 2900, 0.0110),\n    Config(\"small, trimmed prompt\",  0.86,  900, 0.0012),\n    Config(\"small, fine-tuned\",      0.92,  950, 0.0014),\n    Config(\"small -> large cascade\", 0.93, 1400, 0.0038),\n    Config(\"medium, trimmed prompt\", 0.90, 1700, 0.0045),\n]\n\ndef dominated(c, others):  # B dominates A: no worse on all three, strictly better on one\n    return any(o.quality >= c.quality and o.p95_ms <= c.p95_ms\n               and o.cost_per_task <= c.cost_per_task\n               and (o.quality > c.quality or o.p95_ms < c.p95_ms\n                    or o.cost_per_task < c.cost_per_task) for o in others)\n\nfrontier = [c for c in configs if not dominated(c, configs)]\nfeasible = [c for c in frontier if c.quality >= 0.90 and c.p95_ms <= 2000]\nbest = min(feasible, key=lambda c: c.cost_per_task)\nfor c in frontier:\n    print(f\"{c.name:<24} q={c.quality:.2f}  p95={c.p95_ms:>5} ms  ${c.cost_per_task:.4f}/task\")\nprint(\"choose:\", best.name)\n# large, long prompt       q=0.94  p95= 3800 ms  $0.0180/task\n# small, trimmed prompt    q=0.86  p95=  900 ms  $0.0012/task\n# small, fine-tuned        q=0.92  p95=  950 ms  $0.0014/task\n# small -> large cascade   q=0.93  p95= 1400 ms  $0.0038/task\n# choose: small, fine-tuned\n```\n\nTwo configurations vanish immediately: the trimmed large model is dominated by the cascade (same quality,\nfaster, cheaper), and the medium model is dominated by the fine-tuned small one. Under the constraints\n(quality ≥ 0.90, p95 ≤ 2 s) only the fine-tuned small model and the cascade survive, and the fine-tuned\nmodel wins on cost. Note what the table does not show: the one-off cost of producing the fine-tuned model,\nwhich must be amortized over expected volume before the choice is final, and the fact that the cascade's\np95 is driven by the escalated minority, so its p50 is close to the small model's. And the quality gap\nbetween the fine-tuned model (0.92) and the cascade (0.93) is inside the noise of a few-hundred-item eval,\nwhich is exactly why cost, not quality, should break the tie here.","tradeoffs":["A cascade cuts average cost but raises tail latency and adds a confidence gate to maintain; with small-model cost *s*, large-model cost *l* and escalation rate *e*, it beats the large model alone only while `s + e·l < l`, i.e. while `e < 1 − s/l`. At a 10× price gap that is 90%; at 2× it is only 50%.","Trimming context and few-shot examples lowers cost and TTFT on every call, but each removed token is a bet that it was not needed; the trade flips the moment the eval shows quality under the floor.","Thinking or reasoning budgets buy real quality on hard inputs and pure waste on easy ones; the trade is favourable only when gated by task difficulty rather than applied uniformly.","Fine-tuning or distilling a small model shifts the whole frontier but front-loads engineering and must be repeated when the task or base model changes; it pays only when monthly savings exceed the amortized effort, which is a volume threshold.","Response caching gives near-zero cost and latency on hits, but is worthless on long-tail inputs and harmful when answers must reflect fresh data; the trade flips with hit rate and staleness tolerance.","Batch endpoints can cut cost substantially for offline work at the price of minutes-to-hours latency; the trade flips the instant a user is waiting on the result."],"pitfalls":["Optimizing cost with no quality metric in place; the symptom is a falling bill followed weeks later by rising escalation and complaint rates that nobody connects to the change.","Reporting mean latency; the symptom is a green dashboard while users report hangs, because the p95 nobody plotted is three times the mean.","Comparing cost per request instead of per successful task; the symptom is a \"cheaper\" model whose retries, judge calls and human review make the total higher.","Evaluating on a handful of hand-picked prompts; the symptom is a configuration that shines in the demo and degrades on the real traffic distribution.","Reading a one- or two-point eval difference as a real win; the symptom is the \"winning\" configuration swapping places with the runner-up every time the eval is re-run or re-sampled.","Leaving output length unconstrained; the symptom is cost and decode latency creeping up after a prompt or model change made the model chattier.","Treating the frontier as static; the symptom is a once-optimal setup that became dominated after a provider price cut or model update that nobody re-tested."],"when":"Run the full frontier exercise when a feature first approaches production scale, whenever a new model or\nprice list appears, and whenever quality, p95 or cost per task moves outside its band in monitoring. Skip\nit for throwaway prototypes and low-volume internal tools, where the cost of measuring exceeds the cost of\nbeing wrong, but still write down the quality floor so the floor exists when volume arrives. The day-to-day\nform is a dashboard with three lines per feature (quality on a sampled eval, p95, cost per successful task)\nand a habit of re-running the eval on every prompt, model or retrieval change. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is to name all three axes unprompted, insist on cost per successful task, separate frontier-moving\nlevers from frontier-shifting ones, and derive the cascade break-even condition from the escalation rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the eval harness, the traffic replay and the per-config measurement; implements routing, caching and output caps; keeps max_tokens, prompt versions and model ids under version control.","architect":"Sets the quality floor and latency budget per feature, decides which levers are allowed (cascade, distillation, batch paths) and signs off the frontier point the product will run on.","fde":"Rebuilds the frontier inside the customer's constraints, where the eval set is their data, the latency budget is their workflow and the cost ceiling is their contract margin, then defends the choice."},"check":{"q":"A feature runs on a large model at p95 of 4 s, $0.02 per task and 94% on its eval. Product wants\np95 under 2 s and cost under $0.005 per task while keeping quality at 90% or better. Which levers do you\ntry, in what order, and how do you know when you are done?","a":"Start with the levers that change the least: trim the prompt, cap and tighten the output, add\nprompt caching for the shared prefix, and re-measure all three numbers on the full eval and a traffic\nreplay. If that does not clear both constraints, route: a small model first with a confidence or verifier\ngate, escalating to the large model, and compute expected cost as small cost plus escalation rate times\nlarge cost, while checking that the escalated requests do not blow the p95. If escalation stays high, the\nfrontier itself must move: distill the large model's outputs into a fine-tuned small model and amortize\nthat effort against monthly savings at the expected volume. Done means a configuration on the frontier\nsatisfies all three constraints on the eval and the replay, with production monitoring of p95 and cost per\nsuccessful task so a regression is caught in days rather than at the next invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which lever shifts the quality–latency–cost frontier rather than moving along it?","options":["Switching to a larger model tier","Distilling the large model's behaviour into a fine-tuned small model","Raising max_tokens so answers are more complete","Adding more few-shot examples to the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Model tier, output length and prompt length trade one axis for another and stay on the existing frontier. Distillation makes a cheap, fast model genuinely better at this task, so it improves quality without paying in latency or cost: the frontier itself moves."},{"q":"Why is cost per successful task the right cost measure rather than cost per request?","options":["Because providers bill per task rather than per token","Because it includes retries, fallbacks, judge calls and the cost of failed outputs, which per-request cost hides","Because it is easier to compute from the invoice","Because latency is also measured per task"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cheap model that fails more often incurs retries, verifier calls and human handling; per-request cost makes it look like a win while the business pays more. Per successful task counts what the outcome actually cost."},{"q":"A small model costs $0.001 per task and a large one $0.010. Below what escalation rate does a small-first cascade beat sending everything to the large model?","options":["10%","50%","90%","It always beats it, at any escalation rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cascade cost is 0.001 + e × 0.010, which is below 0.010 only while e < 0.9. Above that, nearly every request pays for both models and the cascade is strictly worse on cost and on tail latency."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/break-even-analysis","sources":[],"why":"The question arrives with the first five-figure API invoice: \"would it not be cheaper to run this\nourselves?\" The back-of-envelope answer takes a GPU's hourly price, divides by its benchmark tokens per\nsecond times 3,600, gets a cost per million tokens far below the API's list price, and concludes that\nself-hosting is several times cheaper. That calculation is wrong in three independent ways.\n\nFirst, it assumes the GPU is saturated 24 hours a day. Real interactive traffic is diurnal and bursty; you\nprovision for the peak with headroom to protect latency, so the average utilization of what you bought is a\nfraction, and the effective cost per token is the list cost divided by that fraction. Second, the GPU is the\nvisible line, not the biggest one: someone has to build and run the serving stack, carry the pager, upgrade\nthe model and prove on your evals that an open-weight model matches the hosted one. Third, it compares\ndifferent things: a hosted frontier model against an open-weight model, at different quality.\n\nThe opposite reflex, \"never self-host, it is a distraction\", is equally lazy. At sustained high volume the\nfixed cost amortizes until the marginal token is nearly free, and non-cost drivers such as data residency,\nrate limits during bursts, latency control or a model the vendor does not offer can decide the question on\ntheir own. The break-even analysis exists to replace both reflexes with a curve and a sensitivity band.","how":"### Two cost curves\nWrite monthly cost as a function of monthly volume *V* in tokens.\n\n```\nCost_api(V)  = p_in · V_in + p_out · V_out          ≈ p · V   for a stable input/output mix\nCost_self(V) = F + N(V) · G\n```\n\n`p` is the blended price per token at *your* mix (output tokens usually cost several times input tokens,\nso blend per feature, not per company). `F` is the fixed monthly cost that does not scale with volume:\npeople, platform tooling, eval upkeep. `G` is the monthly cost of one GPU replica. `N(V)` is the number of\nreplicas needed to serve *V*, and it is a step function because you cannot buy a third of a GPU.\n\n### Replica count comes from the peak, not the average\n```\navg_rate  = V / seconds_per_month\npeak_rate = avg_rate · peak_to_average · (1 + headroom)\nN(V)      = ceil(peak_rate / T_slo)\n```\n\n`T_slo` is the sustained tokens per second one replica delivers *at your latency target*, measured on your\nsequence lengths, not the throughput at maximum batch. The distinction matters because continuous batching\ntrades per-request latency for aggregate throughput: the batch size that maximizes tokens per second gives a\np95 nobody would ship. Measure it for your request *shape* (typical input and output lengths), not as a\ngeneric tokens-per-second figure, *because* a prompt token is processed in parallel during prefill and\ncosts far less GPU time than an output token generated one at a time in decode. An API's price ratio\nbetween input and output tokens is not your GPU's cost ratio, so blend both sides on the same mix. The\npeak-to-average ratio comes from your traffic logs; headroom covers failover and\nthe fact that a replica at 100% load is already past its latency target.\n\nThe achieved utilization is `u = avg_rate / (N · T_slo)`, roughly `1 / (peak_to_average · (1 + headroom))`\nonce N is large. Interactive traffic with a peak-to-average of 2–3 and 20% headroom lands around a third.\nBatch or offline workloads can be scheduled flat and push *u* toward one, which is why self-hosting pays off\nfar earlier for them. Utilization is the single most important number in the whole analysis.\n\n### Effective cost per token and the sign test\n```\nc_self = G / (T_slo · seconds_per_month · u)\n```\n\nCompare `c_self` to `p` before anything else. If `c_self > p` at the utilization you can realistically\nachieve, there is no break-even at any volume: fixed costs only widen the gap. If `c_self < p`, the\ncontinuous approximation of the crossover is `V* ≈ F / (p − c_self)`, and the step function makes the real\ncrossover slightly later. The sign test is what an API price cut attacks: halve `p` and a workload that was\ncomfortably past break-even can be on the wrong side of it permanently.\n\n### Payback on the switch\nMoving is itself a one-off cost `M`: building the serving path, evaluating and possibly fine-tuning the\nopen-weight model, load testing, migrating traffic. Even past break-even, the switch pays back only after\n`M / (Cost_api − Cost_self)` months. Put that next to the reservation term and the expected life of the\nmodel choice: a 14-month payback on a decision you expect to revisit within a year is a loss.\n\n### What goes into G and F\n`G` is the accelerator plus its share of the node (CPU, memory, local disk, network), ingress and load\nbalancing, and the difference between on-demand and one- or three-year reserved pricing, which is large.\nOn-prem adds amortized hardware, power, cooling, space and replacement of failed parts. `F` is the fraction\nof platform engineers and SREs, the on-call rotation, serving-stack upgrades, security and compliance work,\nobservability tooling, and the recurring eval and fine-tuning work needed to keep quality parity each time\na new open-weight model or a new hosted model appears. `F` is where most \"it would be cheaper\" spreadsheets\ngo wrong, because it is the line nobody wants to write down.\n\n### Quality parity is a cost, not an assumption\nIf the candidate open-weight model scores below the hosted one on your evals, you either pay to close the\ngap (fine-tuning, which adds a one-off cost and a recurring maintenance line to `F`) or you accept lower\nquality and price that through cost per successful task. Either way, compare the two options at equal\nquality or convert the quality gap into money; comparing at unequal quality is comparing nothing.\n\n### Sensitivity, not a point\nThe honest output is a band. Vary utilization between the batch-flat and interactive-bursty cases, vary\n`p` across list and committed-use pricing and a plausible future price cut, and vary `G` between\non-demand and reserved. Time matters too: a reservation locks `G` for its term, while hosted prices have\ntended to fall and hosted models to improve, so a decision that is marginal today is a bet on the next\ntwelve months of price movement.\n\n### Non-cost factors that can decide it alone\nRegulated data that cannot leave a region or a building; rate limits and quotas that cap bursts during\nyour peak; latency control through co-location; a specific open-weight or custom fine-tuned model the\nvendor does not serve; deprecation and lock-in risk. Any one of these can settle the question regardless\nof cost, but run the arithmetic anyway so the premium being paid for the constraint is known and owned.\n\n### Separate the model choice from the operating model\n\"Hosted API versus self-hosting\" silently bundles two decisions: which model (proprietary or open-weight)\nand who operates it. They can be unbundled. Several providers serve open-weight models per token, and some\nvendors sell dedicated or provisioned capacity for a fixed fee. A per-token open-weight endpoint gives you\nthe open model's price and portability with no `F` for serving; provisioned capacity gives you fixed cost\nand guaranteed throughput without running the stack. Price these as separate rows, *because* the\nself-hosting case often rests on the model being cheaper, not on operating it yourself, and a managed\nendpoint captures that saving without the fixed costs.\n\n### The usual answer is hybrid\nSelf-host the predictable base load, where utilization is high and fixed cost amortizes well, and send\nbursts and rare high-complexity requests to the API, which prices variable demand well. Or split by task:\nhigh-volume simple tasks on a self-hosted small model, frontier-quality tasks on the API. The hybrid wins\nbecause it puts each cost structure on the traffic shape it is built for, at the price of running two\nserving paths and two quality baselines.","example":"A runnable version of the model above, with every number illustrative. The output shows the curves crossing\nand, more importantly, how the crossover moves when one assumption changes.\n\n```python\nimport math\nSEC_MONTH = 30 * 24 * 3600\n\ndef api_cost(tokens_m, price_per_m):\n    return tokens_m * price_per_m\n\ndef self_host_cost(tokens_m, gpu_month, tok_per_s_at_slo, peak_to_avg, headroom, fixed):\n    avg_rate = tokens_m * 1e6 / SEC_MONTH                 # tokens/s averaged over the month\n    peak_rate = avg_rate * peak_to_avg * (1 + headroom)   # what you must provision for\n    replicas = max(1, math.ceil(peak_rate / tok_per_s_at_slo))  # cannot buy 0.3 of a GPU\n    return fixed + replicas * gpu_month, replicas\n\n# Illustrative: blended API price $2.00/M tokens; $3,000 per GPU replica-month (reserved);\n# 2,000 tok/s per replica at the latency target; $16,000/month of people and tooling.\nfor label, price, p2a in ((\"interactive\", 2.00, 2.5), (\"flat batch\", 2.00, 1.2), (\"API cut\", 1.00, 2.5)):\n    print(label)\n    for v in (5_000, 20_000, 50_000, 100_000):            # million tokens per month\n        api = api_cost(v, price)\n        sh, n = self_host_cost(v, 3_000, 2_000, p2a, 0.2, 16_000)\n        print(f\"  {v:>7}M  api=${api:>8,.0f}  self=${sh:>8,.0f} ({n:>2} GPUs)  {'self' if sh < api else 'api'} wins\")\n# interactive:  5,000M api=$10,000 self=$25,000 (3)  20,000M api=$40,000 self=$52,000 (12)\n#               50,000M api=$100,000 self=$103,000 (29)  100,000M api=$200,000 self=$190,000 (58)\n# flat batch:   20,000M api=$40,000 self=$34,000 (6) -> self already wins; 50,000M self=$58,000\n# API cut:      100,000M api=$100,000 self=$190,000 (58) -> api wins at every volume shown\n```\n\nRead the three blocks together, starting with the sign test. One replica serves\n2,000 × 2,592,000 ≈ 5.2 billion tokens a month at full load, so `c_self` = $3,000 / 5,184M ≈ $0.58 per\nmillion at u = 1. Interactive traffic gives u ≈ 1 / (2.5 × 1.2) = 1/3, so `c_self` ≈ $1.74, below the $2\nprice but only just: `V* ≈ 16,000 / (2.00 − 1.74)` ≈ 62 billion tokens a month. That matches the table:\nself-hosting does not win until somewhere past 50 billion tokens a month, an API bill around $100,000. Make the load flat (u ≈ 0.69, `c_self` ≈ $0.83) and the same GPUs serve\nroughly twice the tokens, so the crossover drops to about 14 billion. Halve the API price and the sign test\nfails: $1.74 is above $1.00, and no volume rescues it. The GPU price\nnever changed across the three runs; utilization and the competitor's price did all the work.","tradeoffs":["Self-hosting converts variable spend into fixed commitments; it wins when load is high and flat and loses when load is small or spiky, and the flip is governed by achievable utilization, not by GPU price.","Reserved pricing cuts `G` substantially but locks a term; if traffic falls or a better hosted model appears you keep paying for idle capacity, so it is only worth it with confidence in a year or more of demand.","The hosted API delivers frontier quality with zero platform work, but prices, quotas, regions and deprecations are the vendor's decisions; the trade flips when a quota or residency rule blocks you outright.","Hybrid serving captures the best cost structure for each traffic shape but doubles the surface area: two serving paths, two eval baselines and a routing layer that must keep them consistent.","Matching hosted quality with an open-weight model often needs fine-tuning; that cost is front-loaded and recurs with each base-model generation, which favours the API when model turnover is fast."],"pitfalls":["Using benchmark peak throughput for capacity; the symptom is a plan for 20 GPUs that becomes 60 once p95 latency targets are enforced in load tests.","Omitting people and platform cost; the symptom is a GPU bill below the API bill and a total cost above it, discovered when the headcount request lands.","Comparing against API list price when committed-use discounts exist; the symptom is a break-even computed against a price nobody at your volume actually pays.","Treating the crossover as permanent; the symptom is a self-hosting decision that looked right at one price per million tokens and looks wrong after the next provider price cut.","Ignoring the input/output mix; the symptom is decode-heavy traffic achieving far less than the measured throughput, because decode is bandwidth-bound while the benchmark was prefill-heavy.","Framing it as \"frontier API versus our own GPUs\" and skipping managed open-weight endpoints; the symptom is a self-hosting project whose whole saving came from the cheaper model, which a per-token endpoint would have delivered without the platform team.","Arguing cost when the real driver is residency or quotas; the symptom is weeks of spreadsheet debate while the compliance blocker that actually decides the question goes unaddressed."],"when":"Run the analysis when monthly API spend becomes material, a useful threshold being when it approaches the\nfully loaded cost of one engineer, and again whenever a non-cost driver appears: a residency requirement, a\nquota ceiling during peaks, a vendor deprecation notice. Collect the inputs before opening a spreadsheet:\ntokens per month by feature with the input/output split, the peak-to-average ratio from logs, the latency\ntarget, throughput per replica at that target measured on a candidate open-weight model with your sequence\nlengths, the quality delta on your evals, reserved GPU quotes, and a realistic people plan. The output is a\nbreak-even band under sensitivity and a recommendation that names the premium being paid for whichever\nside is chosen. In interviews, the structural answer is expected: fixed versus variable cost, the\nutilization-driven effective cost per token, the sign test, and the hybrid shape.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures throughput at the latency target on real sequence lengths, extracts the peak-to-average ratio from traffic logs, and keeps the calculator honest with production numbers.","architect":"Owns the decision and its band: sets the utilization assumption, the reservation term, the quality-parity requirement and the hybrid split, and records the non-cost drivers that override the arithmetic.","fde":"Rebuilds the break-even with the customer's volumes, their cloud pricing and their compliance constraints, and explains why their \"GPUs are cheaper\" slide omitted the people and utilization lines."},"check":{"q":"Your API bill is $40,000 a month at a blended $2 per million tokens, so 20 billion tokens a month.\nAn engineer says one GPU does 2,000 tokens per second, so four GPUs at $3,000 a month, $12,000 in total,\nwould replace it. What is wrong with that, and what is the right calculation?","a":"Twenty billion tokens a month is roughly 7,700 tokens per second on average, and four GPUs at\n2,000 tokens per second cover that only if they are saturated every second of the month with no headroom.\nWith a peak-to-average ratio of 2.5 and 20% headroom you must provision about 23,000 tokens per second,\nwhich is twelve replicas, $36,000, before adding the platform engineer, on-call and eval upkeep that make\nit about $52,000, above the $40,000 API bill. The 2,000 figure must also be re-measured at the latency\ntarget on your sequence lengths rather than at maximum batch, and the open-weight model must match the\nhosted one on your evals or the gap must be priced. Then run the sensitivity: a flat batch workload drops\nthe replica count to around six and self-hosting wins, while a committed-use API discount to $1.50 per\nmillion makes the API $30,000 and widens its lead. The honest recommendation is to measure peak-to-average\nand throughput at SLO first, and to consider a hybrid that self-hosts the flat base load and sends bursts\nto the API."},"quiz":[{"q":"For a chosen model and serving stack, which parameter most changes the self-hosted cost per token?","options":["The GPU's hourly list price","The utilization actually achieved after provisioning for peak with headroom","The maximum-batch throughput in the vendor's published benchmark","The network egress price of the cloud region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Effective cost per token is the replica cost divided by the tokens it actually serves. Provisioning for a bursty peak can leave two-thirds or more of the capacity idle, which typically moves the cost per token more than price differences between comparable GPU offers; a vendor benchmark changes no real cost, and egress is small for text tokens."},{"q":"At realistic utilization the self-hosted effective cost per token comes out above the API's price per token. What does that imply?","options":["Break-even will be reached at a high enough volume","There is no break-even at any volume, because the fixed costs only widen the gap","Break-even is reached at a low volume","More GPUs should be added to lower the cost per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the variable part alone is more expensive than the API, adding fixed costs for people and platform makes self-hosting worse at every volume. Only a change in utilization, GPU price or API price can restore a crossover."},{"q":"Why is a hybrid, self-hosting the base load and sending bursts to the API, so often the cheapest shape?","options":["Hosted APIs are always cheaper per token than any self-hosted setup","Fixed capacity amortizes best on flat, predictable load, while bursts are exactly what per-token pricing handles well","It removes the need to evaluate the open-weight model","Self-hosted GPUs cannot technically serve bursty traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each cost structure is matched to the traffic shape it suits: the reserved GPUs run near full utilization on the steady base, and the API absorbs the spiky remainder without idle capacity being paid for. The price is two serving paths to keep consistent."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/finops","sources":["Official docs: FinOps Foundation (FinOps Framework)","Storment & Fuller, 2019 — Cloud FinOps"],"why":"Traditional budgeting assumes spending is decided by purchase orders that finance approves in advance.\nCloud broke that assumption: a line in a config file decides spend, and the invoice arrives a month later.\nLLM APIs break it harder. Spend is per token, so a prompt edit, a retrieval change that doubles context, an\nagent loop that retries, or a traffic spike can move the bill by a multiple with no procurement event and no\ncode reviewer thinking about money. And the provider's invoice is a single line, \"API usage\", that says\nnothing about which product, customer, feature or team caused it. Finance cannot allocate it; engineering\ngets no feedback; the two sides meet only when the number is already alarming.\n\nThe naive responses each fail. Approval gates for every new model or key slow delivery and get bypassed\nwith personal keys, which makes attribution worse. A one-time \"cost project\" saves a slice and then the bill\nclimbs back, because nothing structural changed and nobody is watching. Hard spend caps protect the budget\nby taking the feature down for customers at month end. What actually works is the thing that worked for\nlatency and reliability: timely, attributed data in front of the people making the decisions, clear\nownership, and a loop that runs every week rather than once a year.","how":"### The loop: inform, optimize, operate\nThe FinOps Foundation frames the practice as a cycle. *Inform* means seeing and allocating spend\naccurately and quickly. *Optimize* means acting on it, through both usage and rate levers. *Operate* means\nmaking the first two continuous: budgets, forecasts, reviews, owners and policy. It is a loop *because*\ncosts drift: models are updated, prices change, features ship weekly and traffic mix moves. A snapshot\ngoes stale in a month.\n\n### Inform: meter at the point of call\nProvider invoices lack your context, so attribution has to happen in your own code, at the single point\nevery call passes through: an AI gateway or an SDK wrapper. Each call records the model, input, output and\ncached token counts, latency, tenant, feature, environment, prompt version and a request id that joins to\nyour traces. Cost is computed at record time from a versioned price table and stored alongside the price\nused, *because* prices change and a cost history that silently re-prices itself is useless for trend\nanalysis. Token counts come from the provider's response usage fields, not from your own tokenizer\nestimate, *because* tokenizers differ between models and estimates drift. Events flow to the warehouse\nwhere every other business metric lives, and once a month the metered total is reconciled against the\ninvoice; the gap is a data-quality KPI, and a growing one means a call path is bypassing the gateway or a\nprice in the table is stale. Normalizing cloud and AI billing into one schema is what the FinOps\nFoundation's FOCUS specification is for, which matters once GPU, API and SaaS spend must be read together.\n\nThe gateway is the structural fix for the root cause of unattributable spend: one shared API key per\ncompany. Per-team or per-feature keys plus mandatory tags make unallocated spend visible as a bucket to be\ndriven toward zero. Start with *showback*, showing each team its own spend, and move to *chargeback*,\nactually debiting internal budgets, only once allocation is trusted; chargeback on shaky numbers produces\narguments about the numbers instead of decisions about the spend.\n\n### Shared costs: allocate them by a driver, not evenly\nNot every dollar has a natural tag. A self-hosted GPU pool serves many features; an embedding index, an\neval suite and the platform team serve everyone; a one-year reservation is paid upfront. Allocate each by\nthe driver that causes it: GPU pool cost by GPU-seconds or tokens served per feature (decode tokens weigh\nmore than prefill tokens for the reason above), reservations amortized monthly over the term rather than\nbooked in the month they were paid, platform cost by a stated proxy such as share of requests. Splitting\nevenly is the tempting shortcut and it is wrong *because* it subsidizes the heavy users with the light\nones, which hides exactly the unprofitable tenant the exercise was meant to find. Whatever the rule, write\nit down; disputes about allocation are disputes about the rule, and a written rule ends them.\n\n### Unit economics: telling growth from waste\nTotal spend is the wrong headline. The question is whether cost per unit of value moved: cost per\nsuccessful task, per active user, per document processed, gross margin per tenant. A bill that doubled while\ncost per task stayed flat is growth; a bill that doubled on flat volume is a regression. Only the unit view\ndistinguishes them, and only the unit view lets product price the feature.\n\n### Optimize: usage levers and rate levers are different jobs\n*Usage optimization* means consuming fewer units: prompt trimming, prompt and response caching, routing\ncheap models first, output caps, step and token budgets on agents,\nretiring idle GPU replicas. *Rate optimization* means paying less per unit: committed-use and provisioned\nthroughput discounts, reserved GPUs, negotiated enterprise pricing, and moving offline work to\ndiscounted batch endpoints, which bill the same tokens at a lower price. They are kept\nseparate because they belong to different people (engineering versus procurement), move on different\ntimescales, and mixing them hides which one is actually working.\n\n### Operate: budgets, anomalies, forecasts, owners\nEach feature and team gets a budget with an alert on *forecast* (month-to-date burn rate projected to\nmonth end), not just on the threshold, because by the time a threshold fires the money is spent. Project\nfrom the *recent* daily rate (say, the trailing three days) as well as the month-to-date average, because\na step change on day eight is diluted by seven normal days in the average and shows up in full in the\nrecent rate. Maturity is gradual, and the FinOps Foundation describes it as crawl, walk, run: a daily\nreport first, then budgets and showback, then forecasts, anomaly detection and chargeback. Daily\nanomaly detection runs per dimension: a feature whose tokens per task tripled overnight is a bug, not a\ntrend. A named owner reviews each cost centre weekly. Guardrails live in the gateway: per-key spend\nlimits, model allowlists, default output caps. New features carry a cost estimate through design review,\nexpected tokens per task times expected volume, so cost is discussed before the code exists.\n\n### What is specific to AI spend\nTokens are driven by prompt design, so cost is a code-review concern. Agents multiply spend: a ten-step\nloop is ten calls, and context grows with every step. Evaluation is real spend: a large eval suite run\nagainst a judge model on every pull request can rival production traffic. Cached tokens are priced\ndifferently, so cache hit rate is a cost KPI. For self-hosted models the waste metric is GPU idle time,\nwhich belongs on the same dashboard as token spend.","example":"A minimal gateway-side recorder and a daily review that forecasts on burn rate. Prices are placeholders;\nin a real system they are loaded from the provider's current price list and versioned.\n\n```python\nimport time, uuid\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nPRICES = {  # USD per 1M tokens, illustrative; \"in\" = uncached input tokens\n    \"large-model\": {\"in\": 3.00, \"out\": 15.00, \"cached_in\": 0.30},\n    \"small-model\": {\"in\": 0.15, \"out\": 0.60,  \"cached_in\": 0.015},\n}\nEVENTS = []\n\ndef record_call(model, usage, *, tenant, feature, env, prompt_version):\n    p = PRICES[model]\n    cost = (usage[\"in\"] * p[\"in\"] + usage[\"out\"] * p[\"out\"] + usage[\"cached_in\"] * p[\"cached_in\"]) / 1e6\n    event = {\"ts\": time.time(), \"id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"model\": model, **usage, \"cost_usd\": cost,\n             \"tenant\": tenant, \"feature\": feature, \"env\": env, \"prompt_version\": prompt_version,\n             \"price_version\": \"2026-10\"}   # store the price used so history stays reproducible\n    EVENTS.append(event)\n    return event\n\ndef daily_review(events, budgets_usd, day_of_month, days_in_month=30):\n    spend = defaultdict(float)\n    for e in events:\n        spend[e[\"feature\"]] += e[\"cost_usd\"]\n    for feature, budget in budgets_usd.items():\n        mtd = spend[feature]\n        forecast = mtd / day_of_month * days_in_month        # burn-rate forecast, not a threshold\n        flag = \"OVER-FORECAST\" if forecast > budget else \"ok\"\n        print(f\"{feature:<10} mtd=${mtd:8.2f}  forecast=${forecast:8.2f}  budget=${budget:6.0f}  {flag}\")\n\n# Illustrative traffic after a retrieval change doubled the context sent by 'search'\nfor _ in range(250_000):\n    record_call(\"small-model\", {\"in\": 2400, \"out\": 150, \"cached_in\": 800},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"search\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v12\")\nfor _ in range(50_000):\n    record_call(\"large-model\", {\"in\": 1200, \"out\": 300, \"cached_in\": 0},\n                tenant=\"acme\", feature=\"summarize\", env=\"prod\", prompt_version=\"v3\")\ndaily_review(EVENTS, {\"search\": 300, \"summarize\": 1500}, day_of_month=10)\n# search     mtd=$  115.50  forecast=$  346.50  budget=$   300  OVER-FORECAST\n# summarize  mtd=$  405.00  forecast=$ 1215.00  budget=$  1500  ok\n```\n\nThe point of the output is the first line. On day ten the search feature has spent only $115 of its\n$300 budget, so a threshold alert would stay silent for two more weeks; the burn-rate forecast flags it\ntoday. Because every event carries `prompt_version` and token counts, the next query, tokens per call by\nprompt version, shows that `v12` doubled input tokens, and the fix is a prompt rollback rather than a\nbudget increase. The simulation runs the whole month-to-date at the new rate; in real traffic, if `v12`\nshipped on day eight, the month-to-date average would mix in seven cheaper days, so the burn-rate forecast\nwould understate the overrun and a trailing-days forecast would show it in full. Note too where the money\nis: each search call costs about $0.00046, and 372 of its 462 micro-dollars are input tokens, so context\nsize, not output length, is the lever for that feature.","tradeoffs":["A gateway chokepoint gives complete attribution and enforcement, but it is one more hop of latency and a single point of failure; the trade flips when teams need provider features the gateway cannot pass through.","Chargeback creates real accountability, but needs allocation that teams trust; until allocation is nearly complete, showback produces better decisions with fewer arguments.","Hard per-key spend caps stop runaway bills but can take a customer-facing feature down mid-month; soft forecast alerts plus rate limiting suit production paths, hard caps suit development and evaluation keys.","Commitments such as reserved GPUs or provisioned throughput lower unit price but lock in volume; they pay off only when the baseline is predictable for the whole term.","Fine-grained tags (tenant, feature, prompt version) answer \"which customer is unprofitable\" but demand discipline on every call site; coarse tags are cheap and answer almost nothing."],"pitfalls":["One shared API key for the whole company; the symptom is finance asking who spent the money and nobody being able to answer beyond \"the AI team\".","Alerting only on a monthly threshold; the symptom is an alert that fires on the 28th, after the budget is gone and before anyone can react.","Reporting total spend instead of unit cost; the symptom is a growth month triggering a panic cost cut that damages the product.","Hard-coding prices and never updating them; the symptom is an internal dashboard that disagrees with the invoice by a wide margin and loses everyone's trust.","Ignoring non-production spend; the symptom is production explaining half the bill while evals, load tests and developer experiments own the rest with no owner.","Splitting a shared GPU pool or platform cost evenly across teams; the symptom is a margin report where every tenant looks equally profitable and the heavy tenant's growth never shows up as a cost.","Agents without step and token budgets; the symptom is a single stuck loop becoming the largest line item of the day."],"when":"Start the day there is a bill worth caring about, which for most teams is the first month the API invoice\nexceeds a few hundred dollars or the first GPU reservation. The minimum viable version is one structured\nevent per call with tags, a versioned price table and a daily report by feature; budgets, forecasts,\nanomaly detection and showback follow as spend grows, and chargeback comes last. Measure the practice\nitself: percentage of spend allocated, cost per successful task by feature, cache hit rate, GPU utilization,\nforecast error and time from anomaly to detection. Day to day it shows up as a cost column in the trace\nviewer, a cost estimate in design reviews and a weekly ten-minute review. In interviews, be able to explain\nthe loop, why attribution must happen at the gateway, the usage-versus-rate distinction and why unit cost\nrather than total spend is the headline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments every call with tags and token counts, keeps the price table versioned, adds step and token budgets to agents, and treats a tokens-per-task regression like a latency regression.","architect":"Owns the gateway as the enforcement point, the allocation model, the budget structure and the policy that no feature ships without a cost estimate and an owner.","fde":"Sets up per-tenant metering and showback inside the customer's environment, and turns the first surprising invoice into a cost-per-task conversation about margin rather than a cancellation."},"check":{"q":"The monthly LLM bill jumped from $20,000 to $45,000 and leadership wants a 30% cut by Friday. What\ndo you do first, and why might cutting be the wrong move?","a":"Attribute before acting. Break the $25,000 delta down by feature, tenant, model and prompt version,\nand compute tokens per task and cost per successful task before and after the jump. Three outcomes are\npossible. If volume grew and unit cost is flat, the increase is growth, and the right response is to show\nmargin per task and leave the feature alone. If tokens per task jumped in one feature, it is a regression,\ntypically a retrieval change, a prompt edit or an agent loop retrying, and fixing it recovers most of the\ndelta without touching anything else. If the model or price mix changed, the levers are routing traffic\nback to cheaper models (usage) and committed-use pricing or a tier change (rate). Then install the guardrails that were missing: per-feature budgets\nwith burn-rate forecasts, daily anomaly alerts on tokens per task, per-key limits and a cost estimate in\ndesign review, so the next jump is caught in a day rather than on the invoice."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must token and cost attribution happen in your own gateway rather than from the provider's invoice?","options":["Because provider invoices arrive too late to be useful","Because the invoice carries none of your context: tenant, feature, environment or prompt version","Because providers round costs to the nearest dollar","Because gateways compute costs faster than providers do"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider sees API keys and tokens, not your product structure. Only the code that makes the call knows which customer and feature it serves, so the tags have to be attached there and the cost computed alongside them."},{"q":"Which of these is a usage-optimization lever rather than a rate-optimization lever?","options":["Signing a committed-use discount with the provider","Negotiating enterprise pricing for the large model","Trimming the prompt and capping output length so each task consumes fewer tokens","Reserving GPU instances for a one-year term"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rate levers change the price paid per unit and belong to procurement. Trimming and capping reduce the number of billable units consumed, which is an engineering change to usage; keeping the two apart shows which lever is working."},{"q":"Total spend doubled this month while cost per successful task stayed flat. What is the FinOps reading?","options":["Waste doubled and spend should be cut immediately","Usage doubled; check margin per task and whether the growth is wanted, rather than cutting","Provider prices doubled without notice","Cost attribution is broken and the numbers cannot be trusted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flat unit cost with doubled total means twice as many successful tasks were delivered at the same efficiency. That is growth, and the decision belongs to product and pricing, not to a cost cut that would reduce the value delivered."}]},{"key":"infra-capacity-economics/tco","sources":[],"why":"AI purchasing decisions get framed by the most visible price: dollars per million tokens, dollars per GPU\nhour, dollars per seat. Each of those is real, and each describes only one slice of what the option will\ncost. A per-token price says nothing about the engineer who keeps the prompts working when the provider\nretires the model. A GPU hourly rate leaves out the serving stack, the on-call rotation and the\nthree-year hardware refresh. A per-seat SaaS price leaves out integration, the security review, the\nannual price escalator and what it costs to get your data back out.\n\nThe failure mode is predictable. Option A wins on the headline price, gets chosen, and then the costs\nnobody wrote down arrive as \"unplanned\" work: a migration sprint when a model is deprecated, a re-embedding\njob when the embedding model changes, a quarter of platform work to make the self-hosted cluster reliable.\nSix months in, the cheaper option is the expensive one, and nobody can say whether the decision was wrong\nor just unlucky, because nothing was written down to compare against.\n\nThe naive fix, \"add 20% for overhead\", fails because the hidden costs are not proportional to the visible\none. Engineering cost is roughly fixed regardless of volume, licence cost scales with seats, token cost\nscales with usage, and migration cost arrives in lumps on someone else's schedule. TCO exists to put all\nof those on one timeline, so the comparison is honest and the assumptions can be checked later.","how":"### 1. Fix the outcome and the horizon\nOptions are only comparable if they deliver the same thing: the same task, at the same quality bar on your\nevals, at the same latency and availability target. If the cheaper option scores lower, either add the\ncost of closing the gap (fine-tuning, a reranker, human review) or express both in cost per successful\ntask. The horizon is usually three years: long enough that one-off build costs amortize, and it matches\nthe longest common committed-use term (hardware itself is often depreciated over three to five years). For\na hosted-API product, a horizon longer than the model generation you are building on is fiction, so keep\nit short and model the migration as a cost inside it.\n\n### 2. Enumerate costs in four buckets\n- **Acquire (one-off):** build effort, integration with identity, data and logging systems, procurement\n  and security review, building the eval set, initial data preparation and indexing, hardware purchase.\n- **Operate (recurring):** tokens or GPU hours, licences, vector database and storage, observability,\n  network egress, support contracts, and the fractional people who run it, carry the pager and answer\n  user tickets. On-prem adds power and cooling (facility power is roughly IT power times the site's\n  PUE, power usage effectiveness), space, spares and hardware refresh.\n- **Change (recurring but lumpy):** forced model migrations and the re-evaluation they need, prompt\n  re-tuning, re-embedding a corpus when the embedding model changes (the old vectors are unusable with the\n  new model), serving-stack upgrades, price changes at renewal.\n- **Exit (contingent):** data export, re-integration with a replacement, contract minimums or unused\n  commitments, parallel running during cut-over. You include it because it bounds how locked in you are.\n\n### 3. Cost people honestly\nPeople are usually the biggest line, and the one most often left out because it does not appear on a\ncloud bill. Use a loaded cost per engineer-year (salary plus benefits, overhead, tooling), and estimate\nfractions of FTE per activity. Include opportunity cost when the same engineers would otherwise ship\nproduct: an option that consumes a platform team is not free because the team is already on payroll.\n\n### 4. Count only what the decision changes\nTCO for a decision is *incremental*: include a cost only if it differs between the options from today\nonward. Money already spent, such as GPUs bought last year or a prototype already built, is sunk and\nbelongs in neither column, *because* it is gone whichever option you pick; \"we already paid for the\ncluster\" is an argument for using it only through its resale or opportunity value. Conversely, shared\ncosts that exist either way (the identity system, the data warehouse) cancel out and can be left out of\nboth, which keeps the model small. What remains is the money and people the choice actually commits.\n\n### 5. Put it on a timeline and discount it\nLay costs out by period. Capital purchases are depreciated over their useful life for accounting, but for\na decision compare cash flows. Discount future cash at a rate your finance team provides:\n`NPV = Σ_t cost_t / (1 + r)^t`. Discounting matters *because* options differ in timing: a build is\nfront-loaded, a growing subscription is back-loaded, and undiscounted totals overstate the back-loaded one.\n\n### 6. Model uncertainty as scenarios\nVolume, adoption, provider price moves and model deprecation dates are all uncertain. Rather than one\nnumber, run a base case plus the two or three assumptions that move the answer most (adoption, price,\nteam size), and attach expected costs to risk events as probability times impact. The output is a range\nand a statement of which assumption flips the decision.\n\n### How it relates to its neighbours\nBreak-even analysis is one slice of TCO along the volume axis. Cost modeling supplies the per-request\noperate costs. Unit economics divides TCO by successful outcomes. FinOps is what checks the TCO model\nagainst actual spend once the option is live.","example":"Build a contract-review assistant on a hosted LLM API, or buy a vendor SaaS product priced per seat?\nEvery figure below is illustrative, in thousands of dollars.\n\n```python\nFTE, RATE = 200, 0.10               # loaded engineer-year; discount rate from finance\n\ndef build_on_api(users):\n    upfront = 1.0 * FTE + 30        # 2 engineers x 6 months, plus building the eval set\n    run = [u * 0.12                 # tokens at $120 per active user-year\n           + 0.75 * FTE             # maintenance, on-call, prompt upkeep\n           + 24                     # vector DB, observability, gateway\n           + 40                     # one forced model migration and re-eval per year\n           for u in users]\n    return [upfront] + run\n\ndef buy_saas(users, seat=0.36, escalator=0.07):\n    upfront = 60                    # integration, SSO, security review\n    run = [u * seat * (1 + escalator) ** y + 0.25 * FTE for y, u in enumerate(users)]\n    return [upfront] + run          # licence with annual escalator, plus admin time\n\ndef tco(flows, rate=0.0):\n    return sum(c / (1 + rate) ** t for t, c in enumerate(flows))\n\nfor label, users in ((\"growth\", [500, 1000, 1500]), (\"flat\", [500, 500, 500])):\n    b, s = build_on_api(users), buy_saas(users)\n    print(f\"{label}: build {tco(b):.0f} (NPV {tco(b, RATE):.0f})  buy {tco(s):.0f} (NPV {tco(s, RATE):.0f})\")\n# growth: build 1232 (NPV 1051)  buy 1393 (NPV 1131)\n# flat: build 1052 (NPV 911)  buy 789 (NPV 662)\n```\n\nThree readings. First, in the build option tokens are a minority of cost even in year three ($180k of\n$394k), and over the three years the 0.75 FTE of upkeep ($450k) outweighs tokens ($360k). A 20% token\ndiscount would save about $72k; trimming upkeep by a quarter of an FTE would save $150k. Second, the decision flips on adoption: per-seat pricing scales with users while\nthe build is mostly fixed, so build wins if usage triples and buy wins clearly if it stays flat. That makes\nadoption the assumption to test before committing. Third, discounting halves build's advantage in the\ngrowth case ($161k undiscounted, $80k NPV) because the subscription's large payments come late. A strong\nwrite-up would also add exit cost to the buy side and the option value of reusing the built pipeline for a\nsecond feature, then say which one the recommendation depends on.","tradeoffs":["A full TCO model exposes hidden costs but takes days to build; for a reversible, small decision a one-page estimate is enough, and the full model pays off only when commitments exceed a quarter or so.","Longer horizons amortize build cost and favour building, but in AI they also stretch past the life of the current model generation, so assumptions beyond about two years become guesses.","Discounting gives a fairer comparison between front-loaded and back-loaded options, yet the discount rate is a finance choice, and a high rate can make deferring cost look better than it is.","Including risk-weighted exit and migration costs makes lock-in visible, but probabilities are judgement calls, so present them separately from firm costs instead of folding them in silently.","Committed-use and reserved pricing lowers operate cost per unit, but converts it into a fixed obligation that only pays if utilization materializes; it flips to a loss when adoption undershoots."],"pitfalls":["Comparing the invoice line only, such as tokens versus GPU hours; the symptom is a \"cheaper\" option whose first quarter is consumed by unplanned platform and migration work.","Leaving out people because they are \"already on payroll\"; it shows up as roadmap features slipping while the team keeps the self-hosted or custom-built stack alive.","Comparing options at unequal quality; the symptom is a cheap option that needs a human review queue, or a fine-tuning project, that nobody costed.","Ignoring change costs such as model deprecations and embedding-model upgrades; the symptom is an urgent re-embedding job or migration sprint arriving on the provider's schedule rather than yours.","Presenting one number with false precision; when one assumption, usually adoption, turns out wrong, stakeholders lose trust in the whole analysis instead of updating the input that moved.","Counting sunk costs, such as hardware already bought, as a reason to keep using it; the symptom is a business case for keeping an underused cluster that only works if its purchase price is counted as a saving.","Never revisiting the model after launch; without FinOps actuals to compare against, the next TCO repeats the same blind spots."],"when":"Build a TCO model whenever a decision commits money or people for more than a few months: build versus\nbuy, hosted API versus self-hosting, choosing a vector database or serving platform, signing a committed-use\ncontract, or moving workloads on-prem. Skip the full model for cheap, reversible experiments; there, run the\npilot and measure. Measure loaded people cost, cost per successful task, migration frequency and adoption,\nbecause those dominate. Revisit the model at renewal or when a provider changes price or retires a model.\nIn interviews, the signal is naming the non-invoice lines unprompted and saying which assumption flips the\nrecommendation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Supplies the honest numbers: tokens per task, FTE fractions for upkeep, and how long the last model migration and re-evaluation actually took.","architect":"Owns the comparison frame: equal outcome, horizon, cost buckets, scenarios, and designing for low exit cost so the TCO's lock-in line stays small.","fde":"Builds the customer's TCO for adopting the product, including their integration, security review and change-management effort, which often decides the deal more than licence price."},"check":{"q":"A team proposes self-hosting an open-weight model because GPU cost per token is a third of the API\nprice. What would you ask before agreeing, in terms of total cost of ownership?","a":"First, where \"a third\" came from. If it is the GPU hourly price divided by benchmark throughput, it\nassumes the GPU is busy every second; bursty interactive traffic provisioned for peak with headroom often\nruns near a third utilization, which alone turns a third of the price into roughly the same price, before\nanything else is added. Then whether the comparison is at equal quality on our evals, and if not what\nclosing the gap costs. Then the operate costs beyond GPUs: fractional platform and on-call engineers at\nloaded cost, serving-stack upgrades and observability. Then\nchange costs: how often we will swap models and what each re-evaluation costs. Then exit and commitment\nrisk: reserved capacity we may not fill. I would want this over a two-to-three-year horizon with\nscenarios for volume and API price cuts, and the assumption that flips the answer named explicitly."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should two options be compared at equal quality on your evals before comparing TCO?","options":["Because vendors are required to disclose quality differences in their pricing","Because a quality gap creates real costs, such as fine-tuning or human review, that otherwise go uncounted","Because lower-quality models always use more tokens per request","Because TCO is only defined for options with identical architectures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cheaper option often needs extra work to reach the same outcome. Comparing at unequal quality hides that work; equalize quality or compare cost per successful task instead."},{"q":"In a build-on-API option, token spend is a small share of three-year TCO. What does that imply?","options":["Negotiating a lower token price is the most effective way to cut TCO","The option is under-utilized and should be moved to self-hosting","People and change costs dominate, so reducing upkeep effort moves the total more than token price does","Discounting should be removed because it distorts small cost lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Effort on a line should follow its share of the total. When upkeep FTE is the biggest line, automation and stable interfaces save more than a token discount; self-hosting would usually add people cost."},{"q":"Discounting future costs at 10% narrows the gap between a front-loaded build and a growing subscription. Why?","options":["Discounting inflates upfront costs to reflect risk","The subscription's largest payments come in later years, which discounting weights less than early ones","Discounting applies only to licence fees, not engineering costs","Discounting removes the annual price escalator from the subscription"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each future cost is divided by (1 + r)^t, so late cash counts for less. The subscription grows with adoption and escalators, so its back-loaded payments shrink most; the upfront build cost is untouched."}]}]});
